window.LAB_ASSIGNMENTS = [{"num": 1, "title": "Lab Assignment 1", "subtitle": "Data Cleaning on the Titanic Dataset", "dataset": "Titanic-Dataset.csv", "intro": "WHAT IS THIS FILE?\n-------------------\nThis is a normal Python script (a \".py\" file). It is NOT a special format -\nit is just plain text containing Python code, and you run it from top to\nbottom, one line after another, exactly like reading a recipe.\n\nWHO IS THIS FOR?\n-----------------\nThis file assumes you have NEVER written Python before and have NEVER heard\nof \"pandas\" or \"machine learning\" before. Every single step is explained in\nplain English BEFORE the code that does it, and important lines also have a\nshort comment next to them (comments start with a \"#\" symbol - Python\ncompletely ignores everything after a \"#\" on a line, so comments are 100%\nfor humans reading the code, not for the computer).\n\nHOW TO RUN THIS FILE\n----------------------\n1. Make sure Python is installed on your computer (any Python 3.x version).\n2. Make sure the \"pandas\" library is installed. If it is not installed yet,\n   open your terminal and run:\n        pip install pandas\n3. \"Titanic-Dataset.csv\" lives ONE FOLDER UP from this script, directly\n   inside the \"Lab_assingments\" folder (NOT inside this \"Lab Assignment 1\"\n   folder). This is intentional: the same Titanic dataset will be reused\n   by future lab assignments too, so it is kept in one shared place\n   instead of being copy-pasted into every assignment's own folder.\n4. Run the script from the terminal, from inside this folder, with:\n        python3 lab_assignment_1.py\n   It will print all the results (tables, numbers, explanations) directly\n   in your terminal, in order, and will also save a cleaned copy of the\n   dataset as \"Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\" in this same folder at the end.\n\nWHAT IS A \"DATASET\" AND A \"CSV\" FILE?\n----------------------------------------\nA dataset is simply a big table of information - think of an Excel sheet\nwith rows and columns. A \".csv\" file (\"Comma-Separated Values\") is just a\nplain text way of storing that table: every row of the table is one line\nof text, and every column's value is separated by a comma. pandas (a\nPython library / toolbox specifically built for working with tables of\ndata) can read a .csv file and turn it into something called a\n\"DataFrame\" - which is just pandas' name for \"a table of data that Python\ncan now search, filter, calculate on, and modify\".\n\nTHE DATASET WE ARE USING\n---------------------------\nWe are using the famous \"Titanic\" dataset. It contains information about\n891 real passengers who were on the Titanic ship, including whether they\nsurvived the disaster or not. Each row = one passenger. Each column = one\npiece of information about that passenger (their age, ticket fare, class\nof travel, etc). This is one of the most commonly used beginner datasets\nin all of data science because it has a nice mix of numbers, text,\nmissing values, and different data types - perfect for practicing data\ncleaning, which is exactly what this assignment is about.\n\n==================================================================================", "sections": [{"id": "setup", "kind": "setup", "number": null, "label": "Setup: Loading the Dataset", "chunks": [{"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE LIBRARIES WE NEED@@\n\n\"import\" is a Python keyword that means \"load this external toolbox so I can use the tools/functions inside it\". \"pandas\" is the toolbox for working with tables of data (DataFrames). \"as pd\" just means: from now on, instead of typing the full word \"pandas\" every time, we can type the short nickname \"pd\". This is a very common convention that almost every Python data-science program uses.", "technical": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE REQUIRED LIBRARIES@@\n\nThe script begins by importing pandas, the standard Python library for tabular data manipulation, aliased as pd per near-universal community convention. An import statement binds an external module's namespace into the current script so its functions and classes become callable.", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0: ZAROORI LIBRARIES IMPORT KARNA@@\n\nScript sabse pehle pandas import karta hai — Python ki standard library jo tabular (table-shaped) data ke saath kaam karne ke liye bani hai, aur ise convention ke hisaab se pd naam se alias kiya jaata hai. Import statement kisi external module ke functions/classes ko current script mein use karne layak bana deta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "import pandas as pd"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "\"os\" is a built-in Python toolbox for talking to the operating system. We only use it here to build a reliable file PATH (the \"address\" of a file on your computer), so this script can always find the shared CSV file correctly, no matter which folder you happen to run it from.", "technical": "The os module (Python's standard-library interface to the operating system) is imported solely to construct a filesystem path programmatically, so the script resolves the shared CSV's location correctly regardless of the process's current working directory at invocation time.", "hinglish": "os module (Python ki standard library jo operating system se baat karti hai) sirf isliye import kiya gaya hai taaki ek file path programmatically banaya ja sake — isse script har baar shared CSV ko sahi jagah dhoond legi, chahe tum ise kisi bhi folder se run karo."}, {"type": "code", "text": "import os"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We also set a pandas display option so that when we print a table, it shows ALL the columns instead of hiding some of them with \"...\". This is purely cosmetic (just for nicer/clearer printing in the terminal) and has no effect on the actual data or calculations. We set \"display.width\" to a very large number so a wide table always prints as ONE line per row, instead of pandas splitting a wide table into two stacked blocks (with a confusing \"\\\" at the end of the first block) - one single wide line is easier to read correctly than two blocks you have to mentally stitch back together row by row.", "technical": "Two pandas display options are configured purely for output presentation: display.max_columns=None disables column truncation in printed DataFrames, and display.width is set to a large value so a wide row prints on a single line rather than being wrapped by pandas into stacked column blocks joined by a backslash continuation marker. Neither option affects the underlying data or any computation.", "hinglish": "Do pandas display options set kiye gaye hai — sirf output ko readable banane ke liye. display.max_columns=None ka matlab hai koi bhi column '...' mein chhupega nahi, aur display.width ko bada rakha hai taaki wide table ek hi line mein print ho, do stacked blocks mein '\\' ke saath split hokar confuse na kare. In dono ka actual data ya calculation par koi asar nahi padta."}, {"type": "code", "text": "pd.set_option(\"display.max_columns\", None)\npd.set_option(\"display.width\", 1000)\n\n\ndef section(title):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is a small helper FUNCTION (a reusable mini-program) that we wrote\n    ourselves, just to print a nice, easy-to-spot heading line before each\n    part of the output, so the terminal output is easy to read.\n    \"def\" is the Python keyword used to define (create) a new function.\n    Whenever we call section(\"something\") later in this file, Python jumps\n    up here, runs these two print lines with \"something\" plugged in, and\n    then jumps back to continue where it left off.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 90)\n    print(title)\n    print(\"=\" * 90)"}], "output": "", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-1", "kind": "question", "number": 1, "label": "Take a dataset of your choice and then upload or load the dataset.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 1: LOADING THE DATASET\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "pd.read_csv(...) is a pandas function that reads a .csv file from your computer's disk and loads it into a DataFrame (a table object in Python's memory that we can now work with). We are storing the result in a variable named \"df\" (short for \"DataFrame\" - this name is a very common convention in the data science world, so you will see \"df\" used everywhere in tutorials and real projects).\n\nThe Titanic CSV file lives ONE FOLDER UP from this script (inside the shared \"Lab_assingments\" folder), because the same dataset will be reused by other lab assignments later. Building the path this way (instead of just writing \"../Titanic-Dataset.csv\" directly) makes sure it still works correctly even if you run this script from a different starting folder in your terminal.", "technical": "pd.read_csv() parses the CSV file into a pandas DataFrame — an in-memory, two-dimensional, labeled data structure with rows and typed columns. The result is assigned to the identifier df, the conventional variable name for a primary DataFrame in data-science code.\n\nThe CSV path is resolved relative to the script's own file location (one directory up, inside the shared Lab_assingments folder), rather than relative to the process's current working directory, since the same dataset is intentionally shared across multiple lab assignments and must resolve correctly regardless of the invocation context.", "hinglish": "pd.read_csv() CSV file ko parse karke ek pandas DataFrame banata hai — ek in-memory, two-dimensional, labeled data structure jisme rows aur typed columns hote hai. Result \"df\" variable mein store hota hai, jo data-science code mein primary DataFrame ke liye conventional naam hai.\n\nCSV ka path script ki apni file location ke relative resolve hota hai (ek folder upar, shared Lab_assingments folder ke andar), na ki jahan se script run kiya gaya us current working directory ke relative — kyunki yehi dataset jaan-boojh kar kai lab assignments mein share kiya gaya hai, isliye ye kahin se bhi run karo to sahi resolve hona chahiye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "this_script_folder = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))\ncsv_path = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"..\", \"Titanic-Dataset.csv\")\ndf = pd.read_csv(csv_path)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df.shape tells us the size of the table as (number_of_rows, number_of_columns). It is NOT a function you \"call\" with parentheses - it is an attribute (a stored property) of the DataFrame, so we just read it directly.", "technical": "df.shape is an attribute (not a method) that returns a tuple (n_rows, n_columns) describing the DataFrame's dimensions — accessed without parentheses since it is a stored property rather than a callable.", "hinglish": "df.shape ek attribute hai (method nahi), jo (n_rows, n_columns) — ek tuple — return karta hai jisse DataFrame ka size pata chalta hai. Ise bina parentheses ke access karte hai kyunki ye ek stored property hai, koi callable function nahi."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(f\"The dataset has been loaded successfully.\")\nprint(f\"Number of rows (passengers): {df.shape[0]}\")\nprint(f\"Number of columns (pieces of information per passenger): {df.shape[1]}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df.head(5) shows us just the FIRST 5 rows of the table, so we can quickly eyeball what the data actually looks like without printing all 891 rows.", "technical": "df.head(5) returns the first n rows of the DataFrame (n=5 by default when called without an argument), letting you inspect the schema and sample values without materializing the full 891-row table in the terminal.", "hinglish": "df.head(5) DataFrame ki pehli n rows return karta hai (bina argument diye default n=5 hota hai), taaki tum bina 891 rows print kiye hi schema aur sample values dekh sako."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\nHere are the first 5 rows of the dataset, just to see what it looks like:\")\nprint(df.head())"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df.dtypes shows us, for every column, what TYPE of data pandas thinks it contains: \"int64\" = whole numbers, \"float64\" = decimal numbers, \"object\"/\"str\" = text (or a mix of text and missing values). Knowing the data type of each column matters a lot later, because you handle missing numbers differently from missing text.", "technical": "df.dtypes reports the inferred data type of every column: int64 for whole numbers, float64 for decimal numbers, and object/str for text (or a mixed column containing missing values). Correctly identifying each column's dtype matters because missing-value handling strategy differs between numeric and categorical columns.", "hinglish": "df.dtypes har column ka inferred data type batata hai: int64 (poore numbers), float64 (decimal numbers), aur object/str (text, ya koi column jisme missing values bhi mix hai). Har column ka sahi dtype pehchanna zaroori hai kyunki numeric aur categorical columns mein missing-value handle karne ki strategy alag hoti hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\nData type of every column (int64/float64 = numeric, object = text):\")\nprint(df.dtypes)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A quick plain-English description of what each column in this dataset means, since a total beginner won't know what \"SibSp\" or \"Parch\" mean:", "technical": "A brief data dictionary is provided for domain context, since correctly interpreting abbreviated column names such as SibSp and Parch is a prerequisite for any downstream decision in this pipeline.", "hinglish": "Yahan ek chhota data dictionary diya gaya hai domain context ke liye, kyunki SibSp aur Parch jaise abbreviated column names ko sahi samajhna is pure pipeline ke aage ke har decision ke liye zaroori hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\nWhat each column means (for reference while reading this script):\n  PassengerId : a unique serial number given to each passenger row (1, 2, 3, ...)\n  Survived    : 0 = did not survive, 1 = survived\n  Pclass      : ticket class the passenger travelled in (1 = 1st/best class, 3 = 3rd/cheapest class)\n  Name        : the passenger's full name\n  Sex         : male / female\n  Age         : the passenger's age in years\n  SibSp       : number of Siblings/Spouses travelling with the passenger\n  Parch       : number of Parents/Children travelling with the passenger\n  Ticket      : the passenger's ticket number/code (a mix of letters and numbers)\n  Fare        : how much money the passenger paid for the ticket\n  Cabin       : the cabin (room) number the passenger stayed in, if known\n  Embarked    : the port where the passenger boarded the ship (C, Q, or S)\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "The dataset has been loaded successfully.\nNumber of rows (passengers): 891\nNumber of columns (pieces of information per passenger): 12\n\nHere are the first 5 rows of the dataset, just to see what it looks like:\n   PassengerId  Survived  Pclass                                               Name     Sex   Age  SibSp  Parch            Ticket     Fare Cabin Embarked\n0            1         0       3                            Braund, Mr. Owen Harris    male  22.0      1      0         A/5 21171   7.2500   NaN        S\n1            2         1       1  Cumings, Mrs. John Bradley (Florence Briggs Th...  female  38.0      1      0          PC 17599  71.2833   C85        C\n2            3         1       3                             Heikkinen, Miss. Laina  female  26.0      0      0  STON/O2. 3101282   7.9250   NaN        S\n3            4         1       1       Futrelle, Mrs. Jacques Heath (Lily May Peel)  female  35.0      1      0            113803  53.1000  C123        S\n4            5         0       3                           Allen, Mr. William Henry    male  35.0      0      0            373450   8.0500   NaN        S\n\nData type of every column (int64/float64 = numeric, object = text):\nPassengerId      int64\nSurvived         int64\nPclass           int64\nName               str\nSex                str\nAge            float64\nSibSp            int64\nParch            int64\nTicket             str\nFare           float64\nCabin              str\nEmbarked           str\ndtype: object\n\nWhat each column means (for reference while reading this script):\n  PassengerId : a unique serial number given to each passenger row (1, 2, 3, ...)\n  Survived    : 0 = did not survive, 1 = survived\n  Pclass      : ticket class the passenger travelled in (1 = 1st/best class, 3 = 3rd/cheapest class)\n  Name        : the passenger's full name\n  Sex         : male / female\n  Age         : the passenger's age in years\n  SibSp       : number of Siblings/Spouses travelling with the passenger\n  Parch       : number of Parents/Children travelling with the passenger\n  Ticket      : the passenger's ticket number/code (a mix of letters and numbers)\n  Fare        : how much money the passenger paid for the ticket\n  Cabin       : the cabin (room) number the passenger stayed in, if known\n  Embarked    : the port where the passenger boarded the ship (C, Q, or S)", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-2", "kind": "question", "number": 2, "label": "Identify the number and percentage of missing values in each column, and handle missing values using a suitable technique.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 2A: FINDING MISSING VALUES\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "\"Missing value\" means: for a given passenger (row) and a given column, we simply do not have that piece of information. In the raw .csv file these show up as empty cells; once pandas loads them, it represents a missing value using a special marker called \"NaN\" (Not a Number).\n\ndf.isnull() returns a new table of the exact same shape as df, but every cell is now either True (this value is missing) or False (this value is present). Then \".sum()\" on top of that adds up all the True values in each column (in Python, True behaves like the number 1 and False like 0 when you add them up), which gives us a per-column COUNT of missing values.", "technical": "A \"missing value\" is a cell with no recorded observation for a given (row, column) pair. pandas represents this internally with the sentinel NaN (Not a Number), regardless of whether the column's other values are numeric or textual.\n\ndf.isnull() returns a boolean DataFrame of identical shape to df, where each cell is True if missing and False otherwise. Chaining .sum() aggregates each column's True count (Python's bool subtype coerces to 1/0 under arithmetic operations), yielding a per-column missing-value count.", "hinglish": "\"Missing value\" ka matlab hai ek cell jisme kisi (row, column) pair ke liye koi observation record hi nahi hai. pandas ise internally ek sentinel value NaN (Not a Number) se represent karta hai — chahe us column ki baaki values numeric ho ya text.\n\ndf.isnull() df ke bilkul same shape ka ek boolean DataFrame return karta hai, jisme har cell True (missing) ya False (present) hota hai. Uske baad .sum() chain karne se har column ke True values ka count aa jaata hai (Python mein bool, arithmetic operations mein 1/0 mein convert ho jaata hai), jisse per-column missing-value count mil jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "missing_count = df.isnull().sum()"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "To get a PERCENTAGE, we divide each column's missing count by the total number of rows (len(df) gives the number of rows in the table), then multiply by 100 to turn the fraction into a percentage, and round it to 2 decimal places using .round(2) so it is easy to read.", "technical": "The percentage is derived by dividing each column's missing count by len(df) (total row count), scaling by 100, and rounding to 2 decimal places via .round(2) for readability.", "hinglish": "Percentage nikalne ke liye har column ka missing count len(df) (total row count) se divide karte hai, 100 se multiply karte hai, aur readability ke liye .round(2) se 2 decimal places tak round kar dete hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "missing_percent = (missing_count / len(df) * 100).round(2)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "pd.DataFrame({...}) here builds a small, new, clean summary table out of the two Series (columns of numbers) we just calculated, so we can print them side by side instead of separately. The dictionary keys (\"Missing Count\", \"Missing %\") become the column headers of this new mini-table.", "technical": "pd.DataFrame({...}) constructs a new summary DataFrame from the two previously computed Series, so both metrics can be inspected side by side in one table. The dictionary keys become that new DataFrame's column labels.", "hinglish": "pd.DataFrame({...}) do pehle se calculate ki hui Series se ek naya summary DataFrame banata hai, taaki dono metrics ek hi table mein side by side dekhi ja sake. Dictionary ki keys us naye DataFrame ke column labels ban jaati hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "missing_summary = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"Missing Count\": missing_count,\n    \"Missing %\": missing_percent\n})"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We only really care about columns that HAVE at least one missing value, so we filter the summary table down to just those rows, and sort them from the most-missing column to the least-missing column, so the worst offenders are immediately visible at the top.", "technical": "The summary is boolean-filtered to rows where Missing Count > 0 (columns with zero missing values are dropped from the view), then sorted descending so the most-affected columns surface first.", "hinglish": "Summary table ko boolean-filter karke sirf un rows tak limit kiya jaata hai jahan Missing Count > 0 ho (zero-missing columns view se hat jaate hai), fir descending order mein sort kiya jaata hai taaki sabse zyada affected columns sabse upar dikhein."}, {"type": "code", "text": "missing_summary = missing_summary[missing_summary[\"Missing Count\"] > 0]\nmissing_summary = missing_summary.sort_values(\"Missing Count\", ascending=False)\n\nprint(\"Columns that contain missing values, and how much of each is missing:\")\nprint(missing_summary)\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nINTERPRETATION (in plain English):\n  - \"Cabin\" is missing for about 77% of all passengers. That is a HUGE\n    proportion of the column - for more than 3 out of every 4 passengers,\n    we simply have no cabin information at all.\n  - \"Age\" is missing for about 20% of passengers (177 out of 891). That is\n    a meaningful chunk, but still a clear minority of the data.\n  - \"Embarked\" is missing for only 2 passengers out of 891 - a tiny, almost\n    negligible amount.\n  Each of these three columns is missing a very different AMOUNT of data,\n  so (as the assignment hints) each one deserves a different, suitable\n  technique for handling it - there is no single \"one size fits all\" fix.\n\"\"\")\n\nsection(\"QUESTION 2B: HANDLING MISSING VALUES\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We make a COPY of the original dataframe before we start changing it. This is a good habit: df.copy() creates a completely independent second table in memory. If we made mistakes while editing \"df\" directly, we could not easily get the original, untouched data back to compare against. By working on \"df_clean\" instead, \"df\" (the original) stays safe and unchanged the whole time.", "technical": "df.copy() is called before any mutation to create an independent deep copy in memory, decoupled from the original df. This is standard defensive practice: it preserves an unmodified baseline for comparison and prevents accidental in-place corruption of the source data during iterative cleaning.", "hinglish": "Koi bhi mutation karne se pehle df.copy() call kiya jaata hai, jo memory mein ek independent deep copy banata hai, original df se alag. Ye ek standard defensive practice hai: isse ek unmodified baseline compare karne ke liye bacha rehta hai, aur cleaning ke dauraan source data accidentally corrupt hone se bach jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "df_clean = df.copy()"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Handling \"Age\" (a NUMERIC column, missing ~20% of the time)@@\n\nFor a numeric column with a moderate amount of missing data, a standard, suitable technique is IMPUTATION: instead of deleting rows (which would throw away all the OTHER good information - Sex, Fare, Pclass, etc. - that those same 177 passengers have), we fill in the gap with a sensible estimated value calculated from the data we DO have.\n\nWe use the MEDIAN (the middle value when all known ages are sorted from smallest to largest) rather than the MEAN (the mathematical average), because Age can have outliers (a few very old or very young passengers), and the median is much less affected/pulled by extreme outlier values than the mean is. This makes the median a more \"robust\" (safer) choice for imputing a numeric column like Age.", "technical": "@@Handling \"Age\" (a numeric column, ~20% missing)@@\n\nFor a numeric column with a moderate missing-value proportion, imputation is the standard technique — replacing each missing cell with a statistically derived estimate rather than discarding the entire row (which would also sacrifice every other, fully-observed feature for that record).\n\nThe median is chosen over the mean because Age's distribution contains outliers, and the median (the 50th percentile) is a robust statistic — unlike the mean, it is not pulled toward extreme values, making it the safer central-tendency estimator for imputation here.", "hinglish": "@@\"Age\" handle karna (ek numeric column, ~20% missing)@@\n\nEk numeric column jisme moderate amount mein missing values ho, uske liye standard technique imputation hoti hai — har missing cell ko ek statistically-derived estimate se replace karna, poori row delete karne ke bajaye (jisse us record ki baaki saari, fully-observed features bhi waste ho jaati).\n\nMean ki jagah median isliye choose kiya gaya hai kyunki Age ki distribution mein outliers hai, aur median (50th percentile) ek robust statistic hai — mean ki tarah extreme values ki taraf khinchta nahi, isliye yahan imputation ke liye safer central-tendency estimator hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "median_age = df_clean[\"Age\"].median()\nprint(f\"Median Age calculated from the known ages: {median_age}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df_clean[\"Age\"].fillna(value) replaces every missing (NaN) entry in the \"Age\" column with the given value, and leaves every already-known Age value completely untouched. We then assign the result back into the same column, which overwrites it with the now-complete version.", "technical": "Series.fillna(value) replaces every NaN entry in that column with the given value while leaving all non-null entries untouched; reassigning to the same column label overwrites it with the completed version.", "hinglish": "Series.fillna(value) us column ke har NaN entry ko diye gaye value se replace kar deta hai, baaki saari non-null entries ko chhue bina. Result ko wapas usi column label mein assign karne se wo column complete version se overwrite ho jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "df_clean[\"Age\"] = df_clean[\"Age\"].fillna(median_age)\nprint(\"-> Filled all missing 'Age' values with the median age (median imputation).\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Handling \"Embarked\" (a CATEGORICAL/TEXT column, missing only 2 times)@@\n\n\"Embarked\" is a text/category column (its only possible values are the letters C, Q, or S), so calculating a \"median\" or \"mean\" makes no sense for it at all - you cannot average three letters. For a categorical column, the equivalent, suitable technique is MODE IMPUTATION: filling in the missing spots with the MODE, which simply means \"the single most frequently occurring value in that column\".", "technical": "@@Handling \"Embarked\" (a categorical column, 2 missing)@@\n\nEmbarked is categorical (its domain is restricted to {C, Q, S}), so mean or median imputation is mathematically undefined — there is no arithmetic average of categorical labels. The categorical analogue is mode imputation: filling missing entries with the modal (most frequent) category.", "hinglish": "@@\"Embarked\" handle karna (ek categorical column, 2 missing)@@\n\nEmbarked categorical hai (iski domain sirf {C, Q, S} tak limited hai), isliye mean ya median imputation mathematically hi undefined hai — categorical labels ka koi arithmetic average nahi hota. Categorical column ke liye equivalent technique mode imputation hai: missing entries ko modal (sabse zyada frequent) category se fill karna."}, {"type": "code", "text": "mode_embarked = df_clean[\"Embarked\"].mode()[0]"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "NOTE: .mode() can technically return more than one value if there is a tie for \"most common\", so it always gives back a small list/Series of candidates even when there's only one answer. We take [0] to grab just the first (and in this dataset, the only) most-common value out of it.", "technical": "Series.mode() can return multiple values in the event of a tie, so it always returns a Series of candidates even when a unique mode exists; indexing [0] extracts the first (here, the only) modal value.", "hinglish": "Series.mode() tie hone ki soorat mein multiple values bhi return kar sakta hai, isliye ye hamesha ek Series of candidates return karta hai — chahe unique mode hi kyun na ho. [0] index lagane se pehli (aur yahan, akeli) modal value nikal aati hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(f\"Most common 'Embarked' port (the mode): '{mode_embarked}'\")\n\ndf_clean[\"Embarked\"] = df_clean[\"Embarked\"].fillna(mode_embarked)\nprint(\"-> Filled the 2 missing 'Embarked' values with the mode (mode imputation).\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Handling \"Cabin\" (missing ~77% of the time)@@\n\nFor \"Cabin\", imputing a value does not make sense at all: a cabin number is a unique-ish piece of information (there is no sensible \"average\" or even \"most common\" cabin that would be a meaningful guess for a random passenger), AND more importantly, 77% of the column is empty - at that point, whatever we invent to fill those gaps would just be noise, not real information, and could mislead any further analysis.\n\nThe suitable technique here, per the assignment's own list of options, is REMOVAL - specifically, removing this ENTIRE COLUMN (not the rows), because it is the column itself that is unreliable, not just a few rows. Removing the affected ROWS instead would mean throwing away 687 out of 891 passengers (77% of our whole dataset!) just because of one column - that would be a huge, unnecessary loss of otherwise-good information in every other column for those passengers.\n\ndf.drop(columns=[...]) removes the listed column(s) from the table.", "technical": "@@Handling \"Cabin\" (~77% missing)@@\n\nImputation is inappropriate here on two grounds: Cabin values are near-unique identifiers with no meaningful central tendency, and at ~77% missing, any imputed value would constitute fabricated signal rather than recovered information, degrading rather than improving data quality.\n\nThe appropriate remediation is column-level removal rather than row-level removal, since the unreliability is intrinsic to the column itself. Dropping the affected rows instead would discard 687 of 891 records (~77% of the dataset) purely due to one column, sacrificing otherwise complete data in every other feature.\n\nDataFrame.drop(columns=[...]) removes the specified column(s) from the DataFrame.", "hinglish": "@@\"Cabin\" handle karna (~77% missing)@@\n\nYahan imputation do wajah se appropriate nahi hai: Cabin values near-unique identifiers hai jinka koi meaningful central tendency nahi hota, aur ~77% missing hone ki wajah se koi bhi imputed value fabricated signal hogi, recovered information nahi — jo data quality ko behtar nahi, kharaab karegi.\n\nSahi remedy row-level removal nahi, balki column-level removal hai, kyunki unreliability khud us column ke andar hi hai. Agar iski jagah affected rows hata di jaayein, to 891 mein se 687 records (~77% dataset) sirf ek column ki wajah se waste ho jaayenge, jabki baaki har feature mein wo data complete tha.\n\nDataFrame.drop(columns=[...]) diye gaye column(s) ko DataFrame se remove kar deta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "df_clean = df_clean.drop(columns=[\"Cabin\"])\nprint(\"-> Dropped the entire 'Cabin' column (77% missing - too unreliable to impute, \"\n      \"and dropping rows instead would have discarded 687 otherwise-good passenger records).\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Let's now confirm that there are NO missing values left anywhere in df_clean.", "technical": "A final isnull().sum() verification confirms zero remaining missing values across all columns of df_clean, closing the missing-data-handling loop.", "hinglish": "Ek final isnull().sum() verification confirm karta hai ki df_clean ke har column mein ab zero missing values bache hai — missing-data-handling ka loop yahan close ho jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\nMissing value count per column AFTER cleaning (should all be 0 now):\")\nprint(df_clean.isnull().sum())\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nNOTE ON THE OTHER TWO TECHNIQUES MENTIONED IN THE ASSIGNMENT\n(Forward/backward filling, and Removal of rows):\n  - \"Forward fill\" (copy the previous row's value downward) and \"backward\n    fill\" (copy the next row's value upward) are techniques best suited to\n    data that has a natural ORDER, most commonly a time series (like daily\n    stock prices or hourly sensor readings), where \"the next reading is\n    probably close to the last one\" is a reasonable assumption. In the\n    Titanic dataset, the rows are just a list of unrelated passengers in\n    no particular meaningful order, so copying \"the previous passenger's\n    Age\" onto a completely different, unrelated passenger would not make\n    logical sense here - which is exactly why we picked median/mode\n    imputation and column removal instead, as the suitable techniques for\n    THIS dataset's columns.\n  - \"Removal of rows\" (dropping any row that has a missing value anywhere)\n    was intentionally avoided for Age and Embarked, for the reason\n    explained above: it would throw away good, complete information in\n    every other column just because of one missing cell.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "Columns that contain missing values, and how much of each is missing:\n          Missing Count  Missing %\nCabin               687      77.10\nAge                 177      19.87\nEmbarked              2       0.22\n\nINTERPRETATION (in plain English):\n  - \"Cabin\" is missing for about 77% of all passengers. That is a HUGE\n    proportion of the column - for more than 3 out of every 4 passengers,\n    we simply have no cabin information at all.\n  - \"Age\" is missing for about 20% of passengers (177 out of 891). That is\n    a meaningful chunk, but still a clear minority of the data.\n  - \"Embarked\" is missing for only 2 passengers out of 891 - a tiny, almost\n    negligible amount.\n  Each of these three columns is missing a very different AMOUNT of data,\n  so (as the assignment hints) each one deserves a different, suitable\n  technique for handling it - there is no single \"one size fits all\" fix.\n\nMedian Age calculated from the known ages: 28.0\n-> Filled all missing 'Age' values with the median age (median imputation).\nMost common 'Embarked' port (the mode): 'S'\n-> Filled the 2 missing 'Embarked' values with the mode (mode imputation).\n-> Dropped the entire 'Cabin' column (77% missing - too unreliable to impute, and dropping rows instead would have discarded 687 otherwise-good passenger records).\n\nMissing value count per column AFTER cleaning (should all be 0 now):\nPassengerId    0\nSurvived       0\nPclass         0\nName           0\nSex            0\nAge            0\nSibSp          0\nParch          0\nTicket         0\nFare           0\nEmbarked       0\ndtype: int64\n\nNOTE ON THE OTHER TWO TECHNIQUES MENTIONED IN THE ASSIGNMENT\n(Forward/backward filling, and Removal of rows):\n  - \"Forward fill\" (copy the previous row's value downward) and \"backward\n    fill\" (copy the next row's value upward) are techniques best suited to\n    data that has a natural ORDER, most commonly a time series (like daily\n    stock prices or hourly sensor readings), where \"the next reading is\n    probably close to the last one\" is a reasonable assumption. In the\n    Titanic dataset, the rows are just a list of unrelated passengers in\n    no particular meaningful order, so copying \"the previous passenger's\n    Age\" onto a completely different, unrelated passenger would not make\n    logical sense here - which is exactly why we picked median/mode\n    imputation and column removal instead, as the suitable techniques for\n    THIS dataset's columns.\n  - \"Removal of rows\" (dropping any row that has a missing value anywhere)\n    was intentionally avoided for Age and Embarked, for the reason\n    explained above: it would throw away good, complete information in\n    every other column just because of one missing cell.", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-3", "kind": "question", "number": 3, "label": "Identify duplicate records, display them, and remove duplicate records where appropriate.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 3: FINDING AND REMOVING DUPLICATE RECORDS\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A \"duplicate record\" means two (or more) ROWS in the table that have IDENTICAL values in every single column - i.e., the exact same passenger information appears more than once.\n\ndf_clean.duplicated() returns True/False for every row: True means \"this exact row has already appeared earlier in the table\" (the very first occurrence of a repeated row is marked False; only the later, repeated copies are marked True). .sum() then counts how many rows are flagged as duplicates.", "technical": "A \"duplicate record\" is a row whose values are identical, column for column, to another row in the table.\n\nDataFrame.duplicated() returns a boolean mask across rows: False for a row's first occurrence, True for every subsequent identical occurrence. Chaining .sum() over this mask yields the total duplicate-row count.", "hinglish": "\"Duplicate record\" ek aisi row hai jiski values, column-by-column, table ki kisi doosri row se bilkul identical hai.\n\nDataFrame.duplicated() rows ke across ek boolean mask return karta hai: kisi row ki pehli occurrence ke liye False, aur uske baad har identical repeat occurrence ke liye True. Is mask par .sum() chain karne se total duplicate-row count mil jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "num_duplicates = df_clean.duplicated().sum()\nprint(f\"Number of fully duplicate rows found: {num_duplicates}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df_clean[df_clean.duplicated()] uses the True/False list from above to FILTER the table down to just the duplicate rows, so we can actually look at them (the assignment specifically asks us to \"display\" them).", "technical": "Boolean-indexing df_clean with df_clean.duplicated() filters the DataFrame down to only the flagged duplicate rows, satisfying the requirement to explicitly display them.", "hinglish": "df_clean ko df_clean.duplicated() se boolean-index karne se DataFrame filter hokar sirf flagged duplicate rows tak reh jaata hai — isse assignment ki requirement puri hoti hai ki duplicates ko explicitly display kiya jaaye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "duplicate_rows = df_clean[df_clean.duplicated()]\nprint(\"\\nThe duplicate rows themselves (if any):\")\nprint(duplicate_rows if len(duplicate_rows) > 0 else \"(none - see explanation below)\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We also specifically check the \"PassengerId\" column on its own, since it is supposed to be a unique ID for every passenger. If the same PassengerId appeared twice, that would be a strong, separate red flag for duplicated/duplicated-by-mistake data, even if a few other columns happened to differ slightly (e.g. due to a typo).", "technical": "PassengerId is additionally checked in isolation, since it is intended as a unique identifier; a repeated PassengerId would be a strong independent signal of duplicated or corrupted data even if minor discrepancies existed elsewhere in the row.", "hinglish": "PassengerId ko alag se bhi check kiya jaata hai, kyunki ye ek unique identifier hone ke liye bana hai; agar koi PassengerId repeat ho jaaye, to ye duplicated ya corrupted data ka ek strong, independent signal hoga — chahe row ke baaki hisson mein chhote-mote farak kyun na ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "duplicate_ids = df_clean[\"PassengerId\"].duplicated().sum()\nprint(f\"\\nNumber of repeated 'PassengerId' values (should be a unique ID per row): {duplicate_ids}\")\n\nif num_duplicates > 0:"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "drop_duplicates() removes the extra copies and keeps only the first occurrence of each repeated row. We only run this if duplicates were actually found, otherwise there is nothing to remove.", "technical": "DataFrame.drop_duplicates() retains only the first occurrence of each repeated row, discarding subsequent duplicates; it is invoked conditionally, only when duplicates were actually detected.", "hinglish": "DataFrame.drop_duplicates() har repeated row ki sirf pehli occurrence ko rakhta hai, baaki duplicates hata deta hai; ise conditionally call kiya jaata hai, sirf tab jab duplicates actually detect hue ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    df_clean = df_clean.drop_duplicates()\n    print(f\"-> Removed {num_duplicates} duplicate row(s). New row count: {df_clean.shape[0]}\")\nelse:\n    print(\"\"\"\n-> RESULT: This particular Titanic dataset does not actually contain any\n   fully duplicated rows, and every PassengerId is unique (891 unique IDs\n   for 891 rows). This makes sense, because PassengerId acts like a\n   built-in \"row number\" that was assigned once per real passenger when\n   this dataset was first put together, so no accidental duplicate row\n   could realistically appear without also repeating a PassengerId.\n   The code above (df_clean.drop_duplicates()) is still written and would\n   correctly remove any duplicates if this were run on a different,\n   messier dataset that did contain repeated rows - it just has nothing\n   to do on this particular file.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "Number of fully duplicate rows found: 0\n\nThe duplicate rows themselves (if any):\n(none - see explanation below)\n\nNumber of repeated 'PassengerId' values (should be a unique ID per row): 0\n\n-> RESULT: This particular Titanic dataset does not actually contain any\n   fully duplicated rows, and every PassengerId is unique (891 unique IDs\n   for 891 rows). This makes sense, because PassengerId acts like a\n   built-in \"row number\" that was assigned once per real passenger when\n   this dataset was first put together, so no accidental duplicate row\n   could realistically appear without also repeating a PassengerId.\n   The code above (df_clean.drop_duplicates()) is still written and would\n   correctly remove any duplicates if this were run on a different,\n   messier dataset that did contain repeated rows - it just has nothing\n   to do on this particular file.", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-4", "kind": "question", "number": 4, "label": "Identify columns that are Irrelevant / Redundant / Identifier / Not useful for further analysis. Remove them and explain why.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 4: IDENTIFYING AND REMOVING UNHELPFUL COLUMNS\")\n\nprint(\"Columns currently remaining in the dataset:\")\nprint(list(df_clean.columns))\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nLet's go column by column and classify each one. Here, \"further analysis\"\nmeans: if we were later trying to study or predict something about these\npassengers (for example, predicting whether a passenger survived), would\nthis column actually help a model or a human analyst find a genuine\nPATTERN, or would it just get in the way?\n\n  PassengerId -> IDENTIFIER COLUMN.\n      It is just a running serial number (1, 2, 3, ... 891) assigned to\n      each row. It carries no real-world information about the passenger\n      at all (a passenger's chance of survival has nothing to do with\n      which row number they happened to be listed on). Its ONLY purpose\n      was to uniquely label each row, similar to a roll number - useful\n      for looking a specific row up, but useless as an input for finding\n      patterns. -> REMOVE.\n\n  Name -> IDENTIFIER-LIKE / NOT USEFUL IN ITS CURRENT (RAW) FORM.\n      Every single name in this dataset is unique (891 different names for\n      891 rows), so it behaves just like another identifier here, not\n      like a genuine descriptive feature. A person's literal name (e.g.\n      \"Owen Harris\") does not, by itself, influence survival. (Advanced\n      note, beyond this assignment's scope: analysts sometimes extract the\n      person's TITLE, like \"Mr./Mrs./Miss/Master\", out of this column,\n      because a title can hint at age/gender/social status - but that\n      would be creating a brand NEW column from this one, which is a\n      separate technique called \"feature engineering\", not part of basic\n      cleaning.) As it stands, in raw form, it is not useful.\n      -> REMOVE.\n\n  Ticket -> IDENTIFIER-LIKE / NOT USEFUL IN ITS CURRENT (RAW) FORM.\n      This is a messy mix of letters and numbers (like \"A/5 21171\" or\n      \"PC 17599\") that mostly behaves like an internal booking-office\n      code rather than a meaningful, analyzable feature - it has 681\n      different values across 891 rows, so it is \"nearly\" a unique ID in\n      practice, and its exact text format does not represent any\n      measurable quantity or clean category. -> REMOVE.\n\n  Sex, Pclass, Age, SibSp, Parch, Fare, Embarked, Survived ->\n      These all describe a genuine, real-world CHARACTERISTIC of the\n      passenger or their journey (their gender, travel class, age, family\n      size aboard, ticket price, boarding port) or the actual OUTCOME we\n      would eventually want to study/predict (Survived). None of these are\n      identifiers, and none of them are duplicated/redundant copies of\n      each other (each one measures something genuinely different) ->\n      KEEP all of these.\n\n  REDUNDANT COLUMNS check:\n      \"Redundant\" would mean two columns that essentially carry the SAME\n      information twice, just phrased differently. In this dataset, no\n      two columns are exact duplicates of each other's meaning: Pclass\n      (ticket class) and Fare (price paid) are correlated in real life\n      (higher class usually costs more) but they are NOT the same\n      information - Fare is a fine-grained/individual number, Pclass is\n      the passenger's class category, and a person's exact fare can still\n      vary even within the same class (due to discounts, family group\n      bookings, etc). So no column needs to be removed purely for being\n      redundant here.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Now we actually perform the removal, using the reasoning explained above.", "technical": "The actual removal is executed here, implementing the column-classification rationale established above.", "hinglish": "Yahan actual removal execute hoti hai, upar establish ki gayi column-classification reasoning ko implement karte hue."}, {"type": "code", "text": "columns_to_remove = [\"PassengerId\", \"Name\", \"Ticket\"]\ndf_final = df_clean.drop(columns=columns_to_remove)\n\nprint(f\"Removed columns: {columns_to_remove}\")\nprint(\"\\nFinal, cleaned dataset - columns remaining:\")\nprint(list(df_final.columns))\n\nprint(\"\\nFinal, cleaned dataset - first 5 rows:\")\nprint(df_final.head())\n\nprint(f\"\\nFinal shape of the cleaned dataset: {df_final.shape[0]} rows x {df_final.shape[1]} columns\")"}], "output": "Columns currently remaining in the dataset:\n['PassengerId', 'Survived', 'Pclass', 'Name', 'Sex', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'Ticket', 'Fare', 'Embarked']\n\nLet's go column by column and classify each one. Here, \"further analysis\"\nmeans: if we were later trying to study or predict something about these\npassengers (for example, predicting whether a passenger survived), would\nthis column actually help a model or a human analyst find a genuine\nPATTERN, or would it just get in the way?\n\n  PassengerId -> IDENTIFIER COLUMN.\n      It is just a running serial number (1, 2, 3, ... 891) assigned to\n      each row. It carries no real-world information about the passenger\n      at all (a passenger's chance of survival has nothing to do with\n      which row number they happened to be listed on). Its ONLY purpose\n      was to uniquely label each row, similar to a roll number - useful\n      for looking a specific row up, but useless as an input for finding\n      patterns. -> REMOVE.\n\n  Name -> IDENTIFIER-LIKE / NOT USEFUL IN ITS CURRENT (RAW) FORM.\n      Every single name in this dataset is unique (891 different names for\n      891 rows), so it behaves just like another identifier here, not\n      like a genuine descriptive feature. A person's literal name (e.g.\n      \"Owen Harris\") does not, by itself, influence survival. (Advanced\n      note, beyond this assignment's scope: analysts sometimes extract the\n      person's TITLE, like \"Mr./Mrs./Miss/Master\", out of this column,\n      because a title can hint at age/gender/social status - but that\n      would be creating a brand NEW column from this one, which is a\n      separate technique called \"feature engineering\", not part of basic\n      cleaning.) As it stands, in raw form, it is not useful.\n      -> REMOVE.\n\n  Ticket -> IDENTIFIER-LIKE / NOT USEFUL IN ITS CURRENT (RAW) FORM.\n      This is a messy mix of letters and numbers (like \"A/5 21171\" or\n      \"PC 17599\") that mostly behaves like an internal booking-office\n      code rather than a meaningful, analyzable feature - it has 681\n      different values across 891 rows, so it is \"nearly\" a unique ID in\n      practice, and its exact text format does not represent any\n      measurable quantity or clean category. -> REMOVE.\n\n  Sex, Pclass, Age, SibSp, Parch, Fare, Embarked, Survived ->\n      These all describe a genuine, real-world CHARACTERISTIC of the\n      passenger or their journey (their gender, travel class, age, family\n      size aboard, ticket price, boarding port) or the actual OUTCOME we\n      would eventually want to study/predict (Survived). None of these are\n      identifiers, and none of them are duplicated/redundant copies of\n      each other (each one measures something genuinely different) ->\n      KEEP all of these.\n\n  REDUNDANT COLUMNS check:\n      \"Redundant\" would mean two columns that essentially carry the SAME\n      information twice, just phrased differently. In this dataset, no\n      two columns are exact duplicates of each other's meaning: Pclass\n      (ticket class) and Fare (price paid) are correlated in real life\n      (higher class usually costs more) but they are NOT the same\n      information - Fare is a fine-grained/individual number, Pclass is\n      the passenger's class category, and a person's exact fare can still\n      vary even within the same class (due to discounts, family group\n      bookings, etc). So no column needs to be removed purely for being\n      redundant here.\n\nRemoved columns: ['PassengerId', 'Name', 'Ticket']\n\nFinal, cleaned dataset - columns remaining:\n['Survived', 'Pclass', 'Sex', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'Fare', 'Embarked']\n\nFinal, cleaned dataset - first 5 rows:\n   Survived  Pclass     Sex   Age  SibSp  Parch     Fare Embarked\n0         0       3    male  22.0      1      0   7.2500        S\n1         1       1  female  38.0      1      0  71.2833        C\n2         1       3  female  26.0      0      0   7.9250        S\n3         1       1  female  35.0      1      0  53.1000        S\n4         0       3    male  35.0      0      0   8.0500        S\n\nFinal shape of the cleaned dataset: 891 rows x 8 columns", "plots": []}, {"id": "wrapup", "kind": "wrapup", "number": null, "label": "Saving Your Results", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"SAVING THE CLEANED DATASET\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df.to_csv(filename, index=False) writes our final, cleaned DataFrame back out to a new .csv file on disk, so you have a permanent, ready-to-use copy of the cleaned data without needing to re-run this script every time. \"index=False\" tells pandas NOT to write its own internal row-number column into the file (we don't need an extra, meaningless numbering column in the saved file - we already removed PassengerId on purpose!).", "technical": "DataFrame.to_csv(filename, index=False) serializes the cleaned DataFrame back to disk as a persistent .csv file, avoiding the need to re-run the pipeline to obtain the cleaned data again. index=False suppresses writing pandas' internal row-index as an extra column, since a synthetic numbering column is redundant here — PassengerId was already deliberately dropped.", "hinglish": "DataFrame.to_csv(filename, index=False) cleaned DataFrame ko disk par ek persistent .csv file ke roop mein save kar deta hai, taaki cleaned data dobara paane ke liye poori pipeline re-run na karni pade. index=False pandas ke internal row-index ko ek extra column ke roop mein likhne se rok deta hai, kyunki ek synthetic numbering column yahan redundant hai — PassengerId to hum pehle hi jaan-boojh kar drop kar chuke hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "output_filename = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\")\ndf_final.to_csv(output_filename, index=False)\nprint(f\"Saved the final cleaned dataset to '{output_filename}'.\")\n\nprint(\"\\nAll 4 questions of Lab Assignment 1 are complete.\")"}], "output": "Saved the final cleaned dataset to '/Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 1/Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv'.\n\nAll 4 questions of Lab Assignment 1 are complete.", "plots": []}]}, {"num": 2, "title": "Lab Assignment 2", "subtitle": "Visualization, Outlier Detection, Validation & Feature Scaling", "dataset": "Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv", "intro": "WHAT IS THIS FILE, AND WHO IS IT FOR?\n----------------------------------------\nSame as Lab Assignment 1: this is a plain Python script (a \".py\" text file\nfull of Python instructions), written for someone who has NEVER used\nPython, pandas, matplotlib, or scikit-learn before. Every step is\nexplained in plain English before the code, and important lines have\ntheir own short \"#\" comment too.\n\nWHERE DOES THE DATA COME FROM?\n----------------------------------\nThis assignment says to do things like \"validation checks AFTER cleaning\"\n- so it continues on from Lab Assignment 1, where we already cleaned the\nraw Titanic dataset (filled in missing Age/Embarked, dropped the mostly-\nempty Cabin column, removed the identifier-like columns PassengerId/\nName/Ticket). So this script loads the ALREADY-CLEANED file that\nLab Assignment 1 produced:\n    ../Lab Assignment 1/Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\ninstead of the raw original CSV. If you haven't run Lab Assignment 1's\nscript yet, run that one first so this cleaned file actually exists.\n\nHOW TO RUN THIS FILE\n----------------------\n1. Make sure Python 3 is installed.\n2. Make sure these libraries are installed (run this once in your terminal\n   if you don't have them yet):\n        pip install pandas matplotlib scikit-learn\n3. Make sure \"Lab Assignment 1\" has already been run at least once, so\n   \"Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\" exists in its folder.\n4. Run this script from inside THIS folder (\"Lab Assignment 2\"):\n        python3 lab_assignment_2.py\n   It will:\n     - print all explanations/tables/numbers to your terminal, in order,\n     - save every plot as a \".png\" IMAGE FILE inside a new \"plots\" folder\n       created next to this script (a script running in a plain terminal\n       cannot \"pop up\" an interactive window reliably on every computer,\n       so saving each plot as an image file is the most reliable way for\n       you to actually open and look at every chart afterwards),\n     - save two new scaled versions of the dataset as CSV files at the end\n       (one Min-Max normalized, one standardized) - explained in Q4.\n\nA NOTE ON \"OR\" IN THIS ASSIGNMENT (Q4)\n------------------------------------------\nQuestion 4 says to apply \"either Min-Max Normalization, OR Standardization\".\nRather than picking only one and skipping the other, this script deliberately\ndoes BOTH, back-to-back, with a clear explanation of what each one does and\nhow their results differ - so you can see and compare both techniques\ninstead of only ever seeing one of them.\n\n==================================================================================", "sections": [{"id": "setup", "kind": "setup", "number": null, "label": "Setup: Loading the Dataset", "chunks": [{"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE LIBRARIES WE NEED@@\n\n\"os\" -> a built-in Python toolbox for working with file paths, so this script can reliably find files and create folders no matter which directory you run it from.", "technical": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE REQUIRED LIBRARIES@@\n\nos is the standard-library module for filesystem operations, imported here so the script can resolve paths and create directories reliably regardless of the invoking process's working directory.", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0: ZAROORI LIBRARIES IMPORT KARNA@@\n\nos standard-library ka module hai jo filesystem operations ke liye hota hai — ise isliye import kiya gaya hai taaki script paths reliably resolve kar sake aur folders bana sake, chahe process kisi bhi working directory se invoke kiya gaya ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "import os"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "\"pandas\" -> our table/DataFrame toolbox, exactly like in Lab Assignment 1.", "technical": "pandas is imported for tabular data structures, exactly as in Lab Assignment 1.", "hinglish": "pandas ko tabular data structures ke liye import kiya gaya hai, bilkul Lab Assignment 1 ki tarah."}, {"type": "code", "text": "import pandas as pd"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "\"matplotlib.pyplot\" is the standard Python toolbox for DRAWING charts and graphs (scatter plots, bar charts, histograms, line plots, box plots, etc). \"as plt\" is just its usual short nickname, the same way \"pandas\" is nicknamed \"pd\".", "technical": "matplotlib.pyplot is the standard plotting API in the Python data-science stack, used here for every chart type this assignment requires (scatter, bar, histogram, line, box plot). It is aliased as plt by the same convention pandas is aliased as pd.", "hinglish": "matplotlib.pyplot Python data-science stack ki standard plotting API hai, jo is assignment ke har chart type (scatter, bar, histogram, line, box plot) ke liye use hoti hai. Ise plt naam se alias kiya gaya hai, bilkul pandas ko pd alias karne wale convention ki tarah."}, {"type": "code", "text": "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "From scikit-learn (a very popular machine-learning toolbox, imported using its short name \"sklearn\"), we import two specific tools we need for Question 4 (Feature Scaling): - MinMaxScaler       -> squeezes numbers into the range [0, 1] - StandardScaler     -> rescales numbers to have mean 0 and \"spread\" 1 Both are explained in full, in plain English, right before we use them.", "technical": "From scikit-learn (imported under its conventional alias sklearn), two specific transformers required for Question 4 (Feature Scaling) are imported: MinMaxScaler, which rescales values into the [0, 1] interval, and StandardScaler, which transforms a column to zero mean and unit variance. Both are explained in full detail immediately before their use.", "hinglish": "scikit-learn se (jise conventionally sklearn alias diya jaata hai), Question 4 (Feature Scaling) ke liye zaroori do specific transformers import kiye gaye hai: MinMaxScaler, jo values ko [0, 1] interval mein rescale karta hai, aur StandardScaler, jo ek column ko zero mean aur unit variance mein transform karta hai. Dono ko unke use hone se pehle hi detail mein explain kiya gaya hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler, StandardScaler"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Cosmetic pandas settings so printed tables are easy to read (does not affect any calculation, only how things look when printed). A very large \"display.width\" makes a wide table always print as ONE line per row, instead of pandas splitting it into two confusing stacked blocks.", "technical": "Two pandas display options are set purely for presentation: unrestricted column display, and a large display.width so a wide row always prints on a single line rather than being split into stacked, backslash-joined blocks.", "hinglish": "Do pandas display options sirf presentation ke liye set kiye gaye hai: columns ka unrestricted display, aur ek bada display.width taaki wide row hamesha ek hi line mein print ho, backslash-joined stacked blocks mein split hone ke bajaye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "pd.set_option(\"display.max_columns\", None)\npd.set_option(\"display.width\", 1000)\n\n\ndef section(title):\n    \"\"\"Small helper to print a clear, easy-to-spot heading before each part\n    of the output. See Lab Assignment 1's script for a full explanation of\n    what a Python function/def is - the idea is identical here.\"\"\"\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 90)\n    print(title)\n    print(\"=\" * 90)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0b: WORK OUT RELIABLE FILE PATHS, AND MAKE A FOLDER TO SAVE PLOTS INTO@@\n\nos.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)) means: \"find the full path of THIS script file, then take just the folder part of that path\". This gives us \"this script's own folder\", regardless of where you were standing in the terminal when you ran the \"python3 ...\" command.", "technical": "@@STEP 0b: DERIVE RELIABLE FILE PATHS AND CREATE A PLOTS DIRECTORY@@\n\nos.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)) resolves the absolute path of this script file and takes just its containing directory — i.e., \"this script's own folder\" — independent of the shell's current working directory at invocation time.", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0b: RELIABLE FILE PATHS NIKALNA AUR PLOTS FOLDER BANANA@@\n\nos.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)) is script file ka absolute path resolve karke sirf uska containing directory nikalta hai — matlab \"is script ka apna folder\" — chahe script jis bhi working directory se run kiya gaya ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "this_script_folder = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "The cleaned CSV from Lab Assignment 1 lives in a sibling folder, i.e. one folder up, then into \"Lab Assignment 1\". os.path.join(...) safely glues folder/file names together into one valid path (it also automatically uses the correct slash style \"/\" or \"\\\" for your operating system).", "technical": "The cleaned CSV produced by Lab Assignment 1 resides in a sibling directory (one level up, then into \"Lab Assignment 1\"). os.path.join(...) concatenates path segments safely and portably, using the correct separator for the host operating system.", "hinglish": "Lab Assignment 1 se bani cleaned CSV ek sibling directory mein hoti hai (ek level upar, fir \"Lab Assignment 1\" ke andar). os.path.join(...) path segments ko safely aur portably jodta hai, host operating system ke sahi separator ka use karke."}, {"type": "code", "text": "cleaned_csv_path = os.path.join(\n    this_script_folder, \"..\", \"Lab Assignment 1\", \"Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\"\n)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "This is where we will save every chart image this script produces.", "technical": "This is the target directory every generated chart image will be written into.", "hinglish": "Yahi wo target directory hai jisme har generate hui chart image save hogi."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plots_folder = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"plots\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True) creates that folder if it does not exist yet. \"exist_ok=True\" simply means \"do not raise an error if the folder is already there from a previous run\" - it just leaves it as is.", "technical": "os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True) creates the directory if absent; exist_ok=True suppresses the exception that would otherwise be raised if the directory already exists from a prior run.", "hinglish": "os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True) directory ko banata hai agar wo pehle se nahi hai; exist_ok=True us exception ko suppress kar deta hai jo warna tab raise hoti jab directory pehle se kisi purani run se already exist karti."}, {"type": "code", "text": "os.makedirs(plots_folder, exist_ok=True)\n\n\ndef save_current_plot(filename):\n    \"\"\"\n    Small helper function: saves whatever chart matplotlib currently has\n    \"on screen\" (in its invisible drawing canvas) to an image file inside\n    our \"plots\" folder, then clears that canvas so the NEXT chart we draw\n    starts fresh and does not accidentally overlap with this one.\n    \"\"\"\n    full_path = os.path.join(plots_folder, filename)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.tight_layout() nudges the chart's margins/spacing so titles and axis labels don't get cut off or overlap - purely cosmetic.", "technical": "plt.tight_layout() adjusts subplot spacing/margins so titles and axis labels are not clipped or overlapping — a purely cosmetic layout call.", "hinglish": "plt.tight_layout() subplot ki spacing/margins adjust karta hai taaki titles aur axis labels clip ya overlap na ho — ye ek purely cosmetic layout call hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    plt.tight_layout()"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.savefig(...) writes the current chart out as a real image file (a .png here) that you can double-click and open like any photo.", "technical": "plt.savefig(...) serializes the currently active figure to disk as an image file (.png here), producing a persistent artifact independent of the Python process.", "hinglish": "plt.savefig(...) currently active figure ko disk par ek image file (.png) ke roop mein serialize kar deta hai, jo Python process se independent, ek persistent artifact ban jaati hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    plt.savefig(full_path)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.close() closes/clears the current chart from memory, so the next plt.plot(...)/plt.bar(...)/etc call starts a brand-new, empty chart.", "technical": "plt.close() releases the current figure from memory, ensuring the next plt.plot(...)/plt.bar(...)/etc call begins drawing onto a fresh, empty canvas rather than accumulating onto the previous figure.", "hinglish": "plt.close() current figure ko memory se release kar deta hai, isse confirm hota hai ki agli plt.plot(...)/plt.bar(...)/etc call ek fresh, empty canvas par draw karegi, purani figure ke upar accumulate hone ke bajaye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    plt.close()\n    print(f\"  Saved plot -> plots/{filename}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0c: LOAD THE ALREADY-CLEANED DATASET FROM LAB ASSIGNMENT 1@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0c: LOAD THE PRE-CLEANED DATASET FROM LAB ASSIGNMENT 1@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0c: LAB ASSIGNMENT 1 SE PRE-CLEANED DATASET LOAD KARNA@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "section(\"LOADING THE CLEANED DATASET FROM LAB ASSIGNMENT 1\")\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(cleaned_csv_path)\nprint(f\"Loaded cleaned dataset from: {cleaned_csv_path}\")\nprint(f\"Shape: {df.shape[0]} rows x {df.shape[1]} columns\")\nprint(\"Columns:\", list(df.columns))\nprint(\"\\nFirst 5 rows:\")\nprint(df.head())\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nReminder of what each remaining column means:\n  Survived : 0 = did not survive, 1 = survived\n  Pclass   : ticket class (1 = 1st/best, 3 = 3rd/cheapest)  -> a CATEGORY, even though it's stored as a number\n  Sex      : male / female                                  -> a CATEGORY (text)\n  Age      : age in years                                   -> a NUMBER\n  SibSp    : number of siblings/spouses aboard               -> a NUMBER (count)\n  Parch    : number of parents/children aboard                -> a NUMBER (count)\n  Fare     : ticket price paid                                -> a NUMBER\n  Embarked : port of boarding (C, Q, or S)                    -> a CATEGORY (text)\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "Loaded cleaned dataset from: /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 2/../Lab Assignment 1/Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\nShape: 891 rows x 8 columns\nColumns: ['Survived', 'Pclass', 'Sex', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'Fare', 'Embarked']\n\nFirst 5 rows:\n   Survived  Pclass     Sex   Age  SibSp  Parch     Fare Embarked\n0         0       3    male  22.0      1      0   7.2500        S\n1         1       1  female  38.0      1      0  71.2833        C\n2         1       3  female  26.0      0      0   7.9250        S\n3         1       1  female  35.0      1      0  53.1000        S\n4         0       3    male  35.0      0      0   8.0500        S\n\nReminder of what each remaining column means:\n  Survived : 0 = did not survive, 1 = survived\n  Pclass   : ticket class (1 = 1st/best, 3 = 3rd/cheapest)  -> a CATEGORY, even though it's stored as a number\n  Sex      : male / female                                  -> a CATEGORY (text)\n  Age      : age in years                                   -> a NUMBER\n  SibSp    : number of siblings/spouses aboard               -> a NUMBER (count)\n  Parch    : number of parents/children aboard                -> a NUMBER (count)\n  Fare     : ticket price paid                                -> a NUMBER\n  Embarked : port of boarding (C, Q, or S)                    -> a CATEGORY (text)", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-1", "kind": "question", "number": 1, "label": "Visualize the data using different plots - scatter plot, bar plot, histogram, line plot.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 1: VISUALIZING THE DATA WITH DIFFERENT PLOT TYPES\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nEach plot type below is chosen because it is the RIGHT tool for a\nparticular kind of question - just like you wouldn't use a hammer to turn\na screw. We explain, for each one, WHY that plot type suits that\nparticular question.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@1a) SCATTER PLOT: Age vs Fare, coloured by whether they survived@@\n\nWHY a scatter plot here? A scatter plot puts one NUMERIC variable on the x-axis and another NUMERIC variable on the y-axis, drawing one dot per row. It is the right tool when you want to see whether TWO numeric columns seem to relate to each other at all (e.g. \"do older passengers tend to pay more, or less, for their ticket?\"). Here we also colour each dot by \"Survived\" (0 or 1) as a bonus, so we can visually see if survival seems to cluster in certain Age/Fare regions.", "technical": "@@(a) Scatter plot: Age vs. Fare, coloured by survival outcome@@\n\nA scatter plot maps one numeric variable to the x-axis and a second numeric variable to the y-axis, rendering one point per observation. It is the appropriate choice for examining whether an association exists between two continuous variables (e.g., does Fare correlate with Age?). Colour-encoding each point by the binary Survived label overlays a categorical dimension, letting any visual clustering of survival outcomes across the Age/Fare plane be inspected at a glance.", "hinglish": "@@(a) Scatter plot: Age vs Fare, Survival ke hisaab se colour kiya gaya@@\n\nScatter plot ek numeric variable ko x-axis par aur doosre numeric variable ko y-axis par map karta hai, har observation ke liye ek point draw karte hue. Ye tab sahi choice hota hai jab check karna ho ki do continuous variables ke beech koi association hai ya nahi (jaise, kya Fare aur Age correlate karte hai?). Har point ko binary Survived label se colour karne se ek categorical dimension overlay ho jaati hai, jisse Age/Fare plane par survival outcomes ka koi visual clustering hai ya nahi, wo ek nazar mein dikh jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"1a) Scatter plot: Age vs Fare (coloured by Survived) ...\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.figure(...) starts a brand new, blank chart canvas of a chosen size (width=8 inches, height=6 inches here) - purely a display preference.", "technical": "plt.figure(figsize=...) initializes a new, blank figure canvas of the specified dimensions (in inches) — a display preference with no bearing on the underlying data.", "hinglish": "plt.figure(figsize=...) di gayi dimensions (inches mein) ka ek naya, blank figure canvas initialize karta hai — ye sirf ek display preference hai, underlying data par iska koi asar nahi."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We split the data into two groups (did not survive / survived) simply so we can give them two different colours AND a legend label. df[df[\"Survived\"] == 0] filters the table down to only the rows where the Survived column equals 0.", "technical": "The data is partitioned into two subsets by boolean indexing on Survived, solely to assign each subset a distinct colour and legend label in separate plt.scatter() calls. df[df[\"Survived\"] == 0] filters to rows where the boolean condition evaluates True.", "hinglish": "Data ko Survived par boolean indexing karke do subsets mein baanta jaata hai, sirf isliye taaki alag plt.scatter() calls mein har subset ko ek distinct colour aur legend label diya ja sake. df[df[\"Survived\"] == 0] filter karke sirf wahi rows deta hai jahan boolean condition True evaluate hoti hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "did_not_survive = df[df[\"Survived\"] == 0]\nsurvived = df[df[\"Survived\"] == 1]"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.scatter(x_values, y_values, ...) draws one dot per (x, y) pair. \"alpha=0.6\" makes the dots slightly see-through, which helps when many dots overlap on top of each other.", "technical": "plt.scatter(x, y, ...) renders one marker per (x, y) pair. The alpha=0.6 parameter sets partial transparency, which improves legibility when many points overplot in a dense region.", "hinglish": "plt.scatter(x, y, ...) har (x, y) pair ke liye ek marker render karta hai. alpha=0.6 parameter partial transparency set karta hai, jisse legibility improve hoti hai jab kisi dense region mein bahut saare points overlap kar rahe ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.scatter(did_not_survive[\"Age\"], did_not_survive[\"Fare\"],\n            alpha=0.6, label=\"Did not survive (0)\", color=\"firebrick\")\nplt.scatter(survived[\"Age\"], survived[\"Fare\"],\n            alpha=0.6, label=\"Survived (1)\", color=\"seagreen\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.xlabel/ylabel/title just add text labels to the chart so anyone looking at the saved image understands what it shows without needing to read this script.", "technical": "plt.xlabel/ylabel/title annotate the figure with descriptive text, so the saved image is self-explanatory without requiring the reader to consult the source code.", "hinglish": "plt.xlabel/ylabel/title figure ko descriptive text se annotate karte hai, taaki saved image khud-explanatory ho aur reader ko samajhne ke liye source code dekhne ki zaroorat na pade."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.xlabel(\"Age (years)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Fare (ticket price)\")\nplt.title(\"Scatter Plot: Age vs Fare, coloured by Survival\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.legend() draws the little labelled colour-key box on the chart, using the \"label=\" text we gave each plt.scatter(...) call above.", "technical": "plt.legend() renders the colour-key box, sourcing its labels from the label= keyword argument supplied to each preceding plt.scatter() call.", "hinglish": "plt.legend() colour-key box render karta hai, jiske labels har pehle wale plt.scatter() call ko diye gaye label= keyword argument se aate hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.legend()\nsave_current_plot(\"1a_scatter_age_vs_fare.png\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\n   How to read this chart: most passengers cluster at low Fare values\n   regardless of age, with a \"tail\" of higher-fare (likely 1st class)\n   passengers spread across many ages. There isn't one single obvious\n   straight-line relationship between Age and Fare, but you can visually\n   see that a good number of the low-Fare, \"everyday\" passengers did NOT\n   survive (more red dots low-down), which hints - just from LOOKING at\n   the picture - that Fare (and by extension, ticket class) might be\n   related to survival chances. A proper statistical/model-based analysis\n   would come later; this plot is just a first visual clue.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@1b) BAR PLOT: number of passengers who survived vs did not, split by Sex@@\n\nWHY a bar plot here? A bar plot is the right tool for comparing COUNTS or totals across a small number of CATEGORIES (as opposed to a scatter plot, which needs two numeric columns). Here, \"Sex\" (male/female) and \"Survived\" (0/1) are both categories, so we count how many passengers fall into each combination and draw one bar per combination.", "technical": "@@(b) Bar plot: survival counts by sex@@\n\nA bar plot is the appropriate chart for comparing aggregate counts across a small number of discrete categories — unlike a scatter plot, it does not require two numeric axes. Since Sex (male/female) and Survived (0/1) are both categorical, tabulating passenger counts per combination and rendering one bar per combination is the natural visualization.", "hinglish": "@@(b) Bar plot: Sex ke hisaab se survival counts@@\n\nBar plot ek chhoti si list of discrete categories ke across aggregate counts compare karne ke liye sahi chart hai — scatter plot ke ulat, isme do numeric axes ki zaroorat nahi hoti. Chunki Sex (male/female) aur Survived (0/1) dono categorical hai, har combination ke passenger counts tabulate karke ek bar per combination render karna hi natural visualization hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\n1b) Bar plot: passenger counts by Sex, split by Survived ...\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "pd.crosstab(a, b) builds a small cross-tabulation table: for every unique value of \"a\" (rows) and \"b\" (columns), it counts how many rows of the original data had that exact combination. Here it gives us a table with Sex as rows (male/female) and Survived as columns (0/1), filled with passenger counts.", "technical": "pd.crosstab(a, b) builds a contingency table: for every unique combination of a's values (rows) and b's values (columns), it counts matching rows in the source data. Here it produces a Sex × Survived contingency table of passenger counts.", "hinglish": "pd.crosstab(a, b) ek contingency table banata hai: a ki values (rows) aur b ki values (columns) ke har unique combination ke liye, ye source data mein matching rows count karta hai. Yahan ye Sex × Survived ka ek contingency table banata hai jisme passenger counts hote hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "counts_table = pd.crosstab(df[\"Sex\"], df[\"Survived\"])\ncounts_table.columns = [\"Did Not Survive (0)\", \"Survived (1)\"]\nprint(\"Counts table used for the bar plot:\")\nprint(counts_table)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "counts_table.plot(kind=\"bar\", ...) is a pandas convenience shortcut that internally calls matplotlib to draw one group of bars per row of the table (here: one group for \"female\", one for \"male\"), with one bar per column inside each group (Did Not Survive / Survived).", "technical": "DataFrame.plot(kind=\"bar\", ...) is a pandas convenience wrapper around matplotlib that draws one group of bars per row of the table (one group for \"female\", one for \"male\"), with one bar per column within each group.", "hinglish": "DataFrame.plot(kind=\"bar\", ...) ek pandas convenience wrapper hai jo matplotlib ko internally call karta hai aur table ki har row ke liye bars ka ek group draw karta hai (\"female\" ke liye ek group, \"male\" ke liye ek), har group ke andar har column ke liye ek bar."}, {"type": "code", "text": "counts_table.plot(kind=\"bar\", ax=plt.gca(), color=[\"firebrick\", \"seagreen\"])"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.gca() means \"get current axes\" - it just tells pandas to draw onto the chart canvas we already opened with plt.figure(...) above, instead of silently opening yet another separate one.", "technical": "plt.gca() (\"get current axes\") directs pandas to draw onto the figure canvas already opened via plt.figure() above, rather than implicitly creating a separate figure.", "hinglish": "plt.gca() (\"get current axes\") pandas ko batata hai ki upar plt.figure() se khole gaye figure canvas par hi draw kare, kisi alag figure ko implicitly create karne ke bajaye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.xlabel(\"Sex\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Number of Passengers\")\nplt.title(\"Bar Plot: Survival Counts by Sex\")\nplt.xticks(rotation=0)  # keep the \"male\"/\"female\" labels horizontal, not tilted\nsave_current_plot(\"1b_bar_survival_by_sex.png\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\n   How to read this chart: this immediately shows a big difference between\n   the two groups - far more female passengers survived than did not,\n   while for male passengers it's the reverse (far more did not survive\n   than did). This is one of the most well-known real patterns in the\n   Titanic dataset (\"women and children first\").\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@1c) HISTOGRAM: distribution of passenger Age@@\n\nWHY a histogram here? A histogram is the right tool for looking at the DISTRIBUTION (the overall shape/spread) of a SINGLE numeric column - it groups values into equal-width \"bins\" (e.g. 0-5 years, 5-10 years, ...) and draws a bar showing how many passengers fall into each bin. This tells us things a plain average never could, like \"is Age fairly evenly spread, or bunched up around a particular range?\".", "technical": "@@(c) Histogram: distribution of passenger Age@@\n\nA histogram is the appropriate chart for visualizing the distribution — shape and spread — of a single numeric variable. It partitions the value range into equal-width bins and renders a bar per bin proportional to the count of observations falling within it, revealing structure (skewness, modality, spread) that a summary statistic alone cannot convey.", "hinglish": "@@(c) Histogram: passenger Age ki distribution@@\n\nHistogram ek single numeric variable ki distribution — shape aur spread — visualize karne ke liye sahi chart hai. Ye value range ko equal-width bins mein baant deta hai aur har bin ke liye ek bar draw karta hai jo us bin mein aane wali observations ke count ke proportional hoti hai — isse structure (skewness, modality, spread) dikhta hai jo akela summary statistic nahi dikha sakta."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\n1c) Histogram: distribution of passenger Age ...\")\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.hist(data, bins=20, ...) automatically splits the Age values into 20 equal-width bins across the full min-to-max range, and counts how many passengers land in each bin.", "technical": "plt.hist(data, bins=20, ...) partitions the Age values into 20 equal-width bins spanning the full observed range, and counts observations per bin.", "hinglish": "plt.hist(data, bins=20, ...) Age values ko poore observed range mein 20 equal-width bins mein baant deta hai, aur har bin mein observations count karta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.hist(df[\"Age\"], bins=20, color=\"steelblue\", edgecolor=\"black\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Age (years)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Number of Passengers\")\nplt.title(\"Histogram: Distribution of Passenger Age\")\nsave_current_plot(\"1c_histogram_age.png\")\n\nprint(f\"\"\"\n   How to read this chart: you should see a large spike right around\n   {df['Age'].median():.0f} years old (that's the median Age value we used\n   back in Lab Assignment 1 to fill in the 177 originally-missing Age\n   entries - so of course a lot of passengers now share that exact value).\n   Besides that artificial spike, most passengers genuinely fall between\n   their late teens and about 40 years old, with far fewer very young\n   children or elderly passengers above 60.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@1d) LINE PLOT: average Fare paid, by passenger class (Pclass)@@\n\nWHY a line plot here, and why THIS particular comparison? A line plot is the right tool when your x-axis values have a natural ORDER and you want to show a TREND as you move along that order (classic example: a value changing over time). The Titanic dataset itself has no dates or times to plot against, but \"Pclass\" (1st, 2nd, 3rd class) IS naturally ordered (1st class is \"better than\" 2nd, which is \"better than\" 3rd), so a line plot showing \"average Fare as class gets worse\" is a meaningful, valid use of a line plot even without a time axis.", "technical": "@@(d) Line plot: average Fare by passenger class@@\n\nA line plot is appropriate when the x-axis has a natural ordering and the goal is to depict a trend across that order — canonically a value changing over time, but any ordinal axis qualifies. Pclass (1st/2nd/3rd) is ordinal even without a time dimension, so plotting average Fare against ordered class is a valid, meaningful use of a line plot.", "hinglish": "@@(d) Line plot: passenger class ke hisaab se average Fare@@\n\nLine plot tab sahi hota hai jab x-axis ka koi natural ordering ho aur goal us order ke across ek trend dikhana ho — classic example time ke saath badalti value hai, lekin koi bhi ordinal axis qualify karta hai. Pclass (1st/2nd/3rd) bina kisi time dimension ke bhi ordinal hai, isliye ordered class ke against average Fare plot karna line plot ka ek valid, meaningful use hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\n1d) Line plot: average Fare by passenger class (Pclass) ...\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df.groupby(\"Pclass\")[\"Fare\"].mean() groups all rows by their Pclass value (1, 2, or 3), then calculates the average Fare within each group. .sort_index() makes sure the classes come out in order 1, 2, 3 (not in some random order), which matters because a line plot's whole point is to show a trend along an ordered axis.", "technical": "df.groupby(\"Pclass\")[\"Fare\"].mean() groups observations by their Pclass value and computes the within-group mean Fare. .sort_index() enforces ascending class order (1, 2, 3), which matters because a line plot's entire purpose is to depict a trend along an ordered axis.", "hinglish": "df.groupby(\"Pclass\")[\"Fare\"].mean() observations ko unke Pclass value ke hisaab se group karta hai aur har group ka mean Fare calculate karta hai. .sort_index() classes ko ascending order (1, 2, 3) mein le aata hai, jo zaroori hai kyunki line plot ka pura purpose hi ek ordered axis ke along trend dikhana hota hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "avg_fare_by_class = df.groupby(\"Pclass\")[\"Fare\"].mean().sort_index()\nprint(\"Average fare per passenger class:\")\nprint(avg_fare_by_class)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "marker=\"o\" draws a small circle at every actual data point along the line, which makes it easy to see exactly where each class's value sits (rather than just a smooth line with no visible individual points).", "technical": "marker=\"o\" renders an explicit marker at every actual data point along the line, making each class's exact value identifiable rather than only showing an unmarked interpolated line.", "hinglish": "marker=\"o\" line ke har actual data point par ek explicit marker render karta hai, jisse har class ki exact value pehchani ja sakti hai, sirf ek bina-marker wali interpolated line dikhane ke bajaye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.plot(avg_fare_by_class.index, avg_fare_by_class.values, marker=\"o\", color=\"darkorange\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Passenger Class (Pclass)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Average Fare Paid\")\nplt.title(\"Line Plot: Average Fare by Passenger Class\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Force the x-axis to only show the actual class numbers (1, 2, 3), not fractional ticks like 1.5, 2.5, since Pclass only ever takes whole values.", "technical": "The x-axis ticks are constrained to the actual class values (1, 2, 3) rather than allowing matplotlib to auto-generate fractional ticks (1.5, 2.5, ...), since Pclass is inherently a small set of discrete integers.", "hinglish": "x-axis ke ticks ko actual class values (1, 2, 3) tak hi constrain kiya gaya hai, matplotlib ko fractional ticks (1.5, 2.5, ...) auto-generate karne dene ke bajaye, kyunki Pclass inherently discrete integers ka ek chhota set hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.xticks(avg_fare_by_class.index)\nsave_current_plot(\"1d_line_avg_fare_by_class.png\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\n   How to read this chart: the line drops sharply from 1st class to 3rd\n   class, confirming that 1st class tickets cost dramatically more on\n   average than 3rd class ones - exactly what you'd expect for a \"better\"\n   travel class, and a good sanity check that our cleaned data still makes\n   real-world sense.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "Each plot type below is chosen because it is the RIGHT tool for a\nparticular kind of question - just like you wouldn't use a hammer to turn\na screw. We explain, for each one, WHY that plot type suits that\nparticular question.\n\n1a) Scatter plot: Age vs Fare (coloured by Survived) ...\n  Saved plot -> plots/1a_scatter_age_vs_fare.png\n\n   How to read this chart: most passengers cluster at low Fare values\n   regardless of age, with a \"tail\" of higher-fare (likely 1st class)\n   passengers spread across many ages. There isn't one single obvious\n   straight-line relationship between Age and Fare, but you can visually\n   see that a good number of the low-Fare, \"everyday\" passengers did NOT\n   survive (more red dots low-down), which hints - just from LOOKING at\n   the picture - that Fare (and by extension, ticket class) might be\n   related to survival chances. A proper statistical/model-based analysis\n   would come later; this plot is just a first visual clue.\n\n\n1b) Bar plot: passenger counts by Sex, split by Survived ...\nCounts table used for the bar plot:\n        Did Not Survive (0)  Survived (1)\nSex                                      \nfemale                   81           233\nmale                    468           109\n  Saved plot -> plots/1b_bar_survival_by_sex.png\n\n   How to read this chart: this immediately shows a big difference between\n   the two groups - far more female passengers survived than did not,\n   while for male passengers it's the reverse (far more did not survive\n   than did). This is one of the most well-known real patterns in the\n   Titanic dataset (\"women and children first\").\n\n\n1c) Histogram: distribution of passenger Age ...\n  Saved plot -> plots/1c_histogram_age.png\n\n   How to read this chart: you should see a large spike right around\n   28 years old (that's the median Age value we used\n   back in Lab Assignment 1 to fill in the 177 originally-missing Age\n   entries - so of course a lot of passengers now share that exact value).\n   Besides that artificial spike, most passengers genuinely fall between\n   their late teens and about 40 years old, with far fewer very young\n   children or elderly passengers above 60.\n\n\n1d) Line plot: average Fare by passenger class (Pclass) ...\nAverage fare per passenger class:\nPclass\n1    84.154687\n2    20.662183\n3    13.675550\nName: Fare, dtype: float64\n  Saved plot -> plots/1d_line_avg_fare_by_class.png\n\n   How to read this chart: the line drops sharply from 1st class to 3rd\n   class, confirming that 1st class tickets cost dramatically more on\n   average than 3rd class ones - exactly what you'd expect for a \"better\"\n   travel class, and a good sanity check that our cleaned data still makes\n   real-world sense.", "plots": [{"filename": "1a_scatter_age_vs_fare.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}, {"filename": "1b_bar_survival_by_sex.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}, {"filename": "1c_histogram_age.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}, {"filename": "1d_line_avg_fare_by_class.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}, {"id": "question-2", "kind": "question", "number": 2, "label": "Identify outliers in at least two numerical variables using a Box plot. Visualize the detected outliers.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 2: DETECTING OUTLIERS WITH BOX PLOTS (Age and Fare)\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHAT IS AN \"OUTLIER\"?\nAn outlier is a data point that is unusually far away from most of the\nother values in its column - so extreme that it looks like it might be a\nmeasurement mistake, a rare/unusual real case, or at least a value that\ncould distort calculations like the average if not handled carefully.\n\nWHAT IS A BOX PLOT, AND HOW DOES IT FIND OUTLIERS?\nA box plot is a chart built from 5 numbers taken from a sorted column:\n  - the minimum \"non-outlier\" value\n  - Q1 (the \"25th percentile\" - 25% of values are below this point)\n  - the median (the middle value, \"50th percentile\")\n  - Q3 (the \"75th percentile\" - 75% of values are below this point)\n  - the maximum \"non-outlier\" value\nIt draws a box from Q1 to Q3 (this box contains the \"middle 50%\" of the\ndata, called the IQR = Interquartile Range = Q3 - Q1), a line inside the\nbox for the median, and \"whiskers\" (thin lines) extending out to the\nfurthest points that are still considered normal. ANY point drawn further\nout than the whiskers is flagged as an outlier and shown as an individual\ndot.\n\nThe standard mathematical rule a box plot uses to decide what counts as\nan outlier (called the \"1.5 x IQR rule\") is:\n    lower_bound = Q1 - 1.5 x IQR\n    upper_bound = Q3 + 1.5 x IQR\nAny value BELOW lower_bound or ABOVE upper_bound is flagged as an outlier.\nWe will calculate this ourselves below (so you can see the exact numbers\nand exact outlier rows, not just look at a picture), and then also draw\nthe actual box plot picture that the assignment asks for.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef detect_outliers_iqr(column_name):\n    \"\"\"\n    A reusable helper function: given the NAME of a numeric column in our\n    dataframe \"df\", this calculates Q1, Q3, the IQR, and the lower/upper\n    bounds using the 1.5 x IQR rule described above, prints a summary, and\n    returns the actual rows of \"df\" that are flagged as outliers for that\n    column.\n    \"\"\""}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": ".quantile(0.25) and .quantile(0.75) calculate Q1 and Q3 directly - pandas does the \"sort the column, then find the 25%/75% mark\" work for us.", "technical": "Series.quantile(0.25) and .quantile(0.75) compute Q1 and Q3 directly, using pandas' built-in percentile-interpolation implementation, rather than requiring a manual sort-and-index computation.", "hinglish": "Series.quantile(0.25) aur .quantile(0.75) Q1 aur Q3 seedhe compute karte hai, pandas ke built-in percentile-interpolation implementation ka use karke — manually sort-aur-index karne ki zaroorat nahi padti."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    q1 = df[column_name].quantile(0.25)\n    q3 = df[column_name].quantile(0.75)\n    iqr = q3 - q1\n    lower_bound = q1 - 1.5 * iqr\n    upper_bound = q3 + 1.5 * iqr\n\n    print(f\"\\n--- Outlier detection for '{column_name}' ---\")\n    print(f\"Q1 (25th percentile): {q1:.2f}\")\n    print(f\"Q3 (75th percentile): {q3:.2f}\")\n    print(f\"IQR (Q3 - Q1)       : {iqr:.2f}\")\n    print(f\"Lower bound (Q1 - 1.5*IQR): {lower_bound:.2f}\")\n    print(f\"Upper bound (Q3 + 1.5*IQR): {upper_bound:.2f}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "This filters df down to only the rows where the column's value is either below the lower bound OR (\"|\" is Python's \"or\" for these row-by-row True/False filters) above the upper bound.", "technical": "This boolean-filters df down to rows where the column value lies outside [lower_bound, upper_bound] — the \"|\" operator is Python's element-wise logical OR across two boolean Series.", "hinglish": "Ye df ko boolean-filter karke sirf unhi rows tak limit karta hai jahan column value [lower_bound, upper_bound] range se bahar ho — \"|\" operator do boolean Series ke beech Python ka element-wise logical OR hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    outlier_rows = df[(df[column_name] < lower_bound) | (df[column_name] > upper_bound)]\n    print(f\"Number of outlier rows found in '{column_name}': {len(outlier_rows)} \"\n          f\"out of {len(df)} total rows ({len(outlier_rows) / len(df) * 100:.1f}%)\")\n\n    return outlier_rows, lower_bound, upper_bound"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Numeric variable 1: Age@@", "technical": "@@Numeric variable 1: Age@@", "hinglish": "@@Numeric variable 1: Age@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "age_outliers, age_lower, age_upper = detect_outliers_iqr(\"Age\")\nprint(\"A few of the actual outlier rows found for 'Age':\")\nprint(age_outliers[[\"Survived\", \"Pclass\", \"Sex\", \"Age\", \"Fare\"]].head(10))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Numeric variable 2: Fare@@", "technical": "@@Numeric variable 2: Fare@@", "hinglish": "@@Numeric variable 2: Fare@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "fare_outliers, fare_lower, fare_upper = detect_outliers_iqr(\"Fare\")\nprint(\"\\nA few of the actual outlier rows found for 'Fare':\")\nprint(fare_outliers[[\"Survived\", \"Pclass\", \"Sex\", \"Age\", \"Fare\"]].head(10))\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nINTERPRETATION:\n  - 'Age' outliers are mostly on the HIGH end (elderly passengers in their\n    60s-80s) - genuinely unusual ages compared to the bulk of passengers,\n    but still perfectly realistic human ages, not data-entry mistakes.\n  - 'Fare' outliers are almost entirely very HIGH fares (some passengers\n    paid dramatically more than most - these were likely 1st class\n    passengers with large private suites/family bookings). Fare cannot go\n    below 0 in a valid record, so there are no \"too low\" Fare outliers\n    here.\n  In both cases, these look like genuine, valid extreme values rather\n  than data errors, so the appropriate handling here is normally to KEEP\n  them but be aware of them (for example, remembering they exist is\n  exactly why Question 4 below considers scaling techniques that reduce\n  the influence of such extreme values).\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Now draw the actual box plots (the picture the assignment asks for)@@", "technical": "@@Rendering the box plots the assignment requires@@", "hinglish": "@@Assignment mein maange gaye box plots draw karna@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\nDrawing box plots for Age and Fare, side by side ...\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "plt.subplots(1, 2, ...) creates ONE figure containing a 1-row-by-2-column grid of small charts (\"axes\") side by side, so we can show the Age box plot and the Fare box plot together in a single image for easy comparison.", "technical": "plt.subplots(1, 2, ...) constructs a single figure containing a 1×2 grid of axes, enabling the Age and Fare box plots to be rendered side by side within one image for direct visual comparison.", "hinglish": "plt.subplots(1, 2, ...) ek hi figure banata hai jisme 1×2 grid of axes hoti hai, jisse Age aur Fare ke box plots ek hi image mein side by side render ho jaate hai direct visual comparison ke liye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 5))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "axes[0].boxplot([...]) draws a box plot of the Age column into the FIRST (leftmost) small chart. dropna() removes any missing values before plotting (there shouldn't be any left after Lab Assignment 1's cleaning, but it's a harmless safety step). Box plots are vertical by default, so we don't need to pass any extra orientation option.", "technical": "axes[0].boxplot([...]) renders the Age column's box plot in the first (leftmost) axes. dropna() strips any missing values prior to plotting (a defensive no-op here, since Lab Assignment 1's cleaning already eliminated them). Box plots default to a vertical orientation, so no explicit orientation argument is required.", "hinglish": "axes[0].boxplot([...]) pehli (leftmost) axes mein Age column ka box plot render karta hai. dropna() plotting se pehle koi bhi missing values hata deta hai (yahan ye ek defensive no-op hai, kyunki Lab Assignment 1 ki cleaning unhe pehle hi hata chuki hai). Box plots default roop se vertical orientation mein hote hai, isliye koi explicit orientation argument dene ki zaroorat nahi."}, {"type": "code", "text": "axes[0].boxplot(df[\"Age\"].dropna())\naxes[0].set_title(\"Box Plot: Age\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"Age (years)\")\n\naxes[1].boxplot(df[\"Fare\"].dropna())\naxes[1].set_title(\"Box Plot: Fare\")\naxes[1].set_ylabel(\"Fare\")\n\nsave_current_plot(\"2a_boxplots_age_and_fare.png\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Also visualize WHICH specific points were flagged, on top of all the data@@\n\nThis second chart makes the detected outliers extra obvious by plotting every passenger's Age (x-axis) and Fare (y-axis) as small grey dots, then drawing the flagged outlier rows again on top in bright red, so you can see exactly which real passengers were flagged, and by which column.", "technical": "@@Additionally visualizing exactly which points were flagged, overlaid on the full data@@\n\nThis second figure makes the flagged outliers unambiguous by plotting every passenger's Age/Fare as a faint grey point, then overplotting the flagged outlier rows in a distinct colour — making it immediately clear which specific records were flagged, and by which variable.", "hinglish": "@@Ye bhi dikhana ki exactly kaunse points flag hue, poore data ke upar@@\n\nYe doosri figure flagged outliers ko bilkul clear kar deti hai — har passenger ke Age/Fare ko ek halke grey point ke roop mein plot karke, fir flagged outlier rows ko ek alag colour mein upar se plot karke — isse turant saaf ho jaata hai ki kaunse specific records flag hue, aur kis variable ki wajah se."}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"Drawing a scatter plot highlighting the specific outlier passengers ...\")\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))\nplt.scatter(df[\"Age\"], df[\"Fare\"], color=\"lightgray\", label=\"Normal passengers\", alpha=0.6)\nplt.scatter(age_outliers[\"Age\"], age_outliers[\"Fare\"], color=\"darkorange\",\n            label=\"Age outliers\", alpha=0.8, marker=\"^\")\nplt.scatter(fare_outliers[\"Age\"], fare_outliers[\"Fare\"], color=\"crimson\",\n            label=\"Fare outliers\", alpha=0.6, marker=\"x\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Age (years)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Fare\")\nplt.title(\"Highlighting Detected Age and Fare Outliers\")\nplt.legend()\nsave_current_plot(\"2b_outliers_highlighted_scatter.png\")"}], "output": "WHAT IS AN \"OUTLIER\"?\nAn outlier is a data point that is unusually far away from most of the\nother values in its column - so extreme that it looks like it might be a\nmeasurement mistake, a rare/unusual real case, or at least a value that\ncould distort calculations like the average if not handled carefully.\n\nWHAT IS A BOX PLOT, AND HOW DOES IT FIND OUTLIERS?\nA box plot is a chart built from 5 numbers taken from a sorted column:\n  - the minimum \"non-outlier\" value\n  - Q1 (the \"25th percentile\" - 25% of values are below this point)\n  - the median (the middle value, \"50th percentile\")\n  - Q3 (the \"75th percentile\" - 75% of values are below this point)\n  - the maximum \"non-outlier\" value\nIt draws a box from Q1 to Q3 (this box contains the \"middle 50%\" of the\ndata, called the IQR = Interquartile Range = Q3 - Q1), a line inside the\nbox for the median, and \"whiskers\" (thin lines) extending out to the\nfurthest points that are still considered normal. ANY point drawn further\nout than the whiskers is flagged as an outlier and shown as an individual\ndot.\n\nThe standard mathematical rule a box plot uses to decide what counts as\nan outlier (called the \"1.5 x IQR rule\") is:\n    lower_bound = Q1 - 1.5 x IQR\n    upper_bound = Q3 + 1.5 x IQR\nAny value BELOW lower_bound or ABOVE upper_bound is flagged as an outlier.\nWe will calculate this ourselves below (so you can see the exact numbers\nand exact outlier rows, not just look at a picture), and then also draw\nthe actual box plot picture that the assignment asks for.\n\n\n--- Outlier detection for 'Age' ---\nQ1 (25th percentile): 22.00\nQ3 (75th percentile): 35.00\nIQR (Q3 - Q1)       : 13.00\nLower bound (Q1 - 1.5*IQR): 2.50\nUpper bound (Q3 + 1.5*IQR): 54.50\nNumber of outlier rows found in 'Age': 66 out of 891 total rows (7.4%)\nA few of the actual outlier rows found for 'Age':\n     Survived  Pclass     Sex    Age     Fare\n7           0       3    male   2.00  21.0750\n11          1       1  female  58.00  26.5500\n15          1       2  female  55.00  16.0000\n16          0       3    male   2.00  29.1250\n33          0       2    male  66.00  10.5000\n54          0       1    male  65.00  61.9792\n78          1       2    male   0.83  29.0000\n94          0       3    male  59.00   7.2500\n96          0       1    male  71.00  34.6542\n116         0       3    male  70.50   7.7500\n\n--- Outlier detection for 'Fare' ---\nQ1 (25th percentile): 7.91\nQ3 (75th percentile): 31.00\nIQR (Q3 - Q1)       : 23.09\nLower bound (Q1 - 1.5*IQR): -26.72\nUpper bound (Q3 + 1.5*IQR): 65.63\nNumber of outlier rows found in 'Fare': 116 out of 891 total rows (13.0%)\n\nA few of the actual outlier rows found for 'Fare':\n     Survived  Pclass     Sex   Age      Fare\n1           1       1  female  38.0   71.2833\n27          0       1    male  19.0  263.0000\n31          1       1  female  28.0  146.5208\n34          0       1    male  28.0   82.1708\n52          1       1  female  49.0   76.7292\n61          1       1  female  38.0   80.0000\n62          0       1    male  45.0   83.4750\n72          0       2    male  21.0   73.5000\n88          1       1  female  23.0  263.0000\n102         0       1    male  21.0   77.2875\n\nINTERPRETATION:\n  - 'Age' outliers are mostly on the HIGH end (elderly passengers in their\n    60s-80s) - genuinely unusual ages compared to the bulk of passengers,\n    but still perfectly realistic human ages, not data-entry mistakes.\n  - 'Fare' outliers are almost entirely very HIGH fares (some passengers\n    paid dramatically more than most - these were likely 1st class\n    passengers with large private suites/family bookings). Fare cannot go\n    below 0 in a valid record, so there are no \"too low\" Fare outliers\n    here.\n  In both cases, these look like genuine, valid extreme values rather\n  than data errors, so the appropriate handling here is normally to KEEP\n  them but be aware of them (for example, remembering they exist is\n  exactly why Question 4 below considers scaling techniques that reduce\n  the influence of such extreme values).\n\n\nDrawing box plots for Age and Fare, side by side ...\n  Saved plot -> plots/2a_boxplots_age_and_fare.png\nDrawing a scatter plot highlighting the specific outlier passengers ...\n  Saved plot -> plots/2b_outliers_highlighted_scatter.png", "plots": [{"filename": "2a_boxplots_age_and_fare.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}, {"filename": "2b_outliers_highlighted_scatter.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}, {"id": "question-3", "kind": "question", "number": 3, "label": "Perform appropriate validation checks after cleaning.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 3: VALIDATION CHECKS AFTER CLEANING\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nThe whole point of this question is to PROVE, with actual checks (not just\nassume), that the cleaning we did in Lab Assignment 1 actually worked and\nthat the dataset is now in a trustworthy state. We go through each check\nthe assignment lists, one at a time.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Check 1: Do any missing values remain?@@", "technical": "@@Check 1: Do any missing values remain?@@", "hinglish": "@@Check 1: Kya koi missing values abhi bhi bache hai?@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"--- Check 1: Missing values ---\")\nmissing_after = df.isnull().sum()\ntotal_missing = missing_after.sum()\nprint(missing_after)\nif total_missing == 0:\n    print(\"PASSED: There are zero missing values left in any column.\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"FAILED: There are still {total_missing} missing values remaining somewhere.\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Check 2: Do any duplicate records remain?@@", "technical": "@@Check 2: Do any duplicate records remain?@@", "hinglish": "@@Check 2: Kya koi duplicate records abhi bhi bache hai?@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\n--- Check 2: Duplicate records ---\")\nduplicate_count = df.duplicated().sum()\nprint(f\"Number of fully duplicated rows: {duplicate_count}\")\nif duplicate_count == 0:\n    print(\"PASSED: No duplicate rows remain.\")\nelse:\n    print(f\"FAILED: {duplicate_count} duplicate row(s) still remain.\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Check 3: Are the numerical values valid (sensible, not impossible)?@@", "technical": "@@Check 3: Are the numeric values valid (logically possible)?@@", "hinglish": "@@Check 3: Kya numeric values valid hai (logically possible)?@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\n--- Check 3: Numerical values are valid ---\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "\"Valid\" for a count or a price means: it cannot logically be negative. (df[\"Fare\"] < 0) creates a True/False column: True wherever Fare is negative. .sum() then counts how many True's there are (i.e. how many rows would be invalid).", "technical": "A count or a monetary amount is \"valid\" only if non-negative. (df[\"Fare\"] < 0) produces a boolean Series, True wherever Fare is negative; .sum() aggregates this into a count of invalid rows.", "hinglish": "Ek count ya monetary amount tabhi \"valid\" hai jab wo non-negative ho. (df[\"Fare\"] < 0) ek boolean Series banata hai, jo True hota hai jahan bhi Fare negative ho; .sum() ise aggregate karke invalid rows ka count de deta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "invalid_fare = (df[\"Fare\"] < 0).sum()\ninvalid_sibsp = (df[\"SibSp\"] < 0).sum()\ninvalid_parch = (df[\"Parch\"] < 0).sum()\ninvalid_age = (df[\"Age\"] < 0).sum()\nprint(f\"Negative Fare values (impossible - can't pay negative money): {invalid_fare}\")\nprint(f\"Negative SibSp values (impossible - can't have negative siblings/spouses): {invalid_sibsp}\")\nprint(f\"Negative Parch values (impossible - can't have negative parents/children): {invalid_parch}\")\nprint(f\"Negative Age values (impossible): {invalid_age}\")\nif invalid_fare == invalid_sibsp == invalid_parch == invalid_age == 0:\n    print(\"PASSED: All numeric columns contain only logically valid (non-negative) values.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"FAILED: At least one numeric column contains impossible negative values.\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Check 4: Do categorical variables only contain valid categories?@@", "technical": "@@Check 4: Do categorical variables contain only valid categories?@@", "hinglish": "@@Check 4: Kya categorical variables mein sirf valid categories hai?@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\n--- Check 4: Categorical variables contain only valid categories ---\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "For each categorical column, we define the FULL set of values that are actually allowed to appear (based on what we know about the Titanic and how this dataset was documented), then check whether anything else (an unexpected, invalid category) has sneaked in.", "technical": "For each categorical column, the complete set of legitimately allowed values is defined from domain knowledge (what the Titanic dataset's documentation specifies), and the observed values are checked for anything outside that set.", "hinglish": "Har categorical column ke liye, domain knowledge se (Titanic dataset ki documentation jo bataati hai us hisaab se) legitimately allowed values ka poora set define kiya jaata hai, aur observed values ko check kiya jaata hai ki us set se bahar to kuch nahi hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "valid_sex_values = {\"male\", \"female\"}\nvalid_embarked_values = {\"C\", \"Q\", \"S\"}\nvalid_pclass_values = {1, 2, 3}\nvalid_survived_values = {0, 1}"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "set(df[\"Sex\"].unique()) collects all of the DIFFERENT values that actually appear in the Sex column (as a \"set\", which is just a collection with no repeated items and no particular order), so we can compare it against the allowed set above.", "technical": "set(df[\"Sex\"].unique()) collects the distinct values actually present in the Sex column into a set (an unordered collection with no duplicates), enabling a direct comparison against the allowed set.", "hinglish": "set(df[\"Sex\"].unique()) Sex column mein actually present distinct values ko ek set (ek unordered collection jisme duplicates nahi hote) mein collect karta hai, jisse allowed set ke against direct comparison ki ja sake."}, {"type": "code", "text": "actual_sex_values = set(df[\"Sex\"].unique())\nactual_embarked_values = set(df[\"Embarked\"].unique())\nactual_pclass_values = set(df[\"Pclass\"].unique())\nactual_survived_values = set(df[\"Survived\"].unique())"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "The \"-\" operator between two sets means \"everything in the first set that is NOT also in the second set\" - i.e. \"which actual values are NOT in our allowed list?\". If this comes out empty, everything is valid.", "technical": "The \"-\" (difference) operator between two sets returns every element of the first set absent from the second — i.e., which observed values are NOT in the allowed list. An empty result confirms validity.", "hinglish": "Do sets ke beech \"-\" (difference) operator pehle set ka har wo element return karta hai jo doosre set mein absent hai — matlab, kaunsi observed values allowed list mein NAHI hai. Empty result validity confirm karta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "unexpected_sex = actual_sex_values - valid_sex_values\nunexpected_embarked = actual_embarked_values - valid_embarked_values\nunexpected_pclass = actual_pclass_values - valid_pclass_values\nunexpected_survived = actual_survived_values - valid_survived_values\n\nprint(f\"Sex      -> values found: {actual_sex_values}      | unexpected values: {unexpected_sex or 'none'}\")\nprint(f\"Embarked -> values found: {actual_embarked_values}   | unexpected values: {unexpected_embarked or 'none'}\")\nprint(f\"Pclass   -> values found: {actual_pclass_values}     | unexpected values: {unexpected_pclass or 'none'}\")\nprint(f\"Survived -> values found: {actual_survived_values}       | unexpected values: {unexpected_survived or 'none'}\")\n\nif not (unexpected_sex or unexpected_embarked or unexpected_pclass or unexpected_survived):\n    print(\"PASSED: Every categorical column only contains its expected, valid categories.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"FAILED: At least one categorical column contains an unexpected value.\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Check 5: Are dates correctly formatted?@@", "technical": "@@Check 5: Are date fields correctly formatted?@@", "hinglish": "@@Check 5: Kya date fields sahi format mein hai?@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\\n--- Check 5: Dates are correctly formatted ---\")\nprint(\"\"\"\nThis particular Titanic dataset does NOT contain any date/time column at\nall (no \"date of travel\", \"date of birth\", etc. is recorded) - so this\nspecific check does not literally apply to this dataset. We flag this\nhonestly rather than skip the question, and show below the GENERAL\ntechnique you WOULD use if a date column existed, so the method itself is\nstill demonstrated.\n\nIf a column called, say, \"Booking_Date\" existed, you would do:\n\n    df[\"Booking_Date_parsed\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"Booking_Date\"], errors=\"coerce\")\n    invalid_dates = df[\"Booking_Date_parsed\"].isnull().sum()\n\npd.to_datetime(...) tries to convert every value in that column into a\nproper Python \"datetime\" value. Any text that does NOT look like a valid\ndate (e.g. \"31/13/2020\", or \"hello\", or a typo) fails to convert.\n\"errors='coerce'\" tells pandas: \"if a value can't be converted, don't\ncrash the whole program - just put a missing value (NaN) there instead\".\nCounting how many NaNs appear AFTER this conversion (that weren't already\nmissing BEFORE it) tells you exactly how many rows had a badly-formatted\ndate.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Check 6: Do values lie within reasonable, domain-specific ranges?@@", "technical": "@@Check 6: Do values fall within reasonable, domain-specific ranges?@@", "hinglish": "@@Check 6: Kya values reasonable, domain-specific ranges ke andar hai?@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"--- Check 6: Domain-specific reasonable ranges ---\")\nprint(\"\"\"\n\"Domain-specific\" means using REAL-WORLD KNOWLEDGE about what these\nnumbers are allowed to realistically be - not just \"is it negative or\nnot\", but \"does this number make sense for a real human being on a real\nship in 1912?\".\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Age: no verified human on record has ever exceeded ~122 years, and this was a passenger ship in 1912, so anything above roughly 100 would be extremely suspicious, and Age obviously cannot be negative either.", "technical": "No verified human lifespan on record exceeds ~122 years, and this dataset describes a 1912 passenger voyage, so any Age exceeding roughly 100 would be highly implausible; Age is also, trivially, non-negative by domain definition.", "hinglish": "Koi bhi verified human lifespan record par ~122 saal se zyada nahi hai, aur ye dataset 1912 ke ek passenger voyage ka hai, isliye roughly 100 se zyada koi bhi Age highly implausible hogi; Age domain-definition ke hisaab se trivially non-negative bhi honi chahiye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "unreasonable_age = df[(df[\"Age\"] < 0) | (df[\"Age\"] > 100)]\nprint(f\"Passengers with an unreasonable Age (below 0 or above 100): {len(unreasonable_age)}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Fare: cannot be negative. As an extra sanity check, the highest confirmed real Titanic ticket fare (a first-class parlour suite) was around 512 - so anything drastically higher than that would be suspicious. We use 600 here as a generous safety margin above the known historical maximum.", "technical": "Fare cannot be negative by definition. As an additional sanity bound, the highest documented genuine Titanic fare (a first-class parlour suite) was approximately 512; a generous margin of 600 above the known historical maximum is used as the upper threshold here.", "hinglish": "Fare definition ke hisaab se negative nahi ho sakta. Ek additional sanity bound ke roop mein, sabse zyada documented genuine Titanic fare (ek first-class parlour suite) approximately 512 tha; yahan known historical maximum se upar ek generous margin ke roop mein 600 ko upper threshold banaya gaya hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "unreasonable_fare = df[(df[\"Fare\"] < 0) | (df[\"Fare\"] > 600)]\nprint(f\"Passengers with an unreasonable Fare (below 0 or above 600): {len(unreasonable_fare)}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Pclass should only ever be 1, 2, or 3 - there is no \"4th class\".", "technical": "Pclass is constrained by domain definition to {1, 2, 3} — no fourth class exists in this schema.", "hinglish": "Pclass domain-definition ke hisaab se sirf {1, 2, 3} tak hi constrained hai — is schema mein koi 4th class exist hi nahi karti."}, {"type": "code", "text": "unreasonable_pclass = df[~df[\"Pclass\"].isin([1, 2, 3])]\nprint(f\"Passengers with an unreasonable Pclass (not 1, 2, or 3): {len(unreasonable_pclass)}\")\n\nif len(unreasonable_age) == 0 and len(unreasonable_fare) == 0 and len(unreasonable_pclass) == 0:\n    print(\"PASSED: All checked columns fall within reasonable, real-world ranges.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"FAILED: At least one value falls outside a reasonable real-world range.\")"}], "output": "The whole point of this question is to PROVE, with actual checks (not just\nassume), that the cleaning we did in Lab Assignment 1 actually worked and\nthat the dataset is now in a trustworthy state. We go through each check\nthe assignment lists, one at a time.\n\n--- Check 1: Missing values ---\nSurvived    0\nPclass      0\nSex         0\nAge         0\nSibSp       0\nParch       0\nFare        0\nEmbarked    0\ndtype: int64\nPASSED: There are zero missing values left in any column.\n\n--- Check 2: Duplicate records ---\nNumber of fully duplicated rows: 116\nFAILED: 116 duplicate row(s) still remain.\n\n--- Check 3: Numerical values are valid ---\nNegative Fare values (impossible - can't pay negative money): 0\nNegative SibSp values (impossible - can't have negative siblings/spouses): 0\nNegative Parch values (impossible - can't have negative parents/children): 0\nNegative Age values (impossible): 0\nPASSED: All numeric columns contain only logically valid (non-negative) values.\n\n--- Check 4: Categorical variables contain only valid categories ---\nSex      -> values found: {'female', 'male'}      | unexpected values: none\nEmbarked -> values found: {'Q', 'C', 'S'}   | unexpected values: none\nPclass   -> values found: {np.int64(1), np.int64(2), np.int64(3)}     | unexpected values: none\nSurvived -> values found: {np.int64(0), np.int64(1)}       | unexpected values: none\nPASSED: Every categorical column only contains its expected, valid categories.\n\n--- Check 5: Dates are correctly formatted ---\n\nThis particular Titanic dataset does NOT contain any date/time column at\nall (no \"date of travel\", \"date of birth\", etc. is recorded) - so this\nspecific check does not literally apply to this dataset. We flag this\nhonestly rather than skip the question, and show below the GENERAL\ntechnique you WOULD use if a date column existed, so the method itself is\nstill demonstrated.\n\nIf a column called, say, \"Booking_Date\" existed, you would do:\n\n    df[\"Booking_Date_parsed\"] = pd.to_datetime(df[\"Booking_Date\"], errors=\"coerce\")\n    invalid_dates = df[\"Booking_Date_parsed\"].isnull().sum()\n\npd.to_datetime(...) tries to convert every value in that column into a\nproper Python \"datetime\" value. Any text that does NOT look like a valid\ndate (e.g. \"31/13/2020\", or \"hello\", or a typo) fails to convert.\n\"errors='coerce'\" tells pandas: \"if a value can't be converted, don't\ncrash the whole program - just put a missing value (NaN) there instead\".\nCounting how many NaNs appear AFTER this conversion (that weren't already\nmissing BEFORE it) tells you exactly how many rows had a badly-formatted\ndate.\n\n--- Check 6: Domain-specific reasonable ranges ---\n\n\"Domain-specific\" means using REAL-WORLD KNOWLEDGE about what these\nnumbers are allowed to realistically be - not just \"is it negative or\nnot\", but \"does this number make sense for a real human being on a real\nship in 1912?\".\n\nPassengers with an unreasonable Age (below 0 or above 100): 0\nPassengers with an unreasonable Fare (below 0 or above 600): 0\nPassengers with an unreasonable Pclass (not 1, 2, or 3): 0\nPASSED: All checked columns fall within reasonable, real-world ranges.", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-4", "kind": "question", "number": 4, "label": "Feature Scaling - apply Min-Max Normalization AND Standardization (both, back to back, on the same chosen columns).", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 4: FEATURE SCALING (Min-Max Normalization AND Standardization)\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHAT IS \"FEATURE SCALING\" AND WHY DO IT AT ALL?\nDifferent numeric columns in this dataset live on very different scales:\nAge ranges roughly 0-80, while Fare ranges roughly 0-512 (and SibSp/Parch\nare small counts, usually 0-8). Many machine learning algorithms (like\nk-nearest-neighbors, or anything using distances/gradients) can end up\nbeing unfairly dominated by whichever column happens to have the LARGEST\nraw numbers - not because that column is actually more important, but\npurely because of the units it happens to be measured in. Feature scaling\nputs every chosen numeric column onto a comparable scale, so no column\nunfairly dominates just because of its raw size.\n\nWHICH COLUMNS ARE \"APPROPRIATE\" TO SCALE HERE?\nWe select: Age, Fare, SibSp, Parch.\n  - These are all genuinely continuous/count-based NUMERIC measurements.\nWe deliberately do NOT scale:\n  - \"Survived\" -> this is the outcome/label (0 or 1) we would eventually\n    want to predict, not an input measurement - scaling a label doesn't\n    make sense and would only confuse its meaning (0 = no, 1 = yes).\n  - \"Pclass\" -> although stored as a number (1, 2, 3), it is really a\n    CATEGORY (which class of ticket), not a measured quantity where\n    \"2\" is meaningfully \"twice as much\" as \"1\" - scaling it numerically\n    would misleadingly treat it like a true measurement.\n  - \"Sex\", \"Embarked\" -> these are text categories, not numbers at all,\n    so numeric scaling does not apply to them (they would first need a\n    different technique entirely, called \"encoding\", which is outside\n    this assignment's scope).\n\"\"\")\n\ncolumns_to_scale = [\"Age\", \"Fare\", \"SibSp\", \"Parch\"]\nprint(f\"Columns selected for scaling: {columns_to_scale}\")\n\nprint(\"\\nOriginal (unscaled) statistics for these columns:\")\nprint(df[columns_to_scale].describe().round(2))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4a) MIN-MAX NORMALIZATION@@", "technical": "@@(a) Min-Max normalization@@", "hinglish": "@@(a) Min-Max normalization@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- METHOD 1: MIN-MAX NORMALIZATION ---\nFormula (applied to every value \"x\" in a column):\n    x_scaled = (x - min(column)) / (max(column) - min(column))\nThis SQUEEZES every value in the column into the range [0, 1] - the\nsmallest original value becomes exactly 0, the largest original value\nbecomes exactly 1, and everything else lands proportionally in between.\nIt's useful when you want every feature bounded within a fixed, known\nrange, but it is quite sensitive to outliers (a single extreme max/min\nvalue stretches or squashes everything else).\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "MinMaxScaler() creates a \"scaler\" object - think of it as a small machine that first needs to LEARN the min and max of your data (via .fit(...)), and can then actually TRANSFORM values using what it learned (via .transform(...)). \".fit_transform(...)\" conveniently does both steps in a single call.", "technical": "MinMaxScaler() instantiates a stateful transformer object: .fit(...) learns the column's min/max from the training data, and .transform(...) applies that learned scaling. .fit_transform(...) is a convenience method performing both steps in one call.", "hinglish": "MinMaxScaler() ek stateful transformer object banata hai: .fit(...) training data se column ka min/max seekhta hai, aur .transform(...) us seekhe hue scaling ko apply karta hai. .fit_transform(...) ek convenience method hai jo dono steps ek hi call mein kar deta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "minmax_scaler = MinMaxScaler()"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "df[columns_to_scale] pulls out just those 4 columns as their own small table. fit_transform(...) on that returns a plain NumPy array of the scaled numbers (not a pandas table), so we immediately wrap it back into a nicely-labelled DataFrame using pd.DataFrame(..., columns=...).", "technical": "df[columns_to_scale] projects the selected 4 columns as their own sub-DataFrame. fit_transform(...) on this returns a plain NumPy array of scaled values (not a pandas object), which is immediately wrapped back into a labeled DataFrame via pd.DataFrame(..., columns=...).", "hinglish": "df[columns_to_scale] selected 4 columns ko unke apne sub-DataFrame ke roop mein project karta hai. Is par fit_transform(...) call karne se scaled values ka ek plain NumPy array return hota hai (pandas object nahi), jise turant pd.DataFrame(..., columns=...) se wapas ek labeled DataFrame mein wrap kar diya jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "minmax_scaled_values = minmax_scaler.fit_transform(df[columns_to_scale])\ndf_minmax = df.copy()\ndf_minmax[columns_to_scale] = minmax_scaled_values\n\nprint(\"Min-Max normalized statistics (every column's min should now be 0.0, max should be 1.0):\")\nprint(df_minmax[columns_to_scale].describe().round(3))\n\nprint(\"\\nFirst 5 rows after Min-Max normalization (compare to the original table above):\")\nprint(df_minmax[columns_to_scale].head())"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4b) STANDARDIZATION@@", "technical": "@@(b) Standardization@@", "hinglish": "@@(b) Standardization@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- METHOD 2: STANDARDIZATION (Z-score scaling) ---\nFormula (applied to every value \"x\" in a column):\n    x_scaled = (x - mean(column)) / standard_deviation(column)\nThis RE-CENTRES every column so its average becomes exactly 0, and\nRE-SCALES it so its standard deviation (a measure of how spread out the\nvalues are) becomes exactly 1. Unlike Min-Max, standardized values are\nNOT bounded to a fixed [0, 1] range - they can still go quite negative or\nquite positive, but a value's sign and size now directly tell you \"how\nmany standard deviations above or below the average is this value?\",\nwhich is generally LESS sensitive to a handful of extreme outliers than\nMin-Max normalization is.\n\"\"\")\n\nstandard_scaler = StandardScaler()\nstandardized_values = standard_scaler.fit_transform(df[columns_to_scale])\ndf_standardized = df.copy()\ndf_standardized[columns_to_scale] = standardized_values\n\nprint(\"Standardized statistics (every column's mean should now be ~0.0, std should be ~1.0):\")\nprint(df_standardized[columns_to_scale].describe().round(3))\n\nprint(\"\\nFirst 5 rows after Standardization (compare to the original table above):\")\nprint(df_standardized[columns_to_scale].head())\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nCOMPARING THE TWO METHODS SIDE BY SIDE (using 'Fare' as the example,\nsince it has the most extreme outliers of our 4 chosen columns):\n  - The single highest Fare value in the ORIGINAL data becomes exactly\n    1.0 under Min-Max Normalization (by definition, since Min-Max always\n    maps the maximum value to 1.0) - and because that one Fare value is\n    such an extreme outlier, it drags the max upward, so most OTHER\n    passengers' Min-Max-scaled fares end up bunched very close to 0.\n  - Under Standardization, that same extreme Fare value simply becomes a\n    large POSITIVE number (many standard deviations above the mean, with\n    no fixed upper limit of 1.0 it has to squeeze everything else under),\n    so the rest of the passengers' standardized fares are spread out more\n    naturally around 0, without being squashed together by that one\n    outlier.\n  This is exactly why, in practice, Standardization is generally the\n  safer default choice on data with outliers (like Fare here), while\n  Min-Max Normalization is more often preferred when you specifically\n  need every value guaranteed to sit within a fixed [0, 1] range (for\n  example, some neural network input layers expect this).\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "WHAT IS \"FEATURE SCALING\" AND WHY DO IT AT ALL?\nDifferent numeric columns in this dataset live on very different scales:\nAge ranges roughly 0-80, while Fare ranges roughly 0-512 (and SibSp/Parch\nare small counts, usually 0-8). Many machine learning algorithms (like\nk-nearest-neighbors, or anything using distances/gradients) can end up\nbeing unfairly dominated by whichever column happens to have the LARGEST\nraw numbers - not because that column is actually more important, but\npurely because of the units it happens to be measured in. Feature scaling\nputs every chosen numeric column onto a comparable scale, so no column\nunfairly dominates just because of its raw size.\n\nWHICH COLUMNS ARE \"APPROPRIATE\" TO SCALE HERE?\nWe select: Age, Fare, SibSp, Parch.\n  - These are all genuinely continuous/count-based NUMERIC measurements.\nWe deliberately do NOT scale:\n  - \"Survived\" -> this is the outcome/label (0 or 1) we would eventually\n    want to predict, not an input measurement - scaling a label doesn't\n    make sense and would only confuse its meaning (0 = no, 1 = yes).\n  - \"Pclass\" -> although stored as a number (1, 2, 3), it is really a\n    CATEGORY (which class of ticket), not a measured quantity where\n    \"2\" is meaningfully \"twice as much\" as \"1\" - scaling it numerically\n    would misleadingly treat it like a true measurement.\n  - \"Sex\", \"Embarked\" -> these are text categories, not numbers at all,\n    so numeric scaling does not apply to them (they would first need a\n    different technique entirely, called \"encoding\", which is outside\n    this assignment's scope).\n\nColumns selected for scaling: ['Age', 'Fare', 'SibSp', 'Parch']\n\nOriginal (unscaled) statistics for these columns:\n          Age    Fare   SibSp   Parch\ncount  891.00  891.00  891.00  891.00\nmean    29.36   32.20    0.52    0.38\nstd     13.02   49.69    1.10    0.81\nmin      0.42    0.00    0.00    0.00\n25%     22.00    7.91    0.00    0.00\n50%     28.00   14.45    0.00    0.00\n75%     35.00   31.00    1.00    0.00\nmax     80.00  512.33    8.00    6.00\n\n--- METHOD 1: MIN-MAX NORMALIZATION ---\nFormula (applied to every value \"x\" in a column):\n    x_scaled = (x - min(column)) / (max(column) - min(column))\nThis SQUEEZES every value in the column into the range [0, 1] - the\nsmallest original value becomes exactly 0, the largest original value\nbecomes exactly 1, and everything else lands proportionally in between.\nIt's useful when you want every feature bounded within a fixed, known\nrange, but it is quite sensitive to outliers (a single extreme max/min\nvalue stretches or squashes everything else).\n\nMin-Max normalized statistics (every column's min should now be 0.0, max should be 1.0):\n           Age     Fare    SibSp    Parch\ncount  891.000  891.000  891.000  891.000\nmean     0.364    0.063    0.065    0.064\nstd      0.164    0.097    0.138    0.134\nmin      0.000    0.000    0.000    0.000\n25%      0.271    0.015    0.000    0.000\n50%      0.347    0.028    0.000    0.000\n75%      0.435    0.061    0.125    0.000\nmax      1.000    1.000    1.000    1.000\n\nFirst 5 rows after Min-Max normalization (compare to the original table above):\n        Age      Fare  SibSp  Parch\n0  0.271174  0.014151  0.125    0.0\n1  0.472229  0.139136  0.125    0.0\n2  0.321438  0.015469  0.000    0.0\n3  0.434531  0.103644  0.125    0.0\n4  0.434531  0.015713  0.000    0.0\n\n--- METHOD 2: STANDARDIZATION (Z-score scaling) ---\nFormula (applied to every value \"x\" in a column):\n    x_scaled = (x - mean(column)) / standard_deviation(column)\nThis RE-CENTRES every column so its average becomes exactly 0, and\nRE-SCALES it so its standard deviation (a measure of how spread out the\nvalues are) becomes exactly 1. Unlike Min-Max, standardized values are\nNOT bounded to a fixed [0, 1] range - they can still go quite negative or\nquite positive, but a value's sign and size now directly tell you \"how\nmany standard deviations above or below the average is this value?\",\nwhich is generally LESS sensitive to a handful of extreme outliers than\nMin-Max normalization is.\n\nStandardized statistics (every column's mean should now be ~0.0, std should be ~1.0):\n           Age     Fare    SibSp    Parch\ncount  891.000  891.000  891.000  891.000\nmean     0.000    0.000    0.000    0.000\nstd      1.001    1.001    1.001    1.001\nmin     -2.224   -0.648   -0.475   -0.474\n25%     -0.566   -0.489   -0.475   -0.474\n50%     -0.105   -0.357   -0.475   -0.474\n75%      0.433   -0.024    0.433   -0.474\nmax      3.892    9.667    6.784    6.974\n\nFirst 5 rows after Standardization (compare to the original table above):\n        Age      Fare     SibSp     Parch\n0 -0.565736 -0.502445  0.432793 -0.473674\n1  0.663861  0.786845  0.432793 -0.473674\n2 -0.258337 -0.488854 -0.474545 -0.473674\n3  0.433312  0.420730  0.432793 -0.473674\n4  0.433312 -0.486337 -0.474545 -0.473674\n\nCOMPARING THE TWO METHODS SIDE BY SIDE (using 'Fare' as the example,\nsince it has the most extreme outliers of our 4 chosen columns):\n  - The single highest Fare value in the ORIGINAL data becomes exactly\n    1.0 under Min-Max Normalization (by definition, since Min-Max always\n    maps the maximum value to 1.0) - and because that one Fare value is\n    such an extreme outlier, it drags the max upward, so most OTHER\n    passengers' Min-Max-scaled fares end up bunched very close to 0.\n  - Under Standardization, that same extreme Fare value simply becomes a\n    large POSITIVE number (many standard deviations above the mean, with\n    no fixed upper limit of 1.0 it has to squeeze everything else under),\n    so the rest of the passengers' standardized fares are spread out more\n    naturally around 0, without being squashed together by that one\n    outlier.\n  This is exactly why, in practice, Standardization is generally the\n  safer default choice on data with outliers (like Fare here), while\n  Min-Max Normalization is more often preferred when you specifically\n  need every value guaranteed to sit within a fixed [0, 1] range (for\n  example, some neural network input layers expect this).", "plots": []}, {"id": "wrapup", "kind": "wrapup", "number": null, "label": "Saving Your Results", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"SAVING BOTH SCALED DATASETS\")\n\nminmax_output_path = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"Titanic-Dataset-MinMaxScaled.csv\")\nstandardized_output_path = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"Titanic-Dataset-Standardized.csv\")\n\ndf_minmax.to_csv(minmax_output_path, index=False)\ndf_standardized.to_csv(standardized_output_path, index=False)\n\nprint(f\"Saved Min-Max normalized dataset -> {minmax_output_path}\")\nprint(f\"Saved standardized dataset       -> {standardized_output_path}\")\n\nprint(f\"\\nAll plots were saved inside: {plots_folder}\")\nprint(\"\\nAll 4 questions of Lab Assignment 2 are complete.\")"}], "output": "Saved Min-Max normalized dataset -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 2/Titanic-Dataset-MinMaxScaled.csv\nSaved standardized dataset       -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 2/Titanic-Dataset-Standardized.csv\n\nAll plots were saved inside: /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 2/plots\n\nAll 4 questions of Lab Assignment 2 are complete.", "plots": []}]}, {"num": 3, "title": "Lab Assignment 3", "subtitle": "Data Smoothing, Z-score Outlier Removal, and Normalization", "dataset": "Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv", "intro": "WHAT IS THIS FILE, AND WHO IS IT FOR?\n----------------------------------------\nSame style as Lab Assignments 1 and 2: a plain Python script written for a\ncomplete beginner who has never touched Python, pandas, matplotlib, or\nscikit-learn before. Every step is explained in plain English before the\ncode that does it, plus short \"#\" comments on the important lines.\n\nWHERE DOES THE DATA COME FROM, AND HOW DO THE 3 QUESTIONS CONNECT?\n------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis script continues the same pipeline as the earlier assignments:\n\n    Lab Assignment 1 -> cleaned the raw Titanic data (missing values,\n                         duplicates, useless columns)\n    Lab Assignment 2 -> visualized it, found outliers with a box plot\n                         (but did not remove them), validated it, scaled it\n    Lab Assignment 3 (THIS FILE) ->\n        Q1: demonstrates 3 different SMOOTHING techniques on a numeric\n            column (this is a separate, standalone demonstration - it does\n            NOT permanently change the main working data, since smoothing\n            is being taught here as its own technique, not as a cleaning\n            step we need for later questions)\n        Q2: detects outliers using the Z-SCORE method (a different method\n            than Lab Assignment 2's box-plot/IQR method) and this time\n            ACTUALLY REMOVES them, producing a smaller, outlier-free\n            dataset\n        Q3: takes that outlier-free dataset from Q2 and applies SEVEN\n            different normalization/scaling techniques to it, one by one\n\nSo Q1 is a standalone demo, while Q2's output directly feeds into Q3.\n\nWe load the already-cleaned CSV that Lab Assignment 1 produced:\n    ../Lab Assignment 1/Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\nMake sure you have run Lab Assignment 1's script at least once already, so\nthat file exists.\n\nHOW TO RUN THIS FILE\n----------------------\n1. Make sure Python 3 is installed.\n2. Make sure these libraries are installed (run once, if needed):\n        pip install pandas numpy matplotlib scikit-learn\n3. Run this script from inside THIS folder (\"Lab Assignment 3\"):\n        python3 lab_assignment_3.py\n   It prints all explanations and tables to the terminal, saves every\n   chart as a \".png\" image inside a new \"plots\" folder next to this\n   script, and saves the outlier-free + 7-times-scaled datasets as CSV\n   files at the end.\n\n==================================================================================", "sections": [{"id": "setup", "kind": "setup", "number": null, "label": "Setup: Loading the Dataset", "chunks": [{"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE LIBRARIES WE NEED@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE REQUIRED LIBRARIES@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0: ZAROORI LIBRARIES IMPORT KARNA@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "import os                      # for building reliable file paths\nimport math                    # for a couple of small math functions (log10, ceil) used in Decimal Scaling\nimport numpy as np             # numpy = \"Numerical Python\" - a toolbox for fast maths on lists of numbers (arrays)\nimport pandas as pd            # our table/DataFrame toolbox, as in every earlier assignment\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt  # our charting toolbox, as in Lab Assignment 2"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "From scikit-learn, we import the ready-made scalers we'll use in Q3. (A couple of the 7 normalization techniques don't have a ready-made scikit-learn tool, so for those we write the formula ourselves by hand - each one is explained fully, in plain English, right before we use it.)", "technical": "The ready-made scalers required for Question 3 are imported from scikit-learn. Two of the seven normalization techniques have no ready-made scikit-learn transformer, so those two are implemented by hand from their formulas instead — each is explained in full immediately before its use.", "hinglish": "Question 3 ke liye zaroori ready-made scalers scikit-learn se import kiye gaye hai. Saat normalization techniques mein se do ke liye koi ready-made scikit-learn transformer nahi hai, isliye unhe unke formulas se hand-se implement kiya gaya hai — har ek ko uske use hone se pehle hi poori tarah explain kiya gaya hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler, StandardScaler, MaxAbsScaler, RobustScaler"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A very large \"display.width\" makes a wide table always print as ONE line per row, instead of pandas splitting it into two confusing stacked blocks.", "technical": "A large display.width forces a wide table to always render as one line per row, rather than pandas splitting it into two confusing stacked blocks.", "hinglish": "Ek bada display.width wide table ko hamesha ek hi line-per-row mein render hone ke liye force karta hai, pandas ise do confusing stacked blocks mein todne ke bajaye."}, {"type": "code", "text": "pd.set_option(\"display.max_columns\", None)\npd.set_option(\"display.width\", 1000)\n\n\ndef section(title):\n    \"\"\"Prints a clear section heading. See Lab Assignment 1 for a full\n    explanation of what a Python function/def is.\"\"\"\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 90)\n    print(title)\n    print(\"=\" * 90)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0b: RELIABLE FILE PATHS + A FOLDER TO SAVE PLOTS INTO@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0b: RELIABLE FILE PATHS + A PLOTS DIRECTORY@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0b: RELIABLE FILE PATHS + EK PLOTS DIRECTORY@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "this_script_folder = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))\ncleaned_csv_path = os.path.join(\n    this_script_folder, \"..\", \"Lab Assignment 1\", \"Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\"\n)\nplots_folder = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"plots\")\nos.makedirs(plots_folder, exist_ok=True)\n\n\ndef save_current_plot(filename):\n    \"\"\"Saves whatever chart is currently drawn to an image file inside our\n    'plots' folder, then clears the canvas so the next chart starts fresh.\n    (Full explanation of every line here is in Lab Assignment 2's script.)\"\"\"\n    full_path = os.path.join(plots_folder, filename)\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(full_path)\n    plt.close()\n    print(f\"  Saved plot -> plots/{filename}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0c: LOAD THE ALREADY-CLEANED DATASET@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0c: LOAD THE PRE-CLEANED DATASET@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0c: PRE-CLEANED DATASET LOAD KARNA@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "section(\"LOADING THE CLEANED DATASET FROM LAB ASSIGNMENT 1\")\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(cleaned_csv_path)\nprint(f\"Loaded cleaned dataset from: {cleaned_csv_path}\")\nprint(f\"Shape: {df.shape[0]} rows x {df.shape[1]} columns\")\nprint(\"Columns:\", list(df.columns))\nprint(df.head())"}], "output": "Loaded cleaned dataset from: /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/../Lab Assignment 1/Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\nShape: 891 rows x 8 columns\nColumns: ['Survived', 'Pclass', 'Sex', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'Fare', 'Embarked']\n   Survived  Pclass     Sex   Age  SibSp  Parch     Fare Embarked\n0         0       3    male  22.0      1      0   7.2500        S\n1         1       1  female  38.0      1      0  71.2833        C\n2         1       3  female  26.0      0      0   7.9250        S\n3         1       1  female  35.0      1      0  53.1000        S\n4         0       3    male  35.0      0      0   8.0500        S", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-1", "kind": "question", "number": 1, "label": "Apply different types of Data Smoothing, with graphs: a) Using bin mean b) Using bin median c) Using bin boundaries", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 1: DATA SMOOTHING BY BINNING (mean / median / boundaries)\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHAT IS \"DATA SMOOTHING\", AND WHY DO IT?\nReal-world numeric data is often \"noisy\" - it jitters up and down a bit\ndue to small random fluctuations, even though there's a genuine, smoother\nunderlying trend. \"Smoothing\" means processing the data so that this small\nrandom jitter is reduced/flattened out, while (hopefully) keeping the\nreal, larger pattern intact.\n\n\"BINNING\" IS ONE WAY TO SMOOTH DATA - HOW IT WORKS:\n  Step 1: SORT the column's values from smallest to largest.\n  Step 2: Split the sorted values into consecutive groups called \"bins\"\n          (here we use EQUAL-FREQUENCY bins, meaning every bin gets the\n          same NUMBER of values in it - e.g. with a bin size of 4, values\n          1-4 form bin 1, values 5-8 form bin 2, and so on).\n  Step 3: replace every value inside a bin with something calculated FROM\n          that bin, so all the values in the bin become smoother/closer to\n          each other. This assignment asks for 3 different ways of doing\n          this last step:\n            a) BIN MEAN     -> replace every value in the bin with the\n                               bin's average (mean).\n            b) BIN MEDIAN   -> replace every value in the bin with the\n                               bin's middle value (median) instead of the\n                               mean (median is less pulled around by any\n                               one extreme value inside that bin).\n            c) BIN BOUNDARIES -> for each value, look at just the two\n                               EDGE values of its bin (the smallest value\n                               in the bin, and the largest value in the\n                               bin), and replace it with WHICHEVER of\n                               those two edges it is numerically closer\n                               to. Unlike mean/median, this technique\n                               always produces one of the two original\n                               boundary numbers, never a \"new\" invented\n                               number.\n\nWHICH COLUMN, AND WHAT BIN SIZE?\nWe smooth the 'Fare' column (ticket price), because it is a real,\ncontinuous numeric measurement with plenty of natural variation - a good\ncandidate to demonstrate smoothing on. We use a bin size of 20 (so every\n20 sorted passengers form one bin) across the full column, which is small\nenough to visibly show a \"staircase\" smoothing effect on the graph without\ncreating too many tiny bins to see clearly.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef smooth_by_binning(sorted_values, bin_size):\n    \"\"\"\n    Takes a NumPy array of values that is ALREADY SORTED (smallest to\n    largest), and a chosen bin_size (how many values go in each bin), and\n    returns THREE new arrays of the same length and order:\n        bin_mean_smoothed      - every value replaced by its bin's mean\n        bin_median_smoothed    - every value replaced by its bin's median\n        bin_boundary_smoothed  - every value replaced by whichever bin\n                                  edge (min or max of that bin) is closer\n    This directly implements the 3-technique binning method taught in\n    class, just written out as reusable Python code.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(sorted_values)\n    bin_mean_smoothed = np.zeros(n)\n    bin_median_smoothed = np.zeros(n)\n    bin_boundary_smoothed = np.zeros(n)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "range(0, n, bin_size) counts 0, bin_size, 2*bin_size, 3*bin_size, ... up to n - i.e. it gives us the STARTING index of every bin.", "technical": "range(0, n, bin_size) generates 0, bin_size, 2×bin_size, 3×bin_size, ..., up to n — i.e., the starting index of every bin.", "hinglish": "range(0, n, bin_size) 0, bin_size, 2×bin_size, 3×bin_size, ..., n tak generate karta hai — matlab har bin ka starting index."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    for start in range(0, n, bin_size):"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "end is the starting index of the NEXT bin (or the very end of the array for the last, possibly-smaller, leftover bin). Python automatically stops slicing safely at the array's real end even if \"end\" would technically go past it.", "technical": "end marks the starting index of the next bin (or the array's true end for the final, potentially smaller, leftover bin). Python's slicing safely caps at the array's actual length even if end technically exceeds it.", "hinglish": "end agle bin ka starting index batata hai (ya last, potentially chhote, leftover bin ke liye array ka asli end). Python ki slicing safely array ki actual length tak hi limit rehti hai, chahe end technically usse zyada ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "        end = start + bin_size"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "This slice grabs just the values that belong to the current bin.", "technical": "This slice extracts exactly the values belonging to the current bin.", "hinglish": "Ye slice current bin ki exactly wahi values nikaalta hai jo usme belong karti hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "        current_bin = sorted_values[start:end]\n\n        bin_mean_value = current_bin.mean()\n        bin_median_value = np.median(current_bin)\n        bin_min = current_bin.min()\n        bin_max = current_bin.max()"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Fill in the mean/median results for every position in this bin.", "technical": "The computed mean/median value is broadcast into every position within this bin.", "hinglish": "Calculate kiya gaya mean/median value is bin ke andar har position mein broadcast (bhar diya) kar diya jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "        bin_mean_smoothed[start:end] = bin_mean_value\n        bin_median_smoothed[start:end] = bin_median_value"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "For bin-boundary smoothing, we must decide, for EACH value individually, whether it's closer to bin_min or bin_max.", "technical": "For bin-boundary smoothing, each value must be individually compared to determine whether it is nearer to bin_min or bin_max.", "hinglish": "Bin-boundary smoothing ke liye, har value ko individually compare karna padta hai ye decide karne ke liye ki wo bin_min ke zyada kareeb hai ya bin_max ke."}, {"type": "code", "text": "        for i, value in enumerate(current_bin):\n            distance_to_min = abs(value - bin_min)\n            distance_to_max = abs(value - bin_max)\n            if distance_to_min <= distance_to_max:\n                bin_boundary_smoothed[start + i] = bin_min\n            else:\n                bin_boundary_smoothed[start + i] = bin_max\n\n    return bin_mean_smoothed, bin_median_smoothed, bin_boundary_smoothed"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@A small, easy-to-read worked example first (like a hand-calculation)@@", "technical": "@@A small, hand-calculation-style worked example first@@", "hinglish": "@@Pehle ek chhota, hand-calculation-jaisa worked example@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"--- A small worked example first, on just 12 sample Fare values ---\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We deliberately pick a small, sorted sample of 12 real Fare values from the dataset here, purely so the printed numbers below are short enough to read and verify by eye - exactly like a textbook worked example.", "technical": "A small, sorted sample of 12 real Fare values is deliberately selected here, purely so the printed figures below stay short enough to verify by manual inspection — the same convention any textbook worked example follows.", "hinglish": "Yahan jaan-boojh kar 12 real Fare values ka ek chhota, sorted sample select kiya gaya hai, sirf isliye taaki neeche print hone wale numbers manual inspection se verify karne layak chhote rahein — bilkul jaise kisi textbook ka worked example follow karta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "sample_fares = np.sort(df[\"Fare\"].to_numpy())[::75][:12]  # pick 12 spread-out real values\nprint(f\"Sample of 12 sorted Fare values: {sample_fares}\")\n\nsample_mean_smoothed, sample_median_smoothed, sample_boundary_smoothed = smooth_by_binning(\n    sample_fares, bin_size=4\n)\nworked_example_table = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"Original\": sample_fares,\n    \"Bin Mean Smoothed\": sample_mean_smoothed.round(2),\n    \"Bin Median Smoothed\": sample_median_smoothed.round(2),\n    \"Bin Boundary Smoothed\": sample_boundary_smoothed.round(2),\n})\nprint(\"\\nWith 3 bins of 4 values each:\")\nprint(worked_example_table)\nprint(\"\"\"\nReading this small example: within each group of 4 sorted values (one\nbin), \"Bin Mean Smoothed\" replaces all 4 with their average, \"Bin Median\nSmoothed\" replaces all 4 with their middle value, and \"Bin Boundary\nSmoothed\" pulls each individual value toward whichever end of its own bin\n(smallest or largest) it was originally closer to.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@Now apply the same 3 techniques across the FULL Fare column, and plot it@@", "technical": "@@Now applying the same 3 techniques across the entire Fare column, and plotting the result@@", "hinglish": "@@Ab yehi 3 techniques poore Fare column par apply karke, result plot karna@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"--- Now applying the same 3 techniques across the full 'Fare' column ---\")\nsorted_fare = np.sort(df[\"Fare\"].to_numpy())\nmean_smoothed, median_smoothed, boundary_smoothed = smooth_by_binning(sorted_fare, bin_size=20)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We plot against a simple 0, 1, 2, 3, ... position index (the x-axis here is just \"the rank/position of a passenger once fares are sorted\", not any real-world quantity) - this is the standard way to visualize smoothing: you can directly see how each smoothing technique flattens the natural jaggedness of the sorted original line into neat \"steps\".", "technical": "The plot uses a plain 0, 1, 2, 3, ... position index on the x-axis (this is just \"a passenger's rank once fares are sorted\", not a real-world quantity) — the standard way to visualize smoothing, since it directly shows how each technique flattens the sorted line's natural jaggedness into discrete \"steps\".", "hinglish": "Plot x-axis par ek plain 0, 1, 2, 3, ... position index use karta hai (ye bas \"fares sort hone ke baad ek passenger ka rank\" hai, koi real-world quantity nahi) — smoothing visualize karne ka standard tarika, kyunki ye seedhe dikhata hai ki har technique sorted line ki natural jaggedness ko discrete \"steps\" mein kaise flatten karti hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "x_positions = np.arange(len(sorted_fare))\nplt.plot(x_positions, sorted_fare, label=\"Original (sorted)\", color=\"lightgray\", linewidth=1)\nplt.plot(x_positions, mean_smoothed, label=\"Bin Mean Smoothed\", color=\"steelblue\")\nplt.plot(x_positions, median_smoothed, label=\"Bin Median Smoothed\", color=\"seagreen\")\nplt.plot(x_positions, boundary_smoothed, label=\"Bin Boundary Smoothed\", color=\"darkorange\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Passenger rank (after sorting by Fare)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Fare\")\nplt.title(\"Data Smoothing by Binning: Mean vs Median vs Boundary (bin size = 20)\")\nplt.legend()\nsave_current_plot(\"1_smoothing_full_fare_comparison.png\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A zoomed-in version (first 100 sorted passengers) makes the \"staircase\" shape of each smoothing technique much easier to actually see, since the full-column plot above compresses 891 points into a small image.", "technical": "A zoomed-in view (the first 100 sorted passengers) makes each technique's \"staircase\" shape substantially easier to inspect, since the full-column plot compresses all 891 points into a small image, obscuring fine detail.", "hinglish": "Ek zoomed-in view (pehle 100 sorted passengers) har technique ki \"staircase\" shape ko dekhna kaafi aasan bana deti hai, kyunki full-column plot saare 891 points ko ek chhoti image mein compress kar deta hai, jisse fine detail chhup jaati hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\nzoom = 100\nplt.plot(x_positions[:zoom], sorted_fare[:zoom], label=\"Original (sorted)\",\n         color=\"lightgray\", linewidth=1, marker=\".\")\nplt.plot(x_positions[:zoom], mean_smoothed[:zoom], label=\"Bin Mean Smoothed\", color=\"steelblue\")\nplt.plot(x_positions[:zoom], median_smoothed[:zoom], label=\"Bin Median Smoothed\", color=\"seagreen\")\nplt.plot(x_positions[:zoom], boundary_smoothed[:zoom], label=\"Bin Boundary Smoothed\", color=\"darkorange\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Passenger rank (after sorting by Fare)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Fare\")\nplt.title(\"Data Smoothing by Binning - Zoomed In (first 100 sorted passengers)\")\nplt.legend()\nsave_current_plot(\"1_smoothing_zoomed_first_100.png\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nHow to read these charts: the light-gray \"Original\" line is jagged because\nreal Fare values jump around a lot even between neighbouring sorted\npassengers. All three smoothing lines turn that jaggedness into flat\n\"steps\" (one flat step per bin of 20 passengers) - Bin Mean and Bin Median\ndraw a SINGLE flat value across the whole step (median is slightly more\nresistant to a single unusually extreme value inside a bin), while Bin\nBoundary instead snaps every point to one of just two possible values (the\nbin's minimum or its maximum), which is why its line looks more like sharp\nup/down jumps between only two levels within a step, rather than one\nperfectly flat line like mean/median smoothing.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "WHAT IS \"DATA SMOOTHING\", AND WHY DO IT?\nReal-world numeric data is often \"noisy\" - it jitters up and down a bit\ndue to small random fluctuations, even though there's a genuine, smoother\nunderlying trend. \"Smoothing\" means processing the data so that this small\nrandom jitter is reduced/flattened out, while (hopefully) keeping the\nreal, larger pattern intact.\n\n\"BINNING\" IS ONE WAY TO SMOOTH DATA - HOW IT WORKS:\n  Step 1: SORT the column's values from smallest to largest.\n  Step 2: Split the sorted values into consecutive groups called \"bins\"\n          (here we use EQUAL-FREQUENCY bins, meaning every bin gets the\n          same NUMBER of values in it - e.g. with a bin size of 4, values\n          1-4 form bin 1, values 5-8 form bin 2, and so on).\n  Step 3: replace every value inside a bin with something calculated FROM\n          that bin, so all the values in the bin become smoother/closer to\n          each other. This assignment asks for 3 different ways of doing\n          this last step:\n            a) BIN MEAN     -> replace every value in the bin with the\n                               bin's average (mean).\n            b) BIN MEDIAN   -> replace every value in the bin with the\n                               bin's middle value (median) instead of the\n                               mean (median is less pulled around by any\n                               one extreme value inside that bin).\n            c) BIN BOUNDARIES -> for each value, look at just the two\n                               EDGE values of its bin (the smallest value\n                               in the bin, and the largest value in the\n                               bin), and replace it with WHICHEVER of\n                               those two edges it is numerically closer\n                               to. Unlike mean/median, this technique\n                               always produces one of the two original\n                               boundary numbers, never a \"new\" invented\n                               number.\n\nWHICH COLUMN, AND WHAT BIN SIZE?\nWe smooth the 'Fare' column (ticket price), because it is a real,\ncontinuous numeric measurement with plenty of natural variation - a good\ncandidate to demonstrate smoothing on. We use a bin size of 20 (so every\n20 sorted passengers form one bin) across the full column, which is small\nenough to visibly show a \"staircase\" smoothing effect on the graph without\ncreating too many tiny bins to see clearly.\n\n--- A small worked example first, on just 12 sample Fare values ---\nSample of 12 sorted Fare values: [ 0.      7.25    7.775   7.925   8.6625 12.2875 14.5    21.     26.25\n 31.275  55.     86.5   ]\n\nWith 3 bins of 4 values each:\n    Original  Bin Mean Smoothed  Bin Median Smoothed  Bin Boundary Smoothed\n0     0.0000               5.74                 7.51                   0.00\n1     7.2500               5.74                 7.51                   7.92\n2     7.7750               5.74                 7.51                   7.92\n3     7.9250               5.74                 7.51                   7.92\n4     8.6625              14.11                13.39                   8.66\n5    12.2875              14.11                13.39                   8.66\n6    14.5000              14.11                13.39                   8.66\n7    21.0000              14.11                13.39                  21.00\n8    26.2500              49.76                43.14                  26.25\n9    31.2750              49.76                43.14                  26.25\n10   55.0000              49.76                43.14                  26.25\n11   86.5000              49.76                43.14                  86.50\n\nReading this small example: within each group of 4 sorted values (one\nbin), \"Bin Mean Smoothed\" replaces all 4 with their average, \"Bin Median\nSmoothed\" replaces all 4 with their middle value, and \"Bin Boundary\nSmoothed\" pulls each individual value toward whichever end of its own bin\n(smallest or largest) it was originally closer to.\n\n--- Now applying the same 3 techniques across the full 'Fare' column ---\n  Saved plot -> plots/1_smoothing_full_fare_comparison.png\n  Saved plot -> plots/1_smoothing_zoomed_first_100.png\n\nHow to read these charts: the light-gray \"Original\" line is jagged because\nreal Fare values jump around a lot even between neighbouring sorted\npassengers. All three smoothing lines turn that jaggedness into flat\n\"steps\" (one flat step per bin of 20 passengers) - Bin Mean and Bin Median\ndraw a SINGLE flat value across the whole step (median is slightly more\nresistant to a single unusually extreme value inside a bin), while Bin\nBoundary instead snaps every point to one of just two possible values (the\nbin's minimum or its maximum), which is why its line looks more like sharp\nup/down jumps between only two levels within a step, rather than one\nperfectly flat line like mean/median smoothing.", "plots": [{"filename": "1_smoothing_full_fare_comparison.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}, {"filename": "1_smoothing_zoomed_first_100.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}, {"id": "question-2", "kind": "question", "number": 2, "label": "Identify outliers using Z-score and remove them.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 2: OUTLIER DETECTION AND REMOVAL USING Z-SCORE\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHAT IS A Z-SCORE?\nA Z-score tells you \"how many standard deviations away from the average is\nthis particular value?\". The formula for a value x in a column is:\n\n    z = (x - mean_of_column) / standard_deviation_of_column\n\n  - z = 0   means the value IS exactly the average.\n  - z = 1   means the value is 1 standard deviation ABOVE the average.\n  - z = -2  means the value is 2 standard deviations BELOW the average.\n\nWHY DOES A LARGE Z-SCORE MEAN \"OUTLIER\"?\nFor data that is roughly bell-shaped (a \"normal distribution\"), about 99.7%\nof all values naturally fall within 3 standard deviations of the mean\n(this is a well-known statistics result called the \"68-95-99.7 rule\").\nSo if a value's Z-score has an ABSOLUTE VALUE greater than 3 (i.e. it is\nmore than 3 standard deviations away from the mean, in EITHER direction),\nit is unusual enough, statistically, to be flagged as an outlier. This is\nthe standard, commonly used Z-score threshold, and the one we use here.\n\nNote this is a DIFFERENT outlier-detection method than Lab Assignment 2's\nbox-plot/IQR method - IQR is based on percentiles/ranking (works well even\nif the data is skewed), while Z-score is based on the mean and standard\ndeviation (works best when the data is roughly bell-shaped). Both are\nvalid, standard techniques; this question specifically asks us to use\nZ-score.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef zscore_outlier_mask(column):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a pandas Series of numbers, returns:\n      - the Z-score of every value (as a pandas Series, same order),\n      - a True/False mask that is True exactly where |z-score| > 3\n        (i.e. True means \"this row is an outlier for this column\").\n    \"\"\"\n    mean_value = column.mean()\n    std_value = column.std()\n    z_scores = (column - mean_value) / std_value\n    is_outlier = z_scores.abs() > 3\n    return z_scores, is_outlier"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We check BOTH numeric columns most likely to contain genuine extreme values: Age and Fare (the same two columns Lab Assignment 2 already flagged as containing IQR-based outliers).", "technical": "Both numeric columns most likely to contain genuine extreme values are checked: Age and Fare — the same two columns Lab Assignment 2 already flagged as containing IQR-based outliers, now re-examined under a different detection method.", "hinglish": "Dono numeric columns check kiye jaate hai jinme genuine extreme values hone ka sabse zyada chance hai: Age aur Fare — wahi do columns jo Lab Assignment 2 ne pehle hi IQR-based outliers ke roop mein flag kiye the, ab ek alag detection method ke through dobara examine kiye ja rahe hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "age_z, age_is_outlier = zscore_outlier_mask(df[\"Age\"])\nfare_z, fare_is_outlier = zscore_outlier_mask(df[\"Fare\"])\n\nprint(f\"Age  -> mean={df['Age'].mean():.2f}, std={df['Age'].std():.2f}, \"\n      f\"outliers with |z|>3: {age_is_outlier.sum()}\")\nprint(f\"Fare -> mean={df['Fare'].mean():.2f}, std={df['Fare'].std():.2f}, \"\n      f\"outliers with |z|>3: {fare_is_outlier.sum()}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "\"|\" between two True/False (boolean) columns means \"OR\" - so this marks a row as an outlier if EITHER its Age OR its Fare Z-score is extreme.", "technical": "\"|\" between two boolean columns is Python's element-wise logical OR - so a row is marked an outlier if EITHER its Age Z-score OR its Fare Z-score is extreme.", "hinglish": "\"|\" do boolean columns ke beech Python ka element-wise logical OR hai — to koi row outlier tab mark hoti hai jab uska Age Z-score YA Fare Z-score, in dono mein se koi bhi extreme ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "combined_outlier_mask = age_is_outlier | fare_is_outlier\noutlier_rows = df[combined_outlier_mask]\nprint(f\"\\nTotal unique rows flagged as an outlier by Age and/or Fare Z-score: {len(outlier_rows)}\")\nprint(\"\\nThe flagged outlier rows:\")\nprint(outlier_rows[[\"Survived\", \"Pclass\", \"Sex\", \"Age\", \"Fare\"]])"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "The \"~\" symbol means \"NOT\" - so this keeps only the rows that are NOT flagged as outliers, which is exactly the removal step the question asks for. We store this in a NEW variable so the earlier \"df\" (with outliers still in it) remains available if we ever wanted to compare against it.", "technical": "The \"~\" (bitwise NOT / logical negation) operator inverts the boolean mask, retaining only rows NOT flagged as outliers — precisely the removal step the question requires. The result is assigned to a NEW variable so the original \"df\" (outliers still included) remains available for comparison.", "hinglish": "\"~\" (bitwise NOT / logical negation) operator boolean mask ko invert kar deta hai, sirf wahi rows rakhta hai jo outlier flag NAHI hui hai — bilkul wahi removal step jo question maangta hai. Result ko ek NAYE variable mein assign kiya jaata hai taaki original \"df\" (jisme outliers abhi bhi hai) comparison ke liye available rahe."}, {"type": "code", "text": "df_no_outliers = df[~combined_outlier_mask].copy()\nprint(f\"\\nRow count before removal: {len(df)}\")\nprint(f\"Row count after removing Z-score outliers: {len(df_no_outliers)}\")\nprint(f\"Rows removed: {len(df) - len(df_no_outliers)}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A quick visual double-check: draw the Fare distribution before and after removing outliers, side by side, so you can literally see the extreme right-hand tail shrink.", "technical": "A visual sanity check renders the Fare distribution before and after outlier removal side by side, making the shrinkage of the extreme right-hand tail directly observable.", "hinglish": "Ek visual sanity check outlier removal se pehle aur baad ki Fare distribution ko side by side render karta hai, jisse extreme right-hand tail ka shrink hona directly dikh jaata hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 5))\naxes[0].hist(df[\"Fare\"], bins=30, color=\"lightcoral\", edgecolor=\"black\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Fare Distribution - BEFORE removing Z-score outliers\")\naxes[0].set_xlabel(\"Fare\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"Number of Passengers\")\n\naxes[1].hist(df_no_outliers[\"Fare\"], bins=30, color=\"seagreen\", edgecolor=\"black\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"Fare Distribution - AFTER removing Z-score outliers\")\naxes[1].set_xlabel(\"Fare\")\naxes[1].set_ylabel(\"Number of Passengers\")\nsave_current_plot(\"2_fare_distribution_before_after_outlier_removal.png\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nNOTE: Just like in Lab Assignment 2, these Z-score-flagged Age/Fare\noutliers are genuine, realistic extreme values (very high fares, unusually\nelderly passengers) rather than data-entry mistakes. Removing them here is\ndone specifically because THIS assignment asks us to practice the \"detect\nwith Z-score, then remove\" workflow end-to-end - in a real project, you\nwould think carefully before permanently deleting real, valid data points\njust for being unusual, since it can also throw away genuinely useful\ninformation. We proceed with the outlier-free dataset for Question 3\nbelow, since normalization techniques (especially Min-Max and Decimal\nScaling) are themselves sensitive to extreme outliers, so cleaning them\nfirst makes the upcoming scaling comparison clearer and fairer.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "WHAT IS A Z-SCORE?\nA Z-score tells you \"how many standard deviations away from the average is\nthis particular value?\". The formula for a value x in a column is:\n\n    z = (x - mean_of_column) / standard_deviation_of_column\n\n  - z = 0   means the value IS exactly the average.\n  - z = 1   means the value is 1 standard deviation ABOVE the average.\n  - z = -2  means the value is 2 standard deviations BELOW the average.\n\nWHY DOES A LARGE Z-SCORE MEAN \"OUTLIER\"?\nFor data that is roughly bell-shaped (a \"normal distribution\"), about 99.7%\nof all values naturally fall within 3 standard deviations of the mean\n(this is a well-known statistics result called the \"68-95-99.7 rule\").\nSo if a value's Z-score has an ABSOLUTE VALUE greater than 3 (i.e. it is\nmore than 3 standard deviations away from the mean, in EITHER direction),\nit is unusual enough, statistically, to be flagged as an outlier. This is\nthe standard, commonly used Z-score threshold, and the one we use here.\n\nNote this is a DIFFERENT outlier-detection method than Lab Assignment 2's\nbox-plot/IQR method - IQR is based on percentiles/ranking (works well even\nif the data is skewed), while Z-score is based on the mean and standard\ndeviation (works best when the data is roughly bell-shaped). Both are\nvalid, standard techniques; this question specifically asks us to use\nZ-score.\n\nAge  -> mean=29.36, std=13.02, outliers with |z|>3: 7\nFare -> mean=32.20, std=49.69, outliers with |z|>3: 20\n\nTotal unique rows flagged as an outlier by Age and/or Fare Z-score: 27\n\nThe flagged outlier rows:\n     Survived  Pclass     Sex   Age      Fare\n27          0       1    male  19.0  263.0000\n88          1       1  female  23.0  263.0000\n96          0       1    male  71.0   34.6542\n116         0       3    male  70.5    7.7500\n118         0       1    male  24.0  247.5208\n258         1       1  female  35.0  512.3292\n299         1       1  female  50.0  247.5208\n311         1       1  female  18.0  262.3750\n341         1       1  female  24.0  263.0000\n377         0       1    male  27.0  211.5000\n380         1       1  female  42.0  227.5250\n438         0       1    male  64.0  263.0000\n493         0       1    male  71.0   49.5042\n527         0       1    male  28.0  221.7792\n557         0       1    male  28.0  227.5250\n630         1       1    male  80.0   30.0000\n672         0       2    male  70.0   10.5000\n679         1       1    male  36.0  512.3292\n689         1       1  female  15.0  211.3375\n700         1       1  female  18.0  227.5250\n716         1       1  female  38.0  227.5250\n730         1       1  female  29.0  211.3375\n737         1       1    male  35.0  512.3292\n742         1       1  female  21.0  262.3750\n745         0       1    male  70.0   71.0000\n779         1       1  female  43.0  211.3375\n851         0       3    male  74.0    7.7750\n\nRow count before removal: 891\nRow count after removing Z-score outliers: 864\nRows removed: 27\n  Saved plot -> plots/2_fare_distribution_before_after_outlier_removal.png\n\nNOTE: Just like in Lab Assignment 2, these Z-score-flagged Age/Fare\noutliers are genuine, realistic extreme values (very high fares, unusually\nelderly passengers) rather than data-entry mistakes. Removing them here is\ndone specifically because THIS assignment asks us to practice the \"detect\nwith Z-score, then remove\" workflow end-to-end - in a real project, you\nwould think carefully before permanently deleting real, valid data points\njust for being unusual, since it can also throw away genuinely useful\ninformation. We proceed with the outlier-free dataset for Question 3\nbelow, since normalization techniques (especially Min-Max and Decimal\nScaling) are themselves sensitive to extreme outliers, so cleaning them\nfirst makes the upcoming scaling comparison clearer and fairer.", "plots": [{"filename": "2_fare_distribution_before_after_outlier_removal.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}, {"id": "question-3", "kind": "question", "number": 3, "label": "Perform 7 different types of data normalization on the outlier-free dataset from Question 2.", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 3: SEVEN NORMALIZATION / SCALING TECHNIQUES\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "We scale 'Age' and 'Fare' - the same two genuine numeric measurement columns used throughout this whole assignment series, for the same reasons explained in Lab Assignment 2 (SibSp/Parch are small integer counts with limited spread, and Pclass/Sex/Embarked are categories, not true continuous measurements).", "technical": "Age and Fare are the columns scaled — the same two genuine continuous measurement columns used throughout this assignment series, for the reasons already established in Lab Assignment 2 (SibSp/Parch are low-cardinality integer counts with limited spread; Pclass/Sex/Embarked are categorical, not true continuous measurements).", "hinglish": "Age aur Fare hi wo columns hai jo scale kiye jaate hai — wahi do genuine continuous measurement columns jo is poori assignment series mein use hue hai, Lab Assignment 2 mein establish ki gayi wajahon se (SibSp/Parch low-cardinality integer counts hai limited spread ke saath; Pclass/Sex/Embarked categorical hai, true continuous measurements nahi)."}, {"type": "code", "text": "columns_to_scale = [\"Age\", \"Fare\"]\nwork_df = df_no_outliers[columns_to_scale].copy()\n\nprint(f\"Working on the OUTLIER-REMOVED data from Question 2 \"\n      f\"({len(work_df)} rows), columns: {columns_to_scale}\\n\")\nprint(\"Original (unscaled) statistics:\")\nprint(work_df.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "This dictionary will collect every scaled version so we can print a final side-by-side comparison table at the very end.", "technical": "This dictionary accumulates every scaled version of the data, so a final side-by-side comparison table can be printed at the end.", "hinglish": "Ye dictionary data ke har scaled version ko accumulate karti hai, taaki aakhir mein ek final side-by-side comparison table print ki ja sake."}, {"type": "code", "text": "results = {}\nresults[\"Original\"] = work_df.copy()"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@3a) MIN-MAX NORMALIZATION@@", "technical": "@@(a) Min-Max normalization@@", "hinglish": "@@(a) Min-Max normalization@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (a) MIN-MAX NORMALIZATION ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - min) / (max - min)\nSqueezes every value into the range [0, 1]. The smallest value in the\ncolumn becomes 0, the largest becomes 1.\n\"\"\")\nminmax_scaler = MinMaxScaler()\nminmax_result = pd.DataFrame(\n    minmax_scaler.fit_transform(work_df), columns=columns_to_scale\n)\nresults[\"Min-Max\"] = minmax_result\nprint(minmax_result.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@3b) MEAN NORMALIZATION@@", "technical": "@@(b) Mean normalization@@", "hinglish": "@@(b) Mean normalization@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (b) MEAN NORMALIZATION ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - mean) / (max - min)\nThis is very similar to Min-Max, but instead of shifting by the MINIMUM\nvalue, it shifts by the column's MEAN (average) instead. The result is\ncentred much closer to 0 (values below the average become negative,\nvalues above the average become positive), while still being divided by\nthe same (max - min) \"range\" as Min-Max. scikit-learn does not have a\nsingle ready-made function for this exact formula, so we calculate it\nourselves directly with pandas/numpy - this is a good example of how,\nonce you understand a formula, you don't strictly need a library function\nfor every single technique.\n\"\"\")\nmean_norm_result = pd.DataFrame()\nfor col in columns_to_scale:\n    col_min = work_df[col].min()\n    col_max = work_df[col].max()\n    col_mean = work_df[col].mean()\n    mean_norm_result[col] = (work_df[col] - col_mean) / (col_max - col_min)\nresults[\"Mean Normalization\"] = mean_norm_result\nprint(mean_norm_result.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@3c) Z-SCORE METHOD (STANDARDIZATION)@@", "technical": "@@(c) Z-score method (standardization)@@", "hinglish": "@@(c) Z-score method (standardization)@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (c) Z-SCORE METHOD (Standardization) ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - mean) / standard_deviation\nThis is the exact same Z-score formula from Question 2 - but this time we\napply it as a SCALING technique (to reshape the whole column), rather than\nas an outlier-detection rule. After this transform, the column's mean\nbecomes 0 and its standard deviation becomes 1.\n\"\"\")\nzscore_scaler = StandardScaler()\nzscore_result = pd.DataFrame(\n    zscore_scaler.fit_transform(work_df), columns=columns_to_scale\n)\nresults[\"Z-score\"] = zscore_result\nprint(zscore_result.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@3d) DECIMAL SCALING@@", "technical": "@@(d) Decimal scaling@@", "hinglish": "@@(d) Decimal scaling@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (d) DECIMAL SCALING ---\nFormula: x_scaled = x / 10^j\nwhere \"j\" is the SMALLEST whole number that makes every scaled value's\nabsolute size less than 1. In practice: j = the number of digits in the\nlargest absolute value in the column (e.g. if the biggest value is 512,\nit has 3 digits, so j = 3, and every value gets divided by 10^3 = 1000).\nThis simply \"moves the decimal point\" of every number the same number of\nplaces to the left - the simplest of all these techniques, and one\nscikit-learn also has no ready-made function for, so we calculate it\nourselves.\n\"\"\")\ndecimal_scaling_result = pd.DataFrame()\nfor col in columns_to_scale:\n    max_abs_value = work_df[col].abs().max()"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "math.log10(max_abs_value) tells us roughly how many digits the number has (e.g. log10(512) is about 2.7). math.floor(...) + 1 turns that into the correct whole number of digits (e.g. 3 for 512). We guard against max_abs_value being 0 (would make log10 undefined).", "technical": "math.log10(max_abs_value) yields the approximate order of magnitude — roughly, the digit count — of the value (e.g., log10(512) ≈ 2.7). math.floor(...) + 1 converts this into the correct integer digit count (e.g., 3 for 512). A guard against max_abs_value being 0 prevents an undefined log10(0) call.", "hinglish": "math.log10(max_abs_value) value ka approximate order of magnitude deta hai — roughly, digits ki count (jaise, log10(512) ≈ 2.7). math.floor(...) + 1 ise sahi integer digit count mein convert kar deta hai (jaise, 512 ke liye 3). max_abs_value ke 0 hone ke against ek guard lagaya gaya hai taaki undefined log10(0) call na ho."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    if max_abs_value == 0:\n        j = 1\n    else:\n        j = math.floor(math.log10(max_abs_value)) + 1\n    decimal_scaling_result[col] = work_df[col] / (10 ** j)\n    print(f\"  Column '{col}': max absolute value = {max_abs_value:.2f} -> \"\n          f\"{j} digits -> dividing every value by 10^{j} = {10**j}\")\nresults[\"Decimal Scaling\"] = decimal_scaling_result\nprint(decimal_scaling_result.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@3e) LOG TRANSFORMER@@", "technical": "@@(e) Log transformer@@", "hinglish": "@@(e) Log transformer@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (e) LOG TRANSFORMER ---\nFormula: x_scaled = natural_log(x)   [natural log, i.e. base \"e\", matching\nthis course's convention - see Lecture 7's Log Transformer notes]\nA log transform is especially useful for columns like Fare that are\nstrongly \"right-skewed\" (most values are small/cheap, but a long tail of\nexpensive outliers stretches the column far to the right) - taking the log\ncompresses that long tail down and pulls the whole distribution closer to\na symmetric, bell-like shape, which many statistical methods work better\nwith.\n\nIMPORTANT REAL DATA CAVEAT WE CHECKED FOR: natural log of 0 is undefined\n(mathematically, it is negative infinity), and this Titanic dataset\ngenuinely contains a small number of passengers with Fare = 0 (crew/staff\ntravelling for free, or comped tickets). Applying a plain log(x) here\nwould produce invalid \"-inf\" results for those exact rows. The standard,\nsafe fix - and what we use here - is \"log1p\", i.e. natural_log(x + 1)\ninstead of natural_log(x): this keeps log(0) = log(1) = 0 (a valid,\nsensible number) instead of crashing into -infinity, while barely changing\nthe result for any larger Fare value. Age has no zero or negative values\nin this dataset, so this caveat only actually matters for Fare, but we\napply log1p consistently to both columns for safety.\n\"\"\")\nzero_fare_count = (work_df[\"Fare\"] == 0).sum()\nprint(f\"(Confirmed: {zero_fare_count} passenger(s) in this outlier-removed \"\n      f\"data have Fare = 0, which is exactly why log1p was needed instead of a plain log.)\")\n\nlog_result = pd.DataFrame()\nfor col in columns_to_scale:"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "np.log1p(x) calculates natural_log(x + 1) directly and safely.", "technical": "np.log1p(x) computes natural_log(x + 1) directly, in a single, numerically safe operation.", "hinglish": "np.log1p(x) natural_log(x + 1) ko seedhe, ek single, numerically safe operation mein calculate kar deta hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    log_result[col] = np.log1p(work_df[col])\nresults[\"Log Transform (log1p)\"] = log_result\nprint(log_result.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@3f) MAXABS TRANSFORMER@@", "technical": "@@(f) MaxAbs transformer@@", "hinglish": "@@(f) MaxAbs transformer@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (f) MAXABS TRANSFORMER ---\nFormula: x_scaled = x / max(|x|)\nDivides every value by the single LARGEST ABSOLUTE value in the column.\nThe largest-magnitude value in the column becomes exactly +1 or -1, and\nevery other value lands somewhere inside [-1, 1]. Since both Age and Fare\nhere only ever contain positive numbers, this behaves very similarly to\nMin-Max in this particular case (both end up in [0, 1]) - but MaxAbs is\nthe better/correct choice in general specifically WHEN a column can\ncontain negative numbers too, since (unlike Min-Max) it doesn't shift the\ndata's zero point, it only rescales around the existing zero.\n\"\"\")\nmaxabs_scaler = MaxAbsScaler()\nmaxabs_result = pd.DataFrame(\n    maxabs_scaler.fit_transform(work_df), columns=columns_to_scale\n)\nresults[\"MaxAbs\"] = maxabs_result\nprint(maxabs_result.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@3g) INTERQUARTILE / ROBUST SCALER@@", "technical": "@@(g) Interquartile / Robust scaler@@", "hinglish": "@@(g) Interquartile / Robust scaler@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (g) INTERQUARTILE SCALER / ROBUST SCALER ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - median) / (Q3 - Q1)\nInstead of using the mean/min/max (all of which can be dragged around a\nlot by a single extreme outlier), this technique centres the data using\nthe MEDIAN, and scales it using the IQR (Q3 - Q1, the width of just the\n\"middle 50%\" of the data - see Lab Assignment 2's Q2 for a full\nexplanation of Q1/Q3/IQR). Because the median and IQR both ignore the most\nextreme values by design, this scaler is far less affected by outliers\nthan any of the other 6 techniques above - which is exactly why it's\ncalled \"Robust\" (robust = \"not easily thrown off by unusual/extreme\nvalues\").\n\"\"\")\nrobust_scaler = RobustScaler()\nrobust_result = pd.DataFrame(\n    robust_scaler.fit_transform(work_df), columns=columns_to_scale\n)\nresults[\"Robust (IQR)\"] = robust_result\nprint(robust_result.describe().round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "FINAL SIDE-BY-SIDE COMPARISON (using Fare only, since it's the more skewed/outlier-prone of our two columns, so differences are most visible)", "technical": "Final side-by-side comparison (Fare only, since it is the more skewed, more outlier-prone of the two columns, making differences most visible).", "hinglish": "Final side-by-side comparison (sirf Fare, kyunki ye dono columns mein zyada skewed aur zyada outlier-prone hai, isliye differences sabse zyada clearly dikhte hai)."}, {"type": "code", "text": "section(\"SIDE-BY-SIDE COMPARISON OF ALL 7 TECHNIQUES (Fare column, first 5 rows)\")\n\ncomparison_table = pd.DataFrame({\n    name: result_df[\"Fare\"].head(5).values for name, result_df in results.items()\n})\nprint(comparison_table.round(3))\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nHow to read this comparison: notice how differently the SAME 5 original\nFare values end up looking depending on which technique is used - some\ntechniques (Min-Max, MaxAbs) force everything into a tight [0, 1] range,\nZ-score and Robust can produce small negative numbers, Decimal Scaling\njust shifts the decimal point without changing the overall \"shape\" of the\nnumbers at all, and Log Transform visibly compresses big values much more\naggressively than small ones. There is no single \"best\" technique for\nevery situation - which one to use depends on the shape of your data\n(especially how skewed it is, and how many outliers it has) and on which\ndownstream machine learning algorithm you plan to use next.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A bar chart comparing how each technique reshapes just the Fare column's mean and standard deviation, so the differences are visible at a glance.", "technical": "A bar chart compares how each technique reshapes the Fare column's mean and standard deviation, making the differences visible at a glance.", "hinglish": "Ek bar chart compare karta hai ki har technique Fare column ke mean aur standard deviation ko kaise reshape karti hai, jisse differences ek nazar mein dikh jaate hai."}, {"type": "code", "text": "means = [result_df[\"Fare\"].mean() for result_df in results.values()]\nstds = [result_df[\"Fare\"].std() for result_df in results.values()]\nlabels = list(results.keys())\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 5))\naxes[0].bar(labels, means, color=\"cornflowerblue\")\naxes[0].set_title(\"Mean of 'Fare' After Each Scaling Technique\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"Mean\")\naxes[0].tick_params(axis=\"x\", rotation=45)\n\naxes[1].bar(labels, stds, color=\"salmon\")\naxes[1].set_title(\"Standard Deviation of 'Fare' After Each Scaling Technique\")\naxes[1].set_ylabel(\"Standard Deviation\")\naxes[1].tick_params(axis=\"x\", rotation=45)\nsave_current_plot(\"3_scaling_techniques_mean_std_comparison.png\")"}], "output": "Working on the OUTLIER-REMOVED data from Question 2 (864 rows), columns: ['Age', 'Fare']\n\nOriginal (unscaled) statistics:\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean    28.979   26.501\nstd     12.499   29.400\nmin      0.420    0.000\n25%     22.000    7.896\n50%     28.000   13.860\n75%     35.000   29.775\nmax     66.000  164.867\n\n--- (a) MIN-MAX NORMALIZATION ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - min) / (max - min)\nSqueezes every value into the range [0, 1]. The smallest value in the\ncolumn becomes 0, the largest becomes 1.\n\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean     0.435    0.161\nstd      0.191    0.178\nmin      0.000    0.000\n25%      0.329    0.048\n50%      0.421    0.084\n75%      0.527    0.181\nmax      1.000    1.000\n\n--- (b) MEAN NORMALIZATION ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - mean) / (max - min)\nThis is very similar to Min-Max, but instead of shifting by the MINIMUM\nvalue, it shifts by the column's MEAN (average) instead. The result is\ncentred much closer to 0 (values below the average become negative,\nvalues above the average become positive), while still being divided by\nthe same (max - min) \"range\" as Min-Max. scikit-learn does not have a\nsingle ready-made function for this exact formula, so we calculate it\nourselves directly with pandas/numpy - this is a good example of how,\nonce you understand a formula, you don't strictly need a library function\nfor every single technique.\n\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean     0.000   -0.000\nstd      0.191    0.178\nmin     -0.435   -0.161\n25%     -0.106   -0.113\n50%     -0.015   -0.077\n75%      0.092    0.020\nmax      0.565    0.839\n\n--- (c) Z-SCORE METHOD (Standardization) ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - mean) / standard_deviation\nThis is the exact same Z-score formula from Question 2 - but this time we\napply it as a SCALING technique (to reshape the whole column), rather than\nas an outlier-detection rule. After this transform, the column's mean\nbecomes 0 and its standard deviation becomes 1.\n\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean     0.000   -0.000\nstd      1.001    1.001\nmin     -2.286   -0.902\n25%     -0.559   -0.633\n50%     -0.078   -0.430\n75%      0.482    0.111\nmax      2.964    4.709\n\n--- (d) DECIMAL SCALING ---\nFormula: x_scaled = x / 10^j\nwhere \"j\" is the SMALLEST whole number that makes every scaled value's\nabsolute size less than 1. In practice: j = the number of digits in the\nlargest absolute value in the column (e.g. if the biggest value is 512,\nit has 3 digits, so j = 3, and every value gets divided by 10^3 = 1000).\nThis simply \"moves the decimal point\" of every number the same number of\nplaces to the left - the simplest of all these techniques, and one\nscikit-learn also has no ready-made function for, so we calculate it\nourselves.\n\n  Column 'Age': max absolute value = 66.00 -> 2 digits -> dividing every value by 10^2 = 100\n  Column 'Fare': max absolute value = 164.87 -> 3 digits -> dividing every value by 10^3 = 1000\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean     0.290    0.027\nstd      0.125    0.029\nmin      0.004    0.000\n25%      0.220    0.008\n50%      0.280    0.014\n75%      0.350    0.030\nmax      0.660    0.165\n\n--- (e) LOG TRANSFORMER ---\nFormula: x_scaled = natural_log(x)   [natural log, i.e. base \"e\", matching\nthis course's convention - see Lecture 7's Log Transformer notes]\nA log transform is especially useful for columns like Fare that are\nstrongly \"right-skewed\" (most values are small/cheap, but a long tail of\nexpensive outliers stretches the column far to the right) - taking the log\ncompresses that long tail down and pulls the whole distribution closer to\na symmetric, bell-like shape, which many statistical methods work better\nwith.\n\nIMPORTANT REAL DATA CAVEAT WE CHECKED FOR: natural log of 0 is undefined\n(mathematically, it is negative infinity), and this Titanic dataset\ngenuinely contains a small number of passengers with Fare = 0 (crew/staff\ntravelling for free, or comped tickets). Applying a plain log(x) here\nwould produce invalid \"-inf\" results for those exact rows. The standard,\nsafe fix - and what we use here - is \"log1p\", i.e. natural_log(x + 1)\ninstead of natural_log(x): this keeps log(0) = log(1) = 0 (a valid,\nsensible number) instead of crashing into -infinity, while barely changing\nthe result for any larger Fare value. Age has no zero or negative values\nin this dataset, so this caveat only actually matters for Fare, but we\napply log1p consistently to both columns for safety.\n\n(Confirmed: 15 passenger(s) in this outlier-removed data have Fare = 0, which is exactly why log1p was needed instead of a plain log.)\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean     3.270    2.900\nstd      0.614    0.893\nmin      0.351    0.000\n25%      3.135    2.186\n50%      3.367    2.699\n75%      3.584    3.427\nmax      4.205    5.111\n\n--- (f) MAXABS TRANSFORMER ---\nFormula: x_scaled = x / max(|x|)\nDivides every value by the single LARGEST ABSOLUTE value in the column.\nThe largest-magnitude value in the column becomes exactly +1 or -1, and\nevery other value lands somewhere inside [-1, 1]. Since both Age and Fare\nhere only ever contain positive numbers, this behaves very similarly to\nMin-Max in this particular case (both end up in [0, 1]) - but MaxAbs is\nthe better/correct choice in general specifically WHEN a column can\ncontain negative numbers too, since (unlike Min-Max) it doesn't shift the\ndata's zero point, it only rescales around the existing zero.\n\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean     0.439    0.161\nstd      0.189    0.178\nmin      0.006    0.000\n25%      0.333    0.048\n50%      0.424    0.084\n75%      0.530    0.181\nmax      1.000    1.000\n\n--- (g) INTERQUARTILE SCALER / ROBUST SCALER ---\nFormula: x_scaled = (x - median) / (Q3 - Q1)\nInstead of using the mean/min/max (all of which can be dragged around a\nlot by a single extreme outlier), this technique centres the data using\nthe MEDIAN, and scales it using the IQR (Q3 - Q1, the width of just the\n\"middle 50%\" of the data - see Lab Assignment 2's Q2 for a full\nexplanation of Q1/Q3/IQR). Because the median and IQR both ignore the most\nextreme values by design, this scaler is far less affected by outliers\nthan any of the other 6 techniques above - which is exactly why it's\ncalled \"Robust\" (robust = \"not easily thrown off by unusual/extreme\nvalues\").\n\n           Age     Fare\ncount  864.000  864.000\nmean     0.075    0.578\nstd      0.961    1.344\nmin     -2.122   -0.633\n25%     -0.462   -0.273\n50%      0.000    0.000\n75%      0.538    0.727\nmax      2.923    6.902\n\n   Original  Min-Max  Mean Normalization  Z-score  Decimal Scaling  Log Transform (log1p)  MaxAbs  Robust (IQR)\n0     7.250    0.044              -0.117   -0.655            0.007                  2.110   0.044        -0.302\n1    71.283    0.432               0.272    1.524            0.071                  4.281   0.432         2.625\n2     7.925    0.048              -0.113   -0.632            0.008                  2.189   0.048        -0.271\n3    53.100    0.322               0.161    0.905            0.053                  3.991   0.322         1.793\n4     8.050    0.049              -0.112   -0.628            0.008                  2.203   0.049        -0.266\n\nHow to read this comparison: notice how differently the SAME 5 original\nFare values end up looking depending on which technique is used - some\ntechniques (Min-Max, MaxAbs) force everything into a tight [0, 1] range,\nZ-score and Robust can produce small negative numbers, Decimal Scaling\njust shifts the decimal point without changing the overall \"shape\" of the\nnumbers at all, and Log Transform visibly compresses big values much more\naggressively than small ones. There is no single \"best\" technique for\nevery situation - which one to use depends on the shape of your data\n(especially how skewed it is, and how many outliers it has) and on which\ndownstream machine learning algorithm you plan to use next.\n\n  Saved plot -> plots/3_scaling_techniques_mean_std_comparison.png", "plots": [{"filename": "3_scaling_techniques_mean_std_comparison.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}, {"id": "wrapup", "kind": "wrapup", "number": null, "label": "Saving Your Results", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"SAVING OUTPUT FILES\")\n\noutlier_free_path = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"Titanic-Dataset-NoOutliers.csv\")\ndf_no_outliers.to_csv(outlier_free_path, index=False)\nprint(f\"Saved outlier-free dataset (Question 2 result) -> {outlier_free_path}\")\n\nfor name, result_df in results.items():\n    if name == \"Original\":\n        continue"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Turn a readable name like \"Log Transform (log1p)\" into a safe filename like \"Log_Transform_log1p\" (no spaces, brackets, etc).", "technical": "A human-readable name such as \"Log Transform (log1p)\" is converted into a filesystem-safe filename such as \"Log_Transform_log1p\" (no spaces, brackets, or other characters a filename cannot contain).", "hinglish": "Ek human-readable naam jaise \"Log Transform (log1p)\" ko ek filesystem-safe filename jaise \"Log_Transform_log1p\" mein convert kiya jaata hai (koi spaces, brackets, ya aise characters nahi jo ek filename mein allowed nahi hote)."}, {"type": "code", "text": "    safe_name = \"\".join(ch if ch.isalnum() else \"_\" for ch in name).strip(\"_\")\n    output_path = os.path.join(this_script_folder, f\"Titanic-Scaled-{safe_name}.csv\")\n    result_df.to_csv(output_path, index=False)\n    print(f\"Saved '{name}' scaled Age/Fare columns -> {output_path}\")\n\nprint(f\"\\nAll plots were saved inside: {plots_folder}\")\nprint(\"\\nAll 3 questions of Lab Assignment 3 are complete.\")"}], "output": "Saved outlier-free dataset (Question 2 result) -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Dataset-NoOutliers.csv\nSaved 'Min-Max' scaled Age/Fare columns -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Scaled-Min_Max.csv\nSaved 'Mean Normalization' scaled Age/Fare columns -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Scaled-Mean_Normalization.csv\nSaved 'Z-score' scaled Age/Fare columns -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Scaled-Z_score.csv\nSaved 'Decimal Scaling' scaled Age/Fare columns -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Scaled-Decimal_Scaling.csv\nSaved 'Log Transform (log1p)' scaled Age/Fare columns -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Scaled-Log_Transform__log1p.csv\nSaved 'MaxAbs' scaled Age/Fare columns -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Scaled-MaxAbs.csv\nSaved 'Robust (IQR)' scaled Age/Fare columns -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/Titanic-Scaled-Robust__IQR.csv\n\nAll plots were saved inside: /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 3/plots\n\nAll 3 questions of Lab Assignment 3 are complete.", "plots": []}]}, {"num": 4, "title": "Lab Assignment 4", "subtitle": "Feature Selection: Correlation, Redundancy, and Distance/Similarity Measures", "dataset": "Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv", "intro": "WHAT IS THIS FILE, AND WHO IS IT FOR?\n----------------------------------------\nSame style as Lab Assignments 1-3: a plain Python script written for a\ncomplete beginner who has never touched Python, pandas, matplotlib, or\nscikit-learn before. Every step is explained in plain English before the\ncode that does it, plus short \"#\" comments on the important lines.\n\nWHERE DOES THE DATA COME FROM, AND HOW DO THE 4 QUESTIONS CONNECT?\n------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis assignment is about FEATURE SELECTION (deciding which columns of a\ndataset are actually useful, and which are wasteful/duplicated) - this is\na completely different topic from cleaning (Lab 1), scaling (Lab 2), or\nsmoothing/outlier-removal (Lab 3), so we start fresh from Lab Assignment\n1's cleaned CSV (not from Lab 3's outlier-removed or scaled versions -\nfeature selection here works on the whole cleaned dataset, and this\nscript does its own standardization later, exactly where it's needed).\n\n    Lab Assignment 1 -> cleaned the raw Titanic data (this is what we load)\n    Lab Assignment 4 (THIS FILE) ->\n        SETUP : turns the two text/category columns ('Sex', 'Embarked')\n                into numeric 0/1 columns, since correlation and all the\n                distance/similarity formulas below only work on numbers\n        Q1    : builds and visualizes the CORRELATION MATRIX of every\n                feature against every other feature\n        Q2    : scans that same correlation matrix for any PAIR of\n                features that are \"highly correlated\" (i.e. saying\n                almost the same thing)\n        Q3    : REMOVES one feature from each highly-correlated pair\n                found in Q2, producing a smaller, non-redundant feature\n                set\n        Q4    : goes back to the ORIGINAL feature list (before Q3's\n                removal) and measures how similar/different every pair\n                of features is using 6 different distance/similarity\n                formulas from the lecture notes (Euclidean, Manhattan,\n                Hamming, Cosine, Jaccard, SMC) - a second, independent\n                way of spotting redundant features, from a totally\n                different angle than plain correlation\n\nSo Q1 -> Q2 -> Q3 is one connected pipeline (correlation-based redundancy\nremoval), while Q4 is a separate, parallel demonstration of 6 more\nredundancy-measuring techniques applied to the same dataset, so you can\ncompare what each technique flags.\n\nWe load the already-cleaned CSV that Lab Assignment 1 produced:\n    ../Lab Assignment 1/Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\nMake sure you have run Lab Assignment 1's script at least once already, so\nthat file exists.\n\nHOW TO RUN THIS FILE\n----------------------\n1. Make sure Python 3 is installed.\n2. Make sure these libraries are installed (run once, if needed):\n        pip install pandas numpy matplotlib scikit-learn\n3. Run this script from inside THIS folder (\"Lab Assignment 4\"):\n        python3 lab_assignment_4.py\n   It prints all explanations and tables to the terminal, saves every\n   chart as a \".png\" image inside a new \"plots\" folder next to this\n   script, and saves the final, redundancy-free feature set as a CSV\n   file at the end.\n\n==================================================================================", "sections": [{"id": "setup", "kind": "setup", "number": null, "label": "Setup: Loading the Dataset", "chunks": [{"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE LIBRARIES WE NEED@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE LIBRARIES WE NEED@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0: IMPORT THE LIBRARIES WE NEED@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "import os                      # for building reliable file paths\nimport numpy as np             # numpy = \"Numerical Python\" - a toolbox for fast maths on lists of numbers (arrays)\nimport pandas as pd            # our table/DataFrame toolbox, as in every earlier assignment\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt  # our charting toolbox, as in Lab Assignments 2 and 3"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "From scikit-learn, we import StandardScaler - the same Z-score scaling tool used in Lab Assignment 3, needed here so that Euclidean/Manhattan/ Cosine distances in Question 4 aren't unfairly dominated by whichever original column happens to have the biggest raw numbers (e.g. 'Fare').", "technical": "From scikit-learn, we import StandardScaler - the same Z-score scaling tool used in Lab Assignment 3, needed here so that Euclidean/Manhattan/ Cosine distances in Question 4 aren't unfairly dominated by whichever original column happens to have the biggest raw numbers (e.g. 'Fare').", "hinglish": "From scikit-learn, we import StandardScaler - the same Z-score scaling tool used in Lab Assignment 3, needed here so that Euclidean/Manhattan/ Cosine distances in Question 4 aren't unfairly dominated by whichever original column happens to have the biggest raw numbers (e.g. 'Fare')."}, {"type": "code", "text": "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A very large \"display.width\" makes a wide table always print as ONE line per row, instead of pandas splitting it into two confusing stacked blocks.", "technical": "A very large \"display.width\" makes a wide table always print as ONE line per row, instead of pandas splitting it into two confusing stacked blocks.", "hinglish": "A very large \"display.width\" makes a wide table always print as ONE line per row, instead of pandas splitting it into two confusing stacked blocks."}, {"type": "code", "text": "pd.set_option(\"display.max_columns\", None)\npd.set_option(\"display.width\", 1000)\n\n\ndef section(title):\n    \"\"\"Prints a clear section heading. See Lab Assignment 1 for a full\n    explanation of what a Python function/def is.\"\"\"\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 90)\n    print(title)\n    print(\"=\" * 90)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0b: RELIABLE FILE PATHS + A FOLDER TO SAVE PLOTS INTO@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0b: RELIABLE FILE PATHS + A FOLDER TO SAVE PLOTS INTO@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0b: RELIABLE FILE PATHS + A FOLDER TO SAVE PLOTS INTO@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "this_script_folder = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))\ncleaned_csv_path = os.path.join(\n    this_script_folder, \"..\", \"Lab Assignment 1\", \"Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\"\n)\nplots_folder = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"plots\")\nos.makedirs(plots_folder, exist_ok=True)\n\n\ndef save_current_plot(filename):\n    \"\"\"Saves whatever chart is currently drawn to an image file inside our\n    'plots' folder, then clears the canvas so the next chart starts fresh.\n    (Full explanation of every line here is in Lab Assignment 2's script.)\"\"\"\n    full_path = os.path.join(plots_folder, filename)\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.savefig(full_path)\n    plt.close()\n    print(f\"  Saved plot -> plots/{filename}\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0c: LOAD THE CLEANED DATASET@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0c: LOAD THE CLEANED DATASET@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0c: LOAD THE CLEANED DATASET@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "section(\"LOADING THE CLEANED DATASET FROM LAB ASSIGNMENT 1\")\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(cleaned_csv_path)\nprint(f\"Loaded cleaned dataset from: {cleaned_csv_path}\")\nprint(f\"Shape: {df.shape[0]} rows x {df.shape[1]} columns\")\nprint(\"Columns:\", list(df.columns))\nprint(df.head())"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@STEP 0d: TURN THE TWO TEXT COLUMNS INTO NUMBERS@@", "technical": "@@STEP 0d: TURN THE TWO TEXT COLUMNS INTO NUMBERS@@", "hinglish": "@@STEP 0d: TURN THE TWO TEXT COLUMNS INTO NUMBERS@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "section(\"SETUP: ENCODING 'Sex' AND 'Embarked' AS NUMBERS\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHY DO WE NEED TO DO THIS FIRST?\nCorrelation and every distance/similarity formula in this assignment\n(Euclidean, Manhattan, Hamming, Cosine, Jaccard, SMC) are all pure MATH\nformulas - they only work on numbers, not text. Our cleaned dataset still\nhas two text (\"category\") columns: 'Sex' (male/female) and 'Embarked'\n(S/C/Q, the port the passenger boarded from). So, before anything else,\nwe convert both into numeric 0/1 columns:\n\n  'Sex' -> ONE new column, 'Sex_male' (1 = male, 0 = female). Since there\n           are only 2 possible values, a single 0/1 column is enough to\n           capture the information with no ambiguity.\n\n  'Embarked' -> THREE new columns, one per port: 'Embarked_C',\n           'Embarked_Q', 'Embarked_S' (each is 1 if the passenger boarded\n           there, 0 otherwise). This standard technique is called\n           \"ONE-HOT ENCODING\" - since Embarked has 3 possible values (not\n           2), we need 3 separate 0/1 columns, exactly one of which is 1\n           for any given passenger.\n\"\"\")\n\nencoded_df = df.copy()\nencoded_df[\"Sex_male\"] = (encoded_df[\"Sex\"] == \"male\").astype(int)\nencoded_df = pd.get_dummies(encoded_df, columns=[\"Embarked\"], prefix=\"Embarked\")\nencoded_df = encoded_df.drop(columns=[\"Sex\"])"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "pd.get_dummies() produces True/False boolean columns on modern pandas - we convert those to plain 0/1 integers so every downstream formula (correlation, distances) sees consistent numeric types throughout.", "technical": "pd.get_dummies() produces True/False boolean columns on modern pandas - we convert those to plain 0/1 integers so every downstream formula (correlation, distances) sees consistent numeric types throughout.", "hinglish": "pd.get_dummies() produces True/False boolean columns on modern pandas - we convert those to plain 0/1 integers so every downstream formula (correlation, distances) sees consistent numeric types throughout."}, {"type": "code", "text": "bool_columns = encoded_df.select_dtypes(include=\"bool\").columns\nencoded_df[bool_columns] = encoded_df[bool_columns].astype(int)\n\nprint(\"After encoding, every column is now numeric:\")\nprint(encoded_df.dtypes)\nprint(f\"\\nNew shape: {encoded_df.shape[0]} rows x {encoded_df.shape[1]} columns\")\nprint(encoded_df.head())\n\nall_feature_columns = list(encoded_df.columns)"}], "output": "Loaded cleaned dataset from: /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 4/../Lab Assignment 1/Titanic-Dataset-Cleaned.csv\nShape: 891 rows x 8 columns\nColumns: ['Survived', 'Pclass', 'Sex', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'Fare', 'Embarked']\n   Survived  Pclass     Sex   Age  SibSp  Parch     Fare Embarked\n0         0       3    male  22.0      1      0   7.2500        S\n1         1       1  female  38.0      1      0  71.2833        C\n2         1       3  female  26.0      0      0   7.9250        S\n3         1       1  female  35.0      1      0  53.1000        S\n4         0       3    male  35.0      0      0   8.0500        S\n\nWHY DO WE NEED TO DO THIS FIRST?\nCorrelation and every distance/similarity formula in this assignment\n(Euclidean, Manhattan, Hamming, Cosine, Jaccard, SMC) are all pure MATH\nformulas - they only work on numbers, not text. Our cleaned dataset still\nhas two text (\"category\") columns: 'Sex' (male/female) and 'Embarked'\n(S/C/Q, the port the passenger boarded from). So, before anything else,\nwe convert both into numeric 0/1 columns:\n\n  'Sex' -> ONE new column, 'Sex_male' (1 = male, 0 = female). Since there\n           are only 2 possible values, a single 0/1 column is enough to\n           capture the information with no ambiguity.\n\n  'Embarked' -> THREE new columns, one per port: 'Embarked_C',\n           'Embarked_Q', 'Embarked_S' (each is 1 if the passenger boarded\n           there, 0 otherwise). This standard technique is called\n           \"ONE-HOT ENCODING\" - since Embarked has 3 possible values (not\n           2), we need 3 separate 0/1 columns, exactly one of which is 1\n           for any given passenger.\n\nAfter encoding, every column is now numeric:\nSurvived        int64\nPclass          int64\nAge           float64\nSibSp           int64\nParch           int64\nFare          float64\nSex_male        int64\nEmbarked_C      int64\nEmbarked_Q      int64\nEmbarked_S      int64\ndtype: object\n\nNew shape: 891 rows x 10 columns\n   Survived  Pclass   Age  SibSp  Parch     Fare  Sex_male  Embarked_C  Embarked_Q  Embarked_S\n0         0       3  22.0      1      0   7.2500         1           0           0           1\n1         1       1  38.0      1      0  71.2833         0           1           0           0\n2         1       3  26.0      0      0   7.9250         0           0           0           1\n3         1       1  35.0      1      0  53.1000         0           0           0           1\n4         0       3  35.0      0      0   8.0500         1           0           0           1", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-1", "kind": "question", "number": 1, "label": "Visualize Correlation Matrix of Features", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 1: VISUALIZE THE CORRELATION MATRIX OF FEATURES\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHAT IS A CORRELATION MATRIX?\nFor every PAIR of numeric columns, the Pearson correlation coefficient\n\"r\" (already covered in the lecture notes on measuring feature\nredundancy) measures how strongly the two columns move together:\n\n  r close to +1  -> as one column goes up, the other reliably goes up too\n  r close to -1  -> as one column goes up, the other reliably goes down\n  r close to  0  -> the two columns show no linear relationship\n\nA \"correlation matrix\" is simply this \"r\" value computed for EVERY\npossible pair of columns at once, arranged as a square table/grid - the\ndiagonal is always exactly 1.0 (any column is perfectly correlated with\nitself).\n\"\"\")\n\ncorrelation_matrix = encoded_df.corr()\nprint(\"Full correlation matrix (rounded to 3 decimals):\")\nprint(correlation_matrix.round(3))"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "A heatmap is the standard way to VISUALIZE a correlation matrix: each cell is colored according to its correlation value, so strong relationships (very red or very blue) jump out immediately, without having to read every individual number in the table above.", "technical": "A heatmap is the standard way to VISUALIZE a correlation matrix: each cell is colored according to its correlation value, so strong relationships (very red or very blue) jump out immediately, without having to read every individual number in the table above.", "hinglish": "A heatmap is the standard way to VISUALIZE a correlation matrix: each cell is colored according to its correlation value, so strong relationships (very red or very blue) jump out immediately, without having to read every individual number in the table above."}, {"type": "code", "text": "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 8))\nim = ax.imshow(correlation_matrix, cmap=\"coolwarm\", vmin=-1, vmax=1)\nax.set_xticks(range(len(all_feature_columns)))\nax.set_yticks(range(len(all_feature_columns)))\nax.set_xticklabels(all_feature_columns, rotation=45, ha=\"right\")\nax.set_yticklabels(all_feature_columns)"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Writing the actual number inside each cell (on top of the color) makes the heatmap readable precisely, not just \"roughly by color\".", "technical": "Writing the actual number inside each cell (on top of the color) makes the heatmap readable precisely, not just \"roughly by color\".", "hinglish": "Writing the actual number inside each cell (on top of the color) makes the heatmap readable precisely, not just \"roughly by color\"."}, {"type": "code", "text": "for i in range(len(all_feature_columns)):\n    for j in range(len(all_feature_columns)):\n        value = correlation_matrix.iloc[i, j]"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "White text shows up better on the darkest (most saturated) red/ blue cells, black text shows up better on the paler, near-zero cells - this simple rule keeps every number readable.", "technical": "White text shows up better on the darkest (most saturated) red/ blue cells, black text shows up better on the paler, near-zero cells - this simple rule keeps every number readable.", "hinglish": "White text shows up better on the darkest (most saturated) red/ blue cells, black text shows up better on the paler, near-zero cells - this simple rule keeps every number readable."}, {"type": "code", "text": "        text_color = \"white\" if abs(value) > 0.6 else \"black\"\n        ax.text(j, i, f\"{value:.2f}\", ha=\"center\", va=\"center\",\n                color=text_color, fontsize=8)\nfig.colorbar(im, ax=ax, label=\"Pearson correlation (r)\")\nax.set_title(\"Correlation Matrix Heatmap - All Encoded Titanic Features\")\nsave_current_plot(\"1_correlation_matrix_heatmap.png\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nHow to read this heatmap: deep red cells mean strong POSITIVE\ncorrelation, deep blue cells mean strong NEGATIVE correlation, and pale/\nwhite cells near 0 mean the two features are essentially unrelated\n(linearly). The diagonal is always deep red (value 1.00) since every\nfeature is perfectly correlated with itself - that's expected and not\nmeaningful on its own. Question 2 below digs into which OFF-diagonal\ncells are strong enough to matter.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "WHAT IS A CORRELATION MATRIX?\nFor every PAIR of numeric columns, the Pearson correlation coefficient\n\"r\" (already covered in the lecture notes on measuring feature\nredundancy) measures how strongly the two columns move together:\n\n  r close to +1  -> as one column goes up, the other reliably goes up too\n  r close to -1  -> as one column goes up, the other reliably goes down\n  r close to  0  -> the two columns show no linear relationship\n\nA \"correlation matrix\" is simply this \"r\" value computed for EVERY\npossible pair of columns at once, arranged as a square table/grid - the\ndiagonal is always exactly 1.0 (any column is perfectly correlated with\nitself).\n\nFull correlation matrix (rounded to 3 decimals):\n            Survived  Pclass    Age  SibSp  Parch   Fare  Sex_male  Embarked_C  Embarked_Q  Embarked_S\nSurvived       1.000  -0.338 -0.065 -0.035  0.082  0.257    -0.543       0.168       0.004      -0.150\nPclass        -0.338   1.000 -0.340  0.083  0.018 -0.549     0.132      -0.243       0.221       0.074\nAge           -0.065  -0.340  1.000 -0.233 -0.172  0.097     0.081       0.030      -0.031      -0.007\nSibSp         -0.035   0.083 -0.233  1.000  0.415  0.160    -0.115      -0.060      -0.026       0.069\nParch          0.082   0.018 -0.172  0.415  1.000  0.216    -0.245      -0.011      -0.081       0.061\nFare           0.257  -0.549  0.097  0.160  0.216  1.000    -0.182       0.269      -0.117      -0.162\nSex_male      -0.543   0.132  0.081 -0.115 -0.245 -0.182     1.000      -0.083      -0.074       0.119\nEmbarked_C     0.168  -0.243  0.030 -0.060 -0.011  0.269    -0.083       1.000      -0.148      -0.783\nEmbarked_Q     0.004   0.221 -0.031 -0.026 -0.081 -0.117    -0.074      -0.148       1.000      -0.499\nEmbarked_S    -0.150   0.074 -0.007  0.069  0.061 -0.162     0.119      -0.783      -0.499       1.000\n  Saved plot -> plots/1_correlation_matrix_heatmap.png\n\nHow to read this heatmap: deep red cells mean strong POSITIVE\ncorrelation, deep blue cells mean strong NEGATIVE correlation, and pale/\nwhite cells near 0 mean the two features are essentially unrelated\n(linearly). The diagonal is always deep red (value 1.00) since every\nfeature is perfectly correlated with itself - that's expected and not\nmeaningful on its own. Question 2 below digs into which OFF-diagonal\ncells are strong enough to matter.", "plots": [{"filename": "1_correlation_matrix_heatmap.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}, {"id": "question-2", "kind": "question", "number": 2, "label": "Identify Highly Correlated Features", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 2: IDENTIFY HIGHLY CORRELATED (REDUNDANT) FEATURES\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHAT COUNTS AS \"HIGHLY CORRELATED\"?\nThere's no single universal cutoff, but a very commonly used rule of\nthumb (and the one we use here) is:\n\n    |r| > 0.7   ->   the two features are considered \"highly correlated\"\n\nThe \"| |\" (absolute value) matters: a correlation of -0.9 is just as\n\"highly correlated\" as +0.9 - the SIGN only tells you the DIRECTION of\nthe relationship (do they move together or oppositely), while the SIZE\n(ignoring sign) tells you the STRENGTH of the relationship, which is what\nwe actually care about for spotting redundancy.\n\nWHY DOES HIGH CORRELATION MEAN \"REDUNDANT\"?\nIf two features are highly correlated, they are carrying almost the SAME\ninformation - so keeping both barely adds anything a model couldn't\nalready infer from just one of them, while still costing extra\ncomputation and (for many models) potentially causing \"multicollinearity\"\nproblems (unstable, hard-to-trust coefficients). This is exactly the\n\"minimizing redundancy\" driver of feature selection from the lecture\nnotes.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef find_highly_correlated_pairs(corr_matrix, threshold=0.7):\n    \"\"\"\n    Scans a correlation matrix (a pandas DataFrame) and returns a list of\n    (feature_a, feature_b, correlation_value) tuples for every PAIR of\n    DIFFERENT features whose absolute correlation exceeds the threshold.\n    Each pair is only returned ONCE (we don't also return the mirror-image\n    duplicate, since corr(A,B) is always equal to corr(B,A)).\n    \"\"\"\n    columns = corr_matrix.columns\n    highly_correlated = []\n    for i in range(len(columns)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(columns)):  # j starts AFTER i, so no self-pairs and no duplicate mirror-pairs\n            value = corr_matrix.iloc[i, j]\n            if abs(value) > threshold:\n                highly_correlated.append((columns[i], columns[j], value))\n    return highly_correlated\n\n\nthreshold = 0.7\nhighly_correlated_pairs = find_highly_correlated_pairs(correlation_matrix, threshold)\n\nprint(f\"Scanning every feature pair for |r| > {threshold} ...\\n\")\nif highly_correlated_pairs:\n    for feature_a, feature_b, value in highly_correlated_pairs:\n        print(f\"  '{feature_a}'  <->  '{feature_b}'   r = {value:.3f}  (|r| = {abs(value):.3f} > {threshold})\")\nelse:\n    print(\"  No feature pair exceeded the threshold.\")\n\nprint(f\"\"\"\nWHY THIS PARTICULAR PAIR SHOWS UP:\n'Embarked_C' and 'Embarked_S' come from ONE-HOT ENCODING the same original\ncolumn ('Embarked'), which only has 3 possible values (C, Q, S) and every\npassenger has EXACTLY ONE of them. Since the S port is by far the most\ncommon (646 of 891 passengers) and C is the second most common, knowing\n\"this passenger did NOT board at S\" already makes it noticeably more\nlikely they boarded at C (because there are only 2 remaining options, and\nC is the bigger one) - hence the strong NEGATIVE correlation between the\ntwo one-hot columns. This is a textbook example of the well-known \"dummy\nvariable trap\": whenever you one-hot-encode a category into N columns,\nthose N columns are never fully independent of each other, because\nknowing all-but-one of them already tells you the last one for free.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "WHAT COUNTS AS \"HIGHLY CORRELATED\"?\nThere's no single universal cutoff, but a very commonly used rule of\nthumb (and the one we use here) is:\n\n    |r| > 0.7   ->   the two features are considered \"highly correlated\"\n\nThe \"| |\" (absolute value) matters: a correlation of -0.9 is just as\n\"highly correlated\" as +0.9 - the SIGN only tells you the DIRECTION of\nthe relationship (do they move together or oppositely), while the SIZE\n(ignoring sign) tells you the STRENGTH of the relationship, which is what\nwe actually care about for spotting redundancy.\n\nWHY DOES HIGH CORRELATION MEAN \"REDUNDANT\"?\nIf two features are highly correlated, they are carrying almost the SAME\ninformation - so keeping both barely adds anything a model couldn't\nalready infer from just one of them, while still costing extra\ncomputation and (for many models) potentially causing \"multicollinearity\"\nproblems (unstable, hard-to-trust coefficients). This is exactly the\n\"minimizing redundancy\" driver of feature selection from the lecture\nnotes.\n\nScanning every feature pair for |r| > 0.7 ...\n\n  'Embarked_C'  <->  'Embarked_S'   r = -0.783  (|r| = 0.783 > 0.7)\n\nWHY THIS PARTICULAR PAIR SHOWS UP:\n'Embarked_C' and 'Embarked_S' come from ONE-HOT ENCODING the same original\ncolumn ('Embarked'), which only has 3 possible values (C, Q, S) and every\npassenger has EXACTLY ONE of them. Since the S port is by far the most\ncommon (646 of 891 passengers) and C is the second most common, knowing\n\"this passenger did NOT board at S\" already makes it noticeably more\nlikely they boarded at C (because there are only 2 remaining options, and\nC is the bigger one) - hence the strong NEGATIVE correlation between the\ntwo one-hot columns. This is a textbook example of the well-known \"dummy\nvariable trap\": whenever you one-hot-encode a category into N columns,\nthose N columns are never fully independent of each other, because\nknowing all-but-one of them already tells you the last one for free.", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-3", "kind": "question", "number": 3, "label": "Remove Redundant Features", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 3: REMOVE REDUNDANT FEATURES\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nHOW DO WE DECIDE *WHICH* FEATURE TO DROP FROM EACH PAIR?\nFor each highly-correlated pair identified in Question 2, we keep the\nfeature that is more strongly correlated with 'Survived' (our target/\nlabel column - the thing we'd ultimately want to predict), and drop the\nother, since the one more related to the actual outcome we care about is\nthe more useful one to keep.\n\"\"\")\n\ntarget_column = \"Survived\"\ncolumns_to_drop = []\nfor feature_a, feature_b, value in highly_correlated_pairs:\n    corr_a_with_target = abs(correlation_matrix.loc[feature_a, target_column])\n    corr_b_with_target = abs(correlation_matrix.loc[feature_b, target_column])\n    if corr_a_with_target >= corr_b_with_target:\n        drop_this_one = feature_b\n        keep_this_one = feature_a\n    else:\n        drop_this_one = feature_a\n        keep_this_one = feature_b\n    columns_to_drop.append(drop_this_one)\n    print(f\"  Pair ('{feature_a}', '{feature_b}'): \"\n          f\"|corr with Survived| is {corr_a_with_target:.3f} for '{feature_a}' \"\n          f\"vs {corr_b_with_target:.3f} for '{feature_b}' \"\n          f\"-> keeping '{keep_this_one}', dropping '{drop_this_one}'\")\n\nreduced_df = encoded_df.drop(columns=columns_to_drop)\n\nprint(f\"\\nColumns BEFORE removal ({len(encoded_df.columns)}): {list(encoded_df.columns)}\")\nprint(f\"Columns dropped as redundant: {columns_to_drop}\")\nprint(f\"Columns AFTER removal ({len(reduced_df.columns)}): {list(reduced_df.columns)}\")\n\nprint(f\"\"\"\nNOTE: Dropping 'Embarked_S' here also happens to match a second, standard\ndata-preparation practice independent of correlation: when one-hot\nencoding a category with N values, it's normal to keep only N-1 of the\ndummy columns (dropping any one of them, e.g. Embarked_S), since the\ndropped column's value can always be perfectly inferred from the other\nN-1 columns being all 0 - keeping all N columns is mathematically\nredundant by construction, which is exactly what the correlation-based\nremoval above caught automatically.\n\"\"\")"}], "output": "HOW DO WE DECIDE *WHICH* FEATURE TO DROP FROM EACH PAIR?\nFor each highly-correlated pair identified in Question 2, we keep the\nfeature that is more strongly correlated with 'Survived' (our target/\nlabel column - the thing we'd ultimately want to predict), and drop the\nother, since the one more related to the actual outcome we care about is\nthe more useful one to keep.\n\n  Pair ('Embarked_C', 'Embarked_S'): |corr with Survived| is 0.168 for 'Embarked_C' vs 0.150 for 'Embarked_S' -> keeping 'Embarked_C', dropping 'Embarked_S'\n\nColumns BEFORE removal (10): ['Survived', 'Pclass', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'Fare', 'Sex_male', 'Embarked_C', 'Embarked_Q', 'Embarked_S']\nColumns dropped as redundant: ['Embarked_S']\nColumns AFTER removal (9): ['Survived', 'Pclass', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'Fare', 'Sex_male', 'Embarked_C', 'Embarked_Q']\n\nNOTE: Dropping 'Embarked_S' here also happens to match a second, standard\ndata-preparation practice independent of correlation: when one-hot\nencoding a category with N values, it's normal to keep only N-1 of the\ndummy columns (dropping any one of them, e.g. Embarked_S), since the\ndropped column's value can always be perfectly inferred from the other\nN-1 columns being all 0 - keeping all N columns is mathematically\nredundant by construction, which is exactly what the correlation-based\nremoval above caught automatically.", "plots": []}, {"id": "question-4", "kind": "question", "number": 4, "label": "Feature Selection Using Distance/Similarity Measures", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"QUESTION 4: SIX DISTANCE/SIMILARITY MEASURES BETWEEN FEATURES\")\n\nprint(\"\"\"\nWHAT ARE WE MEASURING HERE, AND WHY?\nQuestions 1-3 spotted redundant features using CORRELATION. This question\nuses 6 DIFFERENT formulas from the lecture notes on measuring feature\nredundancy, applied the same way: to compare pairs of FEATURES (whole\ncolumns, not individual passengers) against each other, to see if a\ntotally different kind of formula agrees with what correlation already\nfound.\n\nWe split the 10 features (from BEFORE Question 3's removal, so we can\ncompare against the same full set Question 1/2 used) into two groups,\nsince different formulas suit different kinds of data (this exact\ndense-vs-sparse/binary split is in the lecture notes too):\n\n  DENSE, CONTINUOUS features -> Pclass, Age, SibSp, Parch, Fare\n      (used for Euclidean, Manhattan, and Cosine)\n\n  BINARY (0/1) features -> Survived, Sex_male, Embarked_C, Embarked_Q,\n  Embarked_S\n      (used for Hamming, Jaccard, and SMC)\n\nFor the 3 dense-feature formulas, we first STANDARDIZE every column (the\nsame Z-score technique from Lab Assignment 3: subtract the mean, divide\nby the standard deviation) - this is essential here, because otherwise\n'Fare' (values up to several hundred) would completely dominate the\ndistance calculations over something like 'Pclass' (values only 1-3),\npurely due to its bigger raw numbers, not because it's actually more\ndifferent.\n\"\"\")\n\ndense_feature_names = [\"Pclass\", \"Age\", \"SibSp\", \"Parch\", \"Fare\"]\nbinary_feature_names = [\"Survived\", \"Sex_male\", \"Embarked_C\", \"Embarked_Q\", \"Embarked_S\"]\n\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nstandardized_dense = pd.DataFrame(\n    scaler.fit_transform(encoded_df[dense_feature_names]),\n    columns=dense_feature_names,\n)\nbinary_features = encoded_df[binary_feature_names]\n\nprint(\"Standardized dense features (first 5 rows):\")\nprint(standardized_dense.head().round(3))\nprint(\"\\nBinary features (first 5 rows):\")\nprint(binary_features.head())\n\n\ndef pairwise_matrix(feature_names, pair_function):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of feature (column) names and a function that computes\n    one number for a PAIR of columns, builds and returns the full square\n    matrix (as a pandas DataFrame) of that number for every pair of\n    features - reused below for all 6 distance/similarity formulas so we\n    don't repeat the same looping code 6 times.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(feature_names)\n    result = np.zeros((n, n))\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            result[i, j] = pair_function(feature_names[i], feature_names[j])\n    return pd.DataFrame(result, index=feature_names, columns=feature_names)\n\n\ndef most_extreme_off_diagonal(matrix, find_max=True):\n    \"\"\"Finds the (feature_a, feature_b, value) with the largest (or, if\n    find_max=False, smallest) value among all OFF-diagonal cells of a\n    square matrix - used to highlight the single most notable pair for\n    each of the 6 measures below.\"\"\"\n    best = None\n    for i, row_name in enumerate(matrix.index):\n        for j, col_name in enumerate(matrix.columns):\n            if i == j:\n                continue  # skip the diagonal (a feature compared with itself)\n            value = matrix.iloc[i, j]\n            if best is None or (find_max and value > best[2]) or (not find_max and value < best[2]):\n                best = (row_name, col_name, value)\n    return best"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4(i) EUCLIDEAN DISTANCE@@", "technical": "@@4(i) EUCLIDEAN DISTANCE@@", "hinglish": "@@4(i) EUCLIDEAN DISTANCE@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (i) EUCLIDEAN DISTANCE ---\nFormula: d(A, B) = sqrt( sum( (A_i - B_i)^2 ) )\nThe \"straight-line\" distance between two vectors, exactly as covered\n(and hand-verified) in the lecture notes' worked example. Here, each\n\"vector\" is one STANDARDIZED dense feature column (891 values long, one\nper passenger) - so we're measuring how differently two entire columns\nbehave ACROSS ALL PASSENGERS AT ONCE, not comparing two individual\npassengers to each other.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef euclidean_distance(col_a, col_b):\n    a = standardized_dense[col_a].to_numpy()\n    b = standardized_dense[col_b].to_numpy()\n    return np.sqrt(np.sum((a - b) ** 2))\n\n\neuclidean_matrix = pairwise_matrix(dense_feature_names, euclidean_distance)\nprint(\"Euclidean distance matrix (dense features):\")\nprint(euclidean_matrix.round(3))\nclosest_pair = most_extreme_off_diagonal(euclidean_matrix, find_max=False)\nprint(f\"\\nSMALLEST Euclidean distance (= most similar pair): \"\n      f\"'{closest_pair[0]}' <-> '{closest_pair[1]}'  (distance = {closest_pair[2]:.3f})\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4(ii) MANHATTAN DISTANCE@@", "technical": "@@4(ii) MANHATTAN DISTANCE@@", "hinglish": "@@4(ii) MANHATTAN DISTANCE@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (ii) MANHATTAN DISTANCE ---\nFormula: d(A, B) = sum( |A_i - B_i| )\nAlso called \"city-block\" distance - instead of the straight diagonal line\n(Euclidean), it adds up the absolute differences directly, as if you\ncould only move along grid streets, never diagonally. It's less sensitive\nto any single huge difference than Euclidean is (because differences\naren't squared), so it can rank feature pairs slightly differently.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef manhattan_distance(col_a, col_b):\n    a = standardized_dense[col_a].to_numpy()\n    b = standardized_dense[col_b].to_numpy()\n    return np.sum(np.abs(a - b))\n\n\nmanhattan_matrix = pairwise_matrix(dense_feature_names, manhattan_distance)\nprint(\"Manhattan distance matrix (dense features):\")\nprint(manhattan_matrix.round(3))\nclosest_pair = most_extreme_off_diagonal(manhattan_matrix, find_max=False)\nprint(f\"\\nSMALLEST Manhattan distance (= most similar pair): \"\n      f\"'{closest_pair[0]}' <-> '{closest_pair[1]}'  (distance = {closest_pair[2]:.3f})\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4(iii) HAMMING DISTANCE@@", "technical": "@@4(iii) HAMMING DISTANCE@@", "hinglish": "@@4(iii) HAMMING DISTANCE@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (iii) HAMMING DISTANCE ---\nFormula: d(A, B) = (number of positions where A_i != B_i) / total positions\nOriginally designed for comparing strings/binary codes of equal length -\nhere, for two binary (0/1) feature columns, it simply counts, passenger\nby passenger, how often the two columns DISAGREE (one is 0 and the other\nis 1), then divides by the total number of passengers to get a fraction\nbetween 0 (identical on every passenger) and 1 (opposite on every\npassenger).\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef hamming_distance(col_a, col_b):\n    a = binary_features[col_a].to_numpy()\n    b = binary_features[col_b].to_numpy()\n    disagreements = np.sum(a != b)\n    return disagreements / len(a)\n\n\nhamming_matrix = pairwise_matrix(binary_feature_names, hamming_distance)\nprint(\"Hamming distance matrix (binary features):\")\nprint(hamming_matrix.round(3))\nclosest_pair = most_extreme_off_diagonal(hamming_matrix, find_max=False)\nprint(f\"\\nSMALLEST Hamming distance (= most similar pair): \"\n      f\"'{closest_pair[0]}' <-> '{closest_pair[1]}'  (distance = {closest_pair[2]:.3f})\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4(iv) COSINE SIMILARITY@@", "technical": "@@4(iv) COSINE SIMILARITY@@", "hinglish": "@@4(iv) COSINE SIMILARITY@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (iv) COSINE SIMILARITY ---\nFormula: cos(A, B) = (A . B) / (||A|| * ||B||)\nMeasures the ANGLE between two vectors, ignoring their magnitude/length\nentirely - it asks \"do these two columns point in the same DIRECTION\nacross passengers?\", not \"are their raw sizes similar?\". A value of 1\nmeans the two columns point in exactly the same direction (perfectly\nsimilar pattern), 0 means they're at a right angle (unrelated), and -1\nmeans they point in exactly opposite directions.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef cosine_similarity(col_a, col_b):\n    a = standardized_dense[col_a].to_numpy()\n    b = standardized_dense[col_b].to_numpy()\n    dot_product = np.dot(a, b)\n    magnitude_a = np.sqrt(np.sum(a ** 2))\n    magnitude_b = np.sqrt(np.sum(b ** 2))\n    return dot_product / (magnitude_a * magnitude_b)\n\n\ncosine_matrix = pairwise_matrix(dense_feature_names, cosine_similarity)\nprint(\"Cosine similarity matrix (dense features):\")\nprint(cosine_matrix.round(3))\nmost_similar_pair = most_extreme_off_diagonal(cosine_matrix, find_max=True)\nprint(f\"\\nLARGEST Cosine similarity (= most similar pair): \"\n      f\"'{most_similar_pair[0]}' <-> '{most_similar_pair[1]}'  (similarity = {most_similar_pair[2]:.3f})\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4(v) JACCARD DISTANCE@@", "technical": "@@4(v) JACCARD DISTANCE@@", "hinglish": "@@4(v) JACCARD DISTANCE@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (v) JACCARD DISTANCE ---\nFormula: distance(A, B) = 1 - [ (A AND B) / (A OR B) ]\nThe part in [ ] brackets is the \"Jaccard coefficient\" from the lecture\nnotes: treating each binary column as a SET of passengers (the ones where\nthat feature = 1), it's the size of the OVERLAP (passengers where BOTH\nfeatures are 1) divided by the size of the UNION (passengers where\nEITHER feature is 1). Unlike Hamming, Jaccard completely IGNORES\npositions where BOTH columns are 0 together - it only cares about where\nat least one of the two columns is \"switched on\", which is exactly why\nit's the standard choice for SPARSE/binary data (see the lecture notes'\ndense-vs-sparse grouping).\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef jaccard_distance(col_a, col_b):\n    a = binary_features[col_a].to_numpy().astype(bool)\n    b = binary_features[col_b].to_numpy().astype(bool)\n    intersection = np.sum(a & b)\n    union = np.sum(a | b)\n    if union == 0:\n        return 0.0  # both columns are all-zero everywhere - treat as identical (distance 0)\n    jaccard_coefficient = intersection / union\n    return 1 - jaccard_coefficient\n\n\njaccard_matrix = pairwise_matrix(binary_feature_names, jaccard_distance)\nprint(\"Jaccard distance matrix (binary features):\")\nprint(jaccard_matrix.round(3))\nclosest_pair = most_extreme_off_diagonal(jaccard_matrix, find_max=False)\nprint(f\"\\nSMALLEST Jaccard distance (= most similar pair): \"\n      f\"'{closest_pair[0]}' <-> '{closest_pair[1]}'  (distance = {closest_pair[2]:.3f})\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "@@4(vi) SIMPLE MATCHING COEFFICIENT (SMC)@@", "technical": "@@4(vi) SIMPLE MATCHING COEFFICIENT (SMC)@@", "hinglish": "@@4(vi) SIMPLE MATCHING COEFFICIENT (SMC)@@"}, {"type": "code", "text": "print(\"\"\"\n--- (vi) SIMPLE MATCHING COEFFICIENT (SMC) ---\nFormula: SMC(A, B) = (matches) / (total positions)\n                    = (number of positions where A_i == B_i) / total positions\nThis is the \"mirror image\" of Hamming distance and the \"complete\"\ncounterpart to Jaccard: unlike Jaccard, SMC counts BOTH kinds of\nagreement - positions where both columns are 1 AND positions where both\nare 0 - as a \"match\". This makes SMC the right choice when a shared \"0\"\ngenuinely means something meaningful for both features (dense/complete\nbinary data), while Jaccard is better when a shared \"0\" is just \"neither\nfeature happened to apply here\" (sparse binary data) - exactly the\ndistinction drawn in the lecture notes.\n\"\"\")\n\n\ndef simple_matching_coefficient(col_a, col_b):\n    a = binary_features[col_a].to_numpy()\n    b = binary_features[col_b].to_numpy()\n    matches = np.sum(a == b)\n    return matches / len(a)\n\n\nsmc_matrix = pairwise_matrix(binary_feature_names, simple_matching_coefficient)\nprint(\"Simple Matching Coefficient matrix (binary features):\")\nprint(smc_matrix.round(3))\nmost_similar_pair = most_extreme_off_diagonal(smc_matrix, find_max=True)\nprint(f\"\\nLARGEST SMC (= most similar pair): \"\n      f\"'{most_similar_pair[0]}' <-> '{most_similar_pair[1]}'  (SMC = {most_similar_pair[2]:.3f})\")\n\nembarked_c_s_hamming = hamming_matrix.loc[\"Embarked_C\", \"Embarked_S\"]\nembarked_c_s_jaccard = jaccard_matrix.loc[\"Embarked_C\", \"Embarked_S\"]\nembarked_c_s_smc = smc_matrix.loc[\"Embarked_C\", \"Embarked_S\"]\n\nprint(f\"\"\"\nSMC IS EXACTLY \"1 - HAMMING DISTANCE\":\nLook closely at the two matrices above - for any pair of features, SMC\nalways equals 1 - Hamming_distance(that same pair) (e.g. 'Embarked_C' <->\n'Embarked_Q': Hamming = 0.275, SMC = 0.725, and 0.275 + 0.725 = 1.000).\nThis makes sense from the formulas themselves: Hamming distance counts\nthe fraction of DISAGREEING positions, SMC counts the fraction of\nAGREEING positions, and every position is either agreeing or disagreeing\n- so the two always add up to exactly 1, and they will always rank pairs\nidentically (just \"flipped\").\n\nAN IMPORTANT DISAGREEMENT WITH CORRELATION:\nQuestion 2 flagged 'Embarked_C' and 'Embarked_S' as \"highly correlated\"\n(r = -0.783). But look at how ALL THREE binary measures score that exact\nsame pair here: Hamming distance = {embarked_c_s_hamming:.3f} (close to\nthe maximum of 1 - i.e. very DISSIMILAR), Jaccard distance =\n{embarked_c_s_jaccard:.3f} (the absolute maximum possible, 1.000), and\nSMC = {embarked_c_s_smc:.3f} (close to the minimum of 0 - again very\nDISSIMILAR). So Hamming/Jaccard/SMC all say the OPPOSITE of what\ncorrelation said about this exact pair!\n\nWHY THE DISAGREEMENT IS NOT A MISTAKE - IT'S A DIFFERENCE IN WHAT \"SIMILAR\"\nMEANS: correlation treats a STRONG relationship in EITHER direction\n(positive or negative) as \"these two carry the same information, hence\nredundant\" - a passenger who is \"not S\" is quite predictably \"C\" here,\nwhich is exactly why r is large in magnitude. Hamming/Jaccard/SMC, on the\nother hand, are pure AGREEMENT measures - they only count how often the\ntwo columns show the exact SAME value at the exact same passenger, and\n'Embarked_C'/'Embarked_S' almost NEVER show the same value (whenever one\nis 1 the other is 0, since a passenger can only board from one port) - so\nthese measures correctly call them maximally \"different\", even though\nthey're still highly (negatively) PREDICTABLE from each other. This is a\ngenuinely important lesson: \"redundant\" can mean different things under\ndifferent formulas, and a real feature-selection pipeline benefits from\nchecking more than one kind of measure, exactly as this question does.\n\"\"\")"}, {"type": "explanation", "simple": "Two side-by-side heatmap panels: dense-feature measures (Euclidean/ Manhattan/Cosine) on the left group, binary-feature measures (Hamming/ Jaccard/SMC) on the right group - visualizing all 6 matrices at once.", "technical": "Two side-by-side heatmap panels: dense-feature measures (Euclidean/ Manhattan/Cosine) on the left group, binary-feature measures (Hamming/ Jaccard/SMC) on the right group - visualizing all 6 matrices at once.", "hinglish": "Two side-by-side heatmap panels: dense-feature measures (Euclidean/ Manhattan/Cosine) on the left group, binary-feature measures (Hamming/ Jaccard/SMC) on the right group - visualizing all 6 matrices at once."}, {"type": "code", "text": "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 3, figsize=(16, 10))\ndense_matrices = [\n    (\"Euclidean Distance\", euclidean_matrix, \"viridis_r\"),\n    (\"Manhattan Distance\", manhattan_matrix, \"viridis_r\"),\n    (\"Cosine Similarity\", cosine_matrix, \"coolwarm\"),\n]\nbinary_matrices = [\n    (\"Hamming Distance\", hamming_matrix, \"viridis_r\"),\n    (\"Jaccard Distance\", jaccard_matrix, \"viridis_r\"),\n    (\"Simple Matching Coefficient\", smc_matrix, \"viridis\"),\n]\nfor ax, (title, matrix, cmap) in zip(axes[0], dense_matrices):\n    im = ax.imshow(matrix, cmap=cmap)\n    ax.set_xticks(range(len(matrix.columns)))\n    ax.set_yticks(range(len(matrix.index)))\n    ax.set_xticklabels(matrix.columns, rotation=45, ha=\"right\", fontsize=8)\n    ax.set_yticklabels(matrix.index, fontsize=8)\n    ax.set_title(title, fontsize=10)\n    fig.colorbar(im, ax=ax, fraction=0.046)\nfor ax, (title, matrix, cmap) in zip(axes[1], binary_matrices):\n    im = ax.imshow(matrix, cmap=cmap, vmin=0, vmax=1)\n    ax.set_xticks(range(len(matrix.columns)))\n    ax.set_yticks(range(len(matrix.index)))\n    ax.set_xticklabels(matrix.columns, rotation=45, ha=\"right\", fontsize=8)\n    ax.set_yticklabels(matrix.index, fontsize=8)\n    ax.set_title(title, fontsize=10)\n    fig.colorbar(im, ax=ax, fraction=0.046)\nfig.suptitle(\"Question 4: All 6 Distance/Similarity Measures Between Features\", fontsize=13)\nsave_current_plot(\"4_all_six_distance_similarity_heatmaps.png\")"}], "output": "WHAT ARE WE MEASURING HERE, AND WHY?\nQuestions 1-3 spotted redundant features using CORRELATION. This question\nuses 6 DIFFERENT formulas from the lecture notes on measuring feature\nredundancy, applied the same way: to compare pairs of FEATURES (whole\ncolumns, not individual passengers) against each other, to see if a\ntotally different kind of formula agrees with what correlation already\nfound.\n\nWe split the 10 features (from BEFORE Question 3's removal, so we can\ncompare against the same full set Question 1/2 used) into two groups,\nsince different formulas suit different kinds of data (this exact\ndense-vs-sparse/binary split is in the lecture notes too):\n\n  DENSE, CONTINUOUS features -> Pclass, Age, SibSp, Parch, Fare\n      (used for Euclidean, Manhattan, and Cosine)\n\n  BINARY (0/1) features -> Survived, Sex_male, Embarked_C, Embarked_Q,\n  Embarked_S\n      (used for Hamming, Jaccard, and SMC)\n\nFor the 3 dense-feature formulas, we first STANDARDIZE every column (the\nsame Z-score technique from Lab Assignment 3: subtract the mean, divide\nby the standard deviation) - this is essential here, because otherwise\n'Fare' (values up to several hundred) would completely dominate the\ndistance calculations over something like 'Pclass' (values only 1-3),\npurely due to its bigger raw numbers, not because it's actually more\ndifferent.\n\nStandardized dense features (first 5 rows):\n   Pclass    Age  SibSp  Parch   Fare\n0   0.827 -0.566  0.433 -0.474 -0.502\n1  -1.566  0.664  0.433 -0.474  0.787\n2   0.827 -0.258 -0.475 -0.474 -0.489\n3  -1.566  0.433  0.433 -0.474  0.421\n4   0.827  0.433 -0.475 -0.474 -0.486\n\nBinary features (first 5 rows):\n   Survived  Sex_male  Embarked_C  Embarked_Q  Embarked_S\n0         0         1           0           0           1\n1         1         0           1           0           0\n2         1         0           0           0           1\n3         1         0           0           0           1\n4         0         1           0           0           1\n\n--- (i) EUCLIDEAN DISTANCE ---\nFormula: d(A, B) = sqrt( sum( (A_i - B_i)^2 ) )\nThe \"straight-line\" distance between two vectors, exactly as covered\n(and hand-verified) in the lecture notes' worked example. Here, each\n\"vector\" is one STANDARDIZED dense feature column (891 values long, one\nper passenger) - so we're measuring how differently two entire columns\nbehave ACROSS ALL PASSENGERS AT ONCE, not comparing two individual\npassengers to each other.\n\nEuclidean distance matrix (dense features):\n        Pclass     Age   SibSp   Parch    Fare\nPclass   0.000  48.864  40.422  41.823  52.547\nAge     48.864   0.000  46.880  45.710  40.121\nSibSp   40.422  46.880   0.000  32.292  38.698\nParch   41.823  45.710  32.292   0.000  37.372\nFare    52.547  40.121  38.698  37.372   0.000\n\nSMALLEST Euclidean distance (= most similar pair): 'SibSp' <-> 'Parch'  (distance = 32.292)\n\n--- (ii) MANHATTAN DISTANCE ---\nFormula: d(A, B) = sum( |A_i - B_i| )\nAlso called \"city-block\" distance - instead of the straight diagonal line\n(Euclidean), it adds up the absolute differences directly, as if you\ncould only move along grid streets, never diagonally. It's less sensitive\nto any single huge difference than Euclidean is (because differences\naren't squared), so it can rank feature pairs slightly differently.\n\nManhattan distance matrix (dense features):\n          Pclass       Age     SibSp     Parch      Fare\nPclass     0.000  1203.637  1004.694  1025.928  1187.791\nAge     1203.637     0.000   921.329   962.388   798.992\nSibSp   1004.694   921.329     0.000   474.970   563.538\nParch   1025.928   962.388   474.970     0.000   574.938\nFare    1187.791   798.992   563.538   574.938     0.000\n\nSMALLEST Manhattan distance (= most similar pair): 'SibSp' <-> 'Parch'  (distance = 474.970)\n\n--- (iii) HAMMING DISTANCE ---\nFormula: d(A, B) = (number of positions where A_i != B_i) / total positions\nOriginally designed for comparing strings/binary codes of equal length -\nhere, for two binary (0/1) feature columns, it simply counts, passenger\nby passenger, how often the two columns DISAGREE (one is 0 and the other\nis 1), then divides by the total number of passengers to get a fraction\nbetween 0 (identical on every passenger) and 1 (opposite on every\npassenger).\n\nHamming distance matrix (binary features):\n            Survived  Sex_male  Embarked_C  Embarked_Q  Embarked_S\nSurvived       0.000     0.787       0.364       0.403       0.617\nSex_male       0.787     0.000       0.623       0.642       0.383\nEmbarked_C     0.364     0.623       0.000       0.275       0.914\nEmbarked_Q     0.403     0.642       0.275       0.000       0.811\nEmbarked_S     0.617     0.383       0.914       0.811       0.000\n\nSMALLEST Hamming distance (= most similar pair): 'Embarked_C' <-> 'Embarked_Q'  (distance = 0.275)\n\n--- (iv) COSINE SIMILARITY ---\nFormula: cos(A, B) = (A . B) / (||A|| * ||B||)\nMeasures the ANGLE between two vectors, ignoring their magnitude/length\nentirely - it asks \"do these two columns point in the same DIRECTION\nacross passengers?\", not \"are their raw sizes similar?\". A value of 1\nmeans the two columns point in exactly the same direction (perfectly\nsimilar pattern), 0 means they're at a right angle (unrelated), and -1\nmeans they point in exactly opposite directions.\n\nCosine similarity matrix (dense features):\n        Pclass    Age  SibSp  Parch   Fare\nPclass   1.000 -0.340  0.083  0.018 -0.549\nAge     -0.340  1.000 -0.233 -0.172  0.097\nSibSp    0.083 -0.233  1.000  0.415  0.160\nParch    0.018 -0.172  0.415  1.000  0.216\nFare    -0.549  0.097  0.160  0.216  1.000\n\nLARGEST Cosine similarity (= most similar pair): 'SibSp' <-> 'Parch'  (similarity = 0.415)\n\n--- (v) JACCARD DISTANCE ---\nFormula: distance(A, B) = 1 - [ (A AND B) / (A OR B) ]\nThe part in [ ] brackets is the \"Jaccard coefficient\" from the lecture\nnotes: treating each binary column as a SET of passengers (the ones where\nthat feature = 1), it's the size of the OVERLAP (passengers where BOTH\nfeatures are 1) divided by the size of the UNION (passengers where\nEITHER feature is 1). Unlike Hamming, Jaccard completely IGNORES\npositions where BOTH columns are 0 together - it only cares about where\nat least one of the two columns is \"switched on\", which is exactly why\nit's the standard choice for SPARSE/binary data (see the lecture notes'\ndense-vs-sparse grouping).\n\nJaccard distance matrix (binary features):\n            Survived  Sex_male  Embarked_C  Embarked_Q  Embarked_S\nSurvived       0.000     0.865       0.777       0.923       0.715\nSex_male       0.865     0.000       0.854       0.933       0.436\nEmbarked_C     0.777     0.854       0.000       1.000       1.000\nEmbarked_Q     0.923     0.933       1.000       0.000       1.000\nEmbarked_S     0.715     0.436       1.000       1.000       0.000\n\nSMALLEST Jaccard distance (= most similar pair): 'Sex_male' <-> 'Embarked_S'  (distance = 0.436)\n\n--- (vi) SIMPLE MATCHING COEFFICIENT (SMC) ---\nFormula: SMC(A, B) = (matches) / (total positions)\n                    = (number of positions where A_i == B_i) / total positions\nThis is the \"mirror image\" of Hamming distance and the \"complete\"\ncounterpart to Jaccard: unlike Jaccard, SMC counts BOTH kinds of\nagreement - positions where both columns are 1 AND positions where both\nare 0 - as a \"match\". This makes SMC the right choice when a shared \"0\"\ngenuinely means something meaningful for both features (dense/complete\nbinary data), while Jaccard is better when a shared \"0\" is just \"neither\nfeature happened to apply here\" (sparse binary data) - exactly the\ndistinction drawn in the lecture notes.\n\nSimple Matching Coefficient matrix (binary features):\n            Survived  Sex_male  Embarked_C  Embarked_Q  Embarked_S\nSurvived       1.000     0.213       0.636       0.597       0.383\nSex_male       0.213     1.000       0.377       0.358       0.617\nEmbarked_C     0.636     0.377       1.000       0.725       0.086\nEmbarked_Q     0.597     0.358       0.725       1.000       0.189\nEmbarked_S     0.383     0.617       0.086       0.189       1.000\n\nLARGEST SMC (= most similar pair): 'Embarked_C' <-> 'Embarked_Q'  (SMC = 0.725)\n\nSMC IS EXACTLY \"1 - HAMMING DISTANCE\":\nLook closely at the two matrices above - for any pair of features, SMC\nalways equals 1 - Hamming_distance(that same pair) (e.g. 'Embarked_C' <->\n'Embarked_Q': Hamming = 0.275, SMC = 0.725, and 0.275 + 0.725 = 1.000).\nThis makes sense from the formulas themselves: Hamming distance counts\nthe fraction of DISAGREEING positions, SMC counts the fraction of\nAGREEING positions, and every position is either agreeing or disagreeing\n- so the two always add up to exactly 1, and they will always rank pairs\nidentically (just \"flipped\").\n\nAN IMPORTANT DISAGREEMENT WITH CORRELATION:\nQuestion 2 flagged 'Embarked_C' and 'Embarked_S' as \"highly correlated\"\n(r = -0.783). But look at how ALL THREE binary measures score that exact\nsame pair here: Hamming distance = 0.914 (close to\nthe maximum of 1 - i.e. very DISSIMILAR), Jaccard distance =\n1.000 (the absolute maximum possible, 1.000), and\nSMC = 0.086 (close to the minimum of 0 - again very\nDISSIMILAR). So Hamming/Jaccard/SMC all say the OPPOSITE of what\ncorrelation said about this exact pair!\n\nWHY THE DISAGREEMENT IS NOT A MISTAKE - IT'S A DIFFERENCE IN WHAT \"SIMILAR\"\nMEANS: correlation treats a STRONG relationship in EITHER direction\n(positive or negative) as \"these two carry the same information, hence\nredundant\" - a passenger who is \"not S\" is quite predictably \"C\" here,\nwhich is exactly why r is large in magnitude. Hamming/Jaccard/SMC, on the\nother hand, are pure AGREEMENT measures - they only count how often the\ntwo columns show the exact SAME value at the exact same passenger, and\n'Embarked_C'/'Embarked_S' almost NEVER show the same value (whenever one\nis 1 the other is 0, since a passenger can only board from one port) - so\nthese measures correctly call them maximally \"different\", even though\nthey're still highly (negatively) PREDICTABLE from each other. This is a\ngenuinely important lesson: \"redundant\" can mean different things under\ndifferent formulas, and a real feature-selection pipeline benefits from\nchecking more than one kind of measure, exactly as this question does.\n\n  Saved plot -> plots/4_all_six_distance_similarity_heatmaps.png", "plots": [{"filename": "4_all_six_distance_similarity_heatmaps.png", "data_uri": "data:image/png;base64,"}]}, {"id": "wrapup", "kind": "wrapup", "number": null, "label": "Saving Your Results", "chunks": [{"type": "code", "text": "section(\"SAVING OUTPUT FILES\")\n\nencoded_path = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"Titanic-Dataset-Encoded.csv\")\nencoded_df.to_csv(encoded_path, index=False)\nprint(f\"Saved fully-encoded (before redundancy removal) dataset -> {encoded_path}\")\n\nreduced_path = os.path.join(this_script_folder, \"Titanic-Dataset-FeatureSelected.csv\")\nreduced_df.to_csv(reduced_path, index=False)\nprint(f\"Saved final, redundancy-free feature-selected dataset -> {reduced_path}\")\n\nprint(f\"\\nAll plots were saved inside: {plots_folder}\")\nprint(\"\\nAll 4 questions of Lab Assignment 4 are complete.\")"}], "output": "Saved fully-encoded (before redundancy removal) dataset -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 4/Titanic-Dataset-Encoded.csv\nSaved final, redundancy-free feature-selected dataset -> /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 4/Titanic-Dataset-FeatureSelected.csv\n\nAll plots were saved inside: /Users/etech/Desktop/MLT_Python/Lab_assingments/Lab Assignment 4/plots\n\nAll 4 questions of Lab Assignment 4 are complete.", "plots": []}]}];
